<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<usx version="3.0">
  <book code="LUK" style="id">LUKE</book>
  <chapter number="1" style="c" sid="LUK 1"/>
  <para style="s1">Introduction</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 1:1"/>
    Since many others have already undertaken the task of compiling an orderly narrative account concerning the events that have been accomplished among us by God’s sovereign plan,
    <verse eid="LUK 1:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 1:2"/>
    just as those who were eyewitnesses from the very beginning and who became servants of the word have handed down these accounts to us through careful testimony,
    <verse eid="LUK 1:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 1:3"/>
    it seemed good and appropriate to me also, having carefully investigated and traced all these things accurately from the very beginning, to write them down for you in an orderly and sequential manner, most excellent Theophilus.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 1:4"/>
    I write so that you may know with certainty and have full assurance concerning the matters about which you have been instructed.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Prophecy Regarding the Birth of John</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 1:5"/>
    During the reign of Herod, who was king of Judea, there lived a certain priest named Zechariah who belonged to the priestly division of Abijah. His wife was a descendant of Aaron, the first high priest, and her name was Elizabeth.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 1:6"/>
    Both of them were righteous in God’s sight, walking blamelessly and faithfully in all the commandments and ordinances that the Lord had given.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 1:7"/>
    However, they had no child because Elizabeth was barren and unable to conceive. Moreover, both of them were now advanced in years, well past the normal age for childbearing.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 1:8"/>
    One day, while Zechariah was serving as priest before God during his division’s appointed turn of service in the temple,
    <verse eid="LUK 1:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 1:9"/>
    he was chosen by lot according to the established custom of the priesthood to enter the temple sanctuary of the Lord and burn the incense offering.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 1:10"/>
    At that sacred hour when the incense was being burned, the whole assembled crowd of people was praying outside in the courtyard.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 1:11"/>
    Then an angel of the Lord suddenly appeared to Zechariah, standing at the right side of the altar of incense.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 1:12"/>
    When Zechariah saw the angel, he was troubled, and fear overwhelmed him.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 1:13"/>
    But the angel spoke reassuringly to him, saying, “Do not be afraid, Zechariah. Your prayer has been heard and answered by God. Your wife Elizabeth will bear you a son, and you are to name him John.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 1:14"/>
    He will bring you great joy and gladness, and many people will rejoice at his birth.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 1:15"/>
    For he will be great in the sight of the Lord. He must never drink wine or any strong drink, maintaining a Nazirite-like consecration. He will be filled with the Holy Spirit even while still in his mother’s womb.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 1:16"/>
    He will turn many of the children of Israel back to the Lord their God in repentance.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 1:17"/>
    He will go before the Lord in the spirit and power of Elijah the prophet, turning the hearts of fathers back to their children and turning the disobedient to embrace the wisdom of the righteous. In this way, he will make ready for the Lord a people who are fully prepared for His coming.”
    <verse eid="LUK 1:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 1:18"/>
    Zechariah responded to the angel with doubt, saying, “How can I be certain that this will happen? By what means will I know this is true? I am an old man, and my wife is advanced in years.”
    <verse eid="LUK 1:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 1:19"/>
    The angel answered him, saying, “I am Gabriel, who stands continually in the very presence of God. I have been sent specifically to speak to you and to announce to you this good news.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 1:20"/>
    Listen carefully: You will become silent and unable to speak until the day these things actually take place, because you did not believe my words, which will certainly be fulfilled at their appointed time.”
    <verse eid="LUK 1:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 1:21"/>
    Meanwhile, the people outside were waiting for Zechariah, and they began to wonder at his unusual delay in the temple.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 1:22"/>
    When he finally came out, he was unable to speak to them. They quickly realized that he must have seen a vision while in the temple. He kept making signs and gestures to them, but remained unable to speak.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 1:23"/>
    When the days of his appointed priestly service came to an end, he departed from the temple and returned to his home.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 1:24"/>
    After these days, his wife Elizabeth conceived a child. She secluded herself in private for five months, saying with thanksgiving,
    <verse eid="LUK 1:24"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 1:25"/>
    “This is what the Lord has done for me in these days when He looked upon me with favor, removing my disgrace and reproach among people.”
    <verse eid="LUK 1:25"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Prophecy Regarding the Birth of Jesus</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 1:26"/>
    In the sixth month of Elizabeth’s pregnancy, the angel Gabriel was sent from God to a town in the region of Galilee named Nazareth,
    <verse eid="LUK 1:26"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 1:27"/>
    to a virgin who was legally pledged to be married to a man whose name was Joseph, a descendant of the house of David. The virgin’s name was Mary.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:27"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 1:28"/>
    The angel came to her and said, “Greetings, you who have been highly favored by God! The Lord is with you in a special way.”
    <verse eid="LUK 1:28"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 1:29"/>
    But Mary was deeply troubled at this greeting, and she kept pondering in her mind what kind of greeting this might be.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:29"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 1:30"/>
    The angel said to her reassuringly, “Do not be afraid, Mary. You have found favor with God.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:30"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 1:31"/>
    Listen: You will conceive in your womb and give birth to a Son, and you are to name Him Jesus.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:31"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 1:32"/>
    This Child will be great and magnificent. He will be called the Son of the Most High God. The Lord God will give to Him the throne of His ancestor David,
    <verse eid="LUK 1:32"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 1:33"/>
    and He will reign over the house of Jacob forever and ever. His kingdom will have no end—it will be eternal.”
    <verse eid="LUK 1:33"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 1:34"/>
    Mary said to the angel, “How can this be? How will this happen, since I am a virgin?”
    <verse eid="LUK 1:34"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 1:35"/>
    The angel answered her, saying, “The Holy Spirit will come upon you in power, and the power of the Most High will overshadow you like a cloud of glory. Therefore, the Holy One who will be born will be called the Son of God.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:35"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 1:36"/>
    Moreover, Elizabeth your relative has also conceived a son in her old age. This is now the sixth month of pregnancy for her who was called barren.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:36"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 1:37"/>
    For nothing will be impossible with God. No word from God will ever fail to be fulfilled.”
    <verse eid="LUK 1:37"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 1:38"/>
    Mary responded in faith and submission, “Behold, I am the maidservant of the Lord, fully surrendered to His will. May it happen to me exactly according to your word.” Then the angel departed from her presence.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:38"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Mary Visits Elizabeth</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="39" style="v" sid="LUK 1:39"/>
    In those days, Mary arose and traveled with eagerness into the hill country, to a town belonging to the tribe of Judah.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:39"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="40" style="v" sid="LUK 1:40"/>
    She entered the house of Zechariah and greeted Elizabeth.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:40"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="41" style="v" sid="LUK 1:41"/>
    When Elizabeth heard Mary’s greeting, the baby in her womb leaped for joy. Elizabeth was filled with the Holy Spirit and given supernatural insight.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:41"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="42" style="v" sid="LUK 1:42"/>
    She cried out with a loud voice, saying, “Blessed are you among all women, and blessed is the Child you carry in your womb!
    <verse eid="LUK 1:42"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="43" style="v" sid="LUK 1:43"/>
    Why has this gracious thing happened to me, that the mother of my Lord should come to visit me?
    <verse eid="LUK 1:43"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="44" style="v" sid="LUK 1:44"/>
    For the moment the sound of your greeting reached my ears, the baby in my womb leaped for joy and excitement.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:44"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="45" style="v" sid="LUK 1:45"/>
    Blessed is she who believed that there would be a complete fulfillment of what was spoken to her from the Lord.”
    <verse eid="LUK 1:45"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Magnificat</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="46" style="v" sid="LUK 1:46"/>
    Mary responded with a song of praise, saying, 
    <verse eid="LUK 1:46"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">“My soul magnifies and exalts the Lord,</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="47" style="v" sid="LUK 1:47"/>
    and my spirit has rejoiced greatly in God my Savior,
    <verse eid="LUK 1:47"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="48" style="v" sid="LUK 1:48"/>
    for He has looked with favor upon the humble and lowly state of His maidservant. 
    <verse eid="LUK 1:48"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">For look, from this moment forward, all generations throughout history will count me blessed and favored,</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="49" style="v" sid="LUK 1:49"/>
    because the Mighty One has done great and magnificent things for me, 
    <verse eid="LUK 1:49"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">and holy is His name.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="50" style="v" sid="LUK 1:50"/>
    His mercy extends to those who fear Him with reverence,
    <verse eid="LUK 1:50"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">from generation to generation throughout all time.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="51" style="v" sid="LUK 1:51"/>
    He has demonstrated mighty power with His arm. 
    <verse eid="LUK 1:51"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">He has scattered those who are proud in the thoughts and intentions of their hearts.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="52" style="v" sid="LUK 1:52"/>
    He has brought down rulers and the powerful from their thrones, 
    <verse eid="LUK 1:52"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">and He has exalted and lifted up those who are lowly and humble.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="53" style="v" sid="LUK 1:53"/>
    He has filled those who are hungry with good things, 
    <verse eid="LUK 1:53"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">and those who are rich He has sent away empty-handed.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="54" style="v" sid="LUK 1:54"/>
    He has helped Israel His servant, 
    <verse eid="LUK 1:54"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">remembering and fulfilling His mercy,</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="55" style="v" sid="LUK 1:55"/>
    just as He spoke and promised to our fathers, 
    <verse eid="LUK 1:55"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">to Abraham and to his descendants forever.”</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="56" style="v" sid="LUK 1:56"/>
    Mary remained with Elizabeth for approximately three months, then returned to her own home.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:56"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Birth of John</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="57" style="v" sid="LUK 1:57"/>
    The time was fulfilled for Elizabeth to give birth, and she bore a son.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:57"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="58" style="v" sid="LUK 1:58"/>
    Her neighbors and relatives heard that the Lord had greatly demonstrated His mercy to her, and they began to rejoice together with her.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:58"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="59" style="v" sid="LUK 1:59"/>
    On the eighth day, according to Jewish custom, they came to circumcise the child. They wanted to name him after his father, calling him Zechariah.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:59"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="60" style="v" sid="LUK 1:60"/>
    But his mother spoke up and said, “No, he will not be named after his father. He will be called John.”
    <verse eid="LUK 1:60"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="61" style="v" sid="LUK 1:61"/>
    They protested to her, “There is no one among your relatives who is called by this name.”
    <verse eid="LUK 1:61"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="62" style="v" sid="LUK 1:62"/>
    So they made signs to his father Zechariah, inquiring what he would like the child to be called.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:62"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="63" style="v" sid="LUK 1:63"/>
    Zechariah asked for a writing tablet and wrote, “John is his name.” Everyone present marveled at this.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:63"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="64" style="v" sid="LUK 1:64"/>
    Immediately Zechariah’s mouth and tongue were opened and freed. He began to speak, blessing and praising God.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:64"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="65" style="v" sid="LUK 1:65"/>
    Fear and awe came upon all those who lived in the surrounding area, and all these remarkable events were being talked about throughout the entire hill country of Judea.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:65"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="66" style="v" sid="LUK 1:66"/>
    All those who heard about these things kept them in their hearts, pondering them and saying, “What then will this child become? What is his destiny?” For it was evident that the hand of the Lord was powerfully with him.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:66"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Zechariah’s Prophecy</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="67" style="v" sid="LUK 1:67"/>
    Zechariah, the child’s father, was filled with the Holy Spirit and prophesied, saying:
    <verse eid="LUK 1:67"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="68" style="v" sid="LUK 1:68"/>
    “Blessed be the Lord, the God of Israel, 
    <verse eid="LUK 1:68"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">because He has visited His people and accomplished redemption for them.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="69" style="v" sid="LUK 1:69"/>
    He has raised up a horn of salvation—a mighty Savior—for us in the house of David, His servant,
    <verse eid="LUK 1:69"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="70" style="v" sid="LUK 1:70"/>
    just as He spoke through the mouth of His holy prophets from ancient times,
    <verse eid="LUK 1:70"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="71" style="v" sid="LUK 1:71"/>
    promising salvation and deliverance from our enemies and from the hand of all who hate us.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:71"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="72" style="v" sid="LUK 1:72"/>
    He has acted to show mercy to our fathers and to remember His holy covenant,
    <verse eid="LUK 1:72"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="73" style="v" sid="LUK 1:73"/>
    the oath which He swore to Abraham our father. He promised to grant us
    <verse eid="LUK 1:73"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="74" style="v" sid="LUK 1:74"/>
    that we, having been delivered from the hand of our enemies, 
    <verse eid="LUK 1:74"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">might serve Him without fear,</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="75" style="v" sid="LUK 1:75"/>
    in holiness and righteousness before Him all the days of our lives.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:75"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="76" style="v" sid="LUK 1:76"/>
    And you, my child, will be called the prophet of the Most High, 
    <verse eid="LUK 1:76"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">for you will go before the Lord to prepare His ways,</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="77" style="v" sid="LUK 1:77"/>
    giving to His people the knowledge of salvation 
    <verse eid="LUK 1:77"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">through the forgiveness of their sins.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="78" style="v" sid="LUK 1:78"/>
    This will happen because of the tender mercy and compassion of our God, 
    <verse eid="LUK 1:78"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">by which the Sunrise from on high—the Messiah—will visit us.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="79" style="v" sid="LUK 1:79"/>
    He will shine as light on those who sit in darkness 
    <verse eid="LUK 1:79"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">and in the shadow of death, </para>
  <para style="p">and He will guide our feet into the way of peace.”</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="80" style="v" sid="LUK 1:80"/>
    The child John continued to grow physically and to become strong in spirit. He lived in the desert places until the day of his public appearance and ministry to Israel.
    <verse eid="LUK 1:80"/>
  </para>
  <chapter eid="LUK 1"/>
  <chapter number="2" style="c" sid="LUK 2"/>
  <para style="s1">The Birth of Jesus</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 1:18-25</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 2:1"/>
    In those days, a decree went out from Caesar Augustus, the Roman emperor, that a census should be taken to register all the inhabited world under Roman rule.
    <verse eid="LUK 2:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 2:2"/>
    This registration was the first of its kind, taking place when Quirinius was serving as governor of Syria.
    <verse eid="LUK 2:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 2:3"/>
    Everyone went to be registered for the census, each person traveling to his own ancestral town.
    <verse eid="LUK 2:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 2:4"/>
    Joseph also went up from Galilee, from the town of Nazareth, to Judea, to the city of David which is called Bethlehem. He made this journey because he was a descendant of the house and family line of David.
    <verse eid="LUK 2:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 2:5"/>
    He went to be registered along with Mary, who was legally pledged to be married to him and who was expecting a Child.
    <verse eid="LUK 2:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 2:6"/>
    While they were in Bethlehem, the time came for Mary to give birth.
    <verse eid="LUK 2:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 2:7"/>
    She gave birth to her firstborn Son, wrapped Him in swaddling cloths to keep Him warm, and laid Him in a manger—a feeding trough for animals—because there was no room available for them in the inn.
    <verse eid="LUK 2:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Shepherds and the Angels</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 2:8"/>
    In that same region, there were shepherds living out in the fields, keeping watch over their flock during the night.
    <verse eid="LUK 2:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 2:9"/>
    An angel of the Lord suddenly stood before them, and the glory of the Lord shone brilliantly around them. They were terrified and filled with great fear.
    <verse eid="LUK 2:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 2:10"/>
    The angel said to them, “Do not be afraid! I bring you good news of great joy that will be for all people.
    <verse eid="LUK 2:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 2:11"/>
    Today in the city of David, a Savior has been born to you. He is Christ the Lord—the Messiah!
    <verse eid="LUK 2:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 2:12"/>
    This will be the sign for you to identify Him: You will find a baby wrapped in swaddling cloths and lying in a manger.”
    <verse eid="LUK 2:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 2:13"/>
    Suddenly there appeared with the angel a multitude of the heavenly host—a vast army of angels—praising God and saying,
    <verse eid="LUK 2:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 2:14"/>
    “Glory to God in the highest heaven, 
    <verse eid="LUK 2:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">and on earth peace among those with whom He is pleased!”</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 2:15"/>
    When the angels had departed from them and returned to heaven, the shepherds said to one another, “Let us go immediately to Bethlehem and see this thing that has taken place, which the Lord has made known to us.”
    <verse eid="LUK 2:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 2:16"/>
    They came quickly and found both Mary and Joseph, and the baby lying in the manger, just as they had been told.
    <verse eid="LUK 2:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 2:17"/>
    When they saw the Child, they made known to everyone the message that had been told to them by the angels concerning this Child.
    <verse eid="LUK 2:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 2:18"/>
    All who heard the shepherds’ report marveled at what had been told to them.
    <verse eid="LUK 2:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 2:19"/>
    But Mary treasured all these words and events, pondering them carefully in her heart.
    <verse eid="LUK 2:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 2:20"/>
    Then the shepherds returned to their flocks, glorifying and praising God for everything they had heard and seen. It was exactly as it had been told to them.
    <verse eid="LUK 2:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 2:21"/>
    When eight days were fulfilled and the time came for Him to be circumcised according to Jewish law, He was given the name Jesus—the name He had been called by the angel before He was conceived in the womb.
    <verse eid="LUK 2:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Jesus Presented in the Temple</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 2:22"/>
    When the days of their purification were completed according to the Law of Moses, Joseph and Mary brought Jesus up to Jerusalem to present Him to the Lord.
    <verse eid="LUK 2:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 2:23"/>
    This was done as it is written in the Law of the Lord: “Every firstborn male who opens the womb shall be consecrated and called holy to the Lord.”
    <verse eid="LUK 2:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 2:24"/>
    They also came to offer a sacrifice according to what is stated in the Law of the Lord: “a pair of turtledoves or two young pigeons.” 
    <verse eid="LUK 2:24"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Prophecy of Simeon</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 2:25"/>
    There was a man in Jerusalem whose name was Simeon. This man was righteous and devout, eagerly waiting for the consolation of Israel—the coming of the Messiah. The Holy Spirit rested upon him.
    <verse eid="LUK 2:25"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 2:26"/>
    It had been divinely revealed to him by the Holy Spirit that he would not experience death before he had seen the Lord’s Christ—the Messiah.
    <verse eid="LUK 2:26"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 2:27"/>
    Moved by the Spirit, he came into the temple courts. When the parents brought in the child Jesus to perform for Him the customary requirements prescribed by the Law,
    <verse eid="LUK 2:27"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 2:28"/>
    Simeon took Jesus in his arms, blessed God with thanksgiving, and said:
    <verse eid="LUK 2:28"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 2:29"/>
    “Now, Lord, You are releasing Your servant to depart in peace, 
    <verse eid="LUK 2:29"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">according to Your word and promise,</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 2:30"/>
    for my eyes have seen Your salvation,
    <verse eid="LUK 2:30"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 2:31"/>
    which You have made ready in the sight of all peoples everywhere—
    <verse eid="LUK 2:31"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 2:32"/>
    a light that will bring revelation to the Gentiles, 
    <verse eid="LUK 2:32"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">and glory to Your people Israel.”</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 2:33"/>
    Joseph and Mary, His earthly parents, were filled with wonder at what was being said concerning Him.
    <verse eid="LUK 2:33"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 2:34"/>
    Simeon blessed them and said to Mary His mother, “Listen carefully: This Child is appointed to cause the fall and rising of many in Israel. He will be a sign that will be spoken against and rejected by many.
    <verse eid="LUK 2:34"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 2:35"/>
    A sword will pierce through your own soul as well, bringing you deep anguish. All this will happen so that the hidden thoughts and intentions of many hearts may be revealed and exposed.”
    <verse eid="LUK 2:35"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Prophetess Anna</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 2:36"/>
    There was also a prophetess named Anna, the daughter of Phanuel, from the tribe of Asher. She was very advanced in years, having lived with her husband seven years after her marriage.
    <verse eid="LUK 2:36"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 2:37"/>
    Then she lived as a widow until she reached the age of eighty-four. She never left the temple courts, but worshiped God continually night and day with fasting and prayers.
    <verse eid="LUK 2:37"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 2:38"/>
    At that very moment when Simeon was speaking, she approached them. She began to give thanks to God and to speak about the Child to all those who were waiting expectantly for the redemption of Jerusalem.
    <verse eid="LUK 2:38"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Boy Jesus</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="39" style="v" sid="LUK 2:39"/>
    When Joseph and Mary had completed everything required according to the Law of the Lord, they returned to Galilee, to their own town of Nazareth.
    <verse eid="LUK 2:39"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="40" style="v" sid="LUK 2:40"/>
    The Child continued to grow physically and to become strong in body. He was filled with wisdom beyond His years, and the gracious favor of God rested upon Him.
    <verse eid="LUK 2:40"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Jesus at the Temple</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="41" style="v" sid="LUK 2:41"/>
    Every year His parents would travel to Jerusalem for the Feast of the Passover, as faithful Jews.
    <verse eid="LUK 2:41"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="42" style="v" sid="LUK 2:42"/>
    When Jesus was twelve years old, they went up to Jerusalem according to the custom of the feast.
    <verse eid="LUK 2:42"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="43" style="v" sid="LUK 2:43"/>
    After they had completed the days of celebration and were returning home, the boy Jesus remained behind in Jerusalem. His parents did not realize this.
    <verse eid="LUK 2:43"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="44" style="v" sid="LUK 2:44"/>
    Assuming He was somewhere in their traveling company, they went a full day’s journey before they began searching for Him among their relatives and acquaintances.
    <verse eid="LUK 2:44"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="45" style="v" sid="LUK 2:45"/>
    When they did not find Him, they returned anxiously to Jerusalem, searching for Him.
    <verse eid="LUK 2:45"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="46" style="v" sid="LUK 2:46"/>
    After three days of searching, they found Him in the temple courts, sitting among the teachers of the Law, both listening to them and asking them insightful questions.
    <verse eid="LUK 2:46"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="47" style="v" sid="LUK 2:47"/>
    All who heard Him were astonished at His understanding and His answers.
    <verse eid="LUK 2:47"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="48" style="v" sid="LUK 2:48"/>
    When His parents saw Him, they were struck with amazement. His mother said to Him, “Child, why have You treated us this way? Your father and I have been distressed and anxious, searching everywhere for You.”
    <verse eid="LUK 2:48"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="49" style="v" sid="LUK 2:49"/>
    Jesus said to them, “Why were you searching for Me? Did you not know that it was necessary for Me to be in My Father’s house, occupied with My Father's business?”
    <verse eid="LUK 2:49"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="50" style="v" sid="LUK 2:50"/>
    But they did not understand the profound meaning of what He was saying to them.
    <verse eid="LUK 2:50"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="51" style="v" sid="LUK 2:51"/>
    Then Jesus went down with them and returned to Nazareth. He continued to be submissive to them in all things. His mother continued to treasure all these events in her heart.
    <verse eid="LUK 2:51"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="52" style="v" sid="LUK 2:52"/>
    Jesus continued to grow in wisdom and in physical stature, and He increased in favor with both God and people.
    <verse eid="LUK 2:52"/>
  </para>
  <chapter eid="LUK 2"/>
  <chapter number="3" style="c" sid="LUK 3"/>
  <para style="s1">John the Baptist Prepares the Way</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 3:1-12; Mk. 1:1-8; Jn. 1:19-28</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 3:1"/>
    In the fifteenth year of the reign of Tiberius Caesar as Roman emperor, when Pontius Pilate was serving as governor of Judea, and Herod was tetrarch ruling over Galilee, and his brother Philip was tetrarch of the region of Iturea and Trachonitis, and Lysanias was tetrarch of Abilene—
    <verse eid="LUK 3:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 3:2"/>
    during the high priesthood of Annas and Caiaphas, the word of God came powerfully upon John the son of Zechariah in the wilderness.
    <verse eid="LUK 3:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 3:3"/>
    John went throughout all the surrounding region of the Jordan River, preaching a baptism of repentance for the forgiveness of sins.
    <verse eid="LUK 3:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 3:4"/>
    This fulfilled what is written in the book of the words of Isaiah the prophet: 
    <verse eid="LUK 3:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">“The voice of one calling out in the wilderness, </para>
  <para style="p">‘Prepare the way of the Lord, </para>
  <para style="p">make His paths straight and ready.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 3:5"/>
    Every valley shall be filled in, 
    <verse eid="LUK 3:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">and every mountain and hill shall be leveled. </para>
  <para style="p">The crooked places shall become straight, </para>
  <para style="p">and the rough places shall become smooth paths.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 3:6"/>
    Then all humanity will see the salvation of God.’ ”*
    <verse eid="LUK 3:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 3:7"/>
    Therefore John was saying to the crowds that came out to be baptized by him, “You offspring of vipers! Who warned you to flee from the coming wrath of God?
    <verse eid="LUK 3:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 3:8"/>
    Produce fruit that is appropriate to and consistent with repentance. Do not even begin to say to yourselves, ‘We have Abraham as our father.’ I tell you that God is able to raise up children for Abraham from these stones!
    <verse eid="LUK 3:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 3:9"/>
    Even now the axe is laid at the root of the trees, ready to cut. Therefore, every tree that does not produce good fruit is cut down and thrown into the fire of judgment.”
    <verse eid="LUK 3:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 3:10"/>
    The crowds were asking him, “What then should we do? How should we demonstrate true repentance?”
    <verse eid="LUK 3:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 3:11"/>
    John would answer them, saying, “The one who has two tunics should share with the one who has none. The one who has food should do the same and share with those in need.”
    <verse eid="LUK 3:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 3:12"/>
    Even tax collectors—despised for their collaboration with Rome—came to be baptized. They said to him, “Teacher, what should we do?”
    <verse eid="LUK 3:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 3:13"/>
    He said to them, “Collect no more tax money than what has been prescribed for you. Do not use your position to extort people.”
    <verse eid="LUK 3:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 3:14"/>
    Soldiers who were serving in the military were also asking him, “What should we also do?” He said to them, “Do not extort money from anyone by threats or false accusations. Be satisfied with your wages.”
    <verse eid="LUK 3:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 3:15"/>
    As the people were filled with expectation, and all were questioning in their hearts concerning John, wondering whether he might possibly be the Christ,
    <verse eid="LUK 3:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 3:16"/>
    John answered them all, saying, “I baptize you with water as a symbol of repentance, but One who is mightier and more powerful than I is coming. I am not worthy even to untie the strap of His sandals. He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and with fire.
    <verse eid="LUK 3:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 3:17"/>
    His winnowing fork is in His hand, ready to thoroughly clean out His threshing floor. He will gather the wheat into His barn, but the chaff He will burn up with unquenchable fire.”
    <verse eid="LUK 3:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 3:18"/>
    With many other exhortations and warnings, John kept preaching the good news to the people.
    <verse eid="LUK 3:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 3:19"/>
    But Herod the tetrarch, who had been reproved and rebuked by John concerning Herodias—the wife of his brother Philip whom Herod had unlawfully taken—and concerning all the evil acts that Herod had committed,
    <verse eid="LUK 3:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 3:20"/>
    added this sin to all the others: He also confined John in prison, silencing the prophet.
    <verse eid="LUK 3:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Baptism of Jesus</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 3:13-17; Mk. 1:9-11</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 3:21"/>
    When all the people were being baptized, Jesus also was baptized. While He was praying after His baptism, heaven was opened,
    <verse eid="LUK 3:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 3:22"/>
    and the Holy Spirit descended upon Him in bodily form like a dove. A voice came from heaven, declaring, “You are My Son, the Beloved One. In You I am well pleased.”
    <verse eid="LUK 3:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Genealogy of Jesus</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 1:1-17</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 3:23"/>
    Jesus Himself was about thirty years old when He began His public ministry. He was the son, as people supposed, of Joseph, who was the son of Heli,
    <verse eid="LUK 3:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 3:24"/>
    the son of Matthat, the son of Levi, the son of Melchi, the son of Jannai, the son of Joseph,
    <verse eid="LUK 3:24"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 3:25"/>
    the son of Mattathias, the son of Amos, the son of Nahum, the son of Esli, the son of Naggai,
    <verse eid="LUK 3:25"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 3:26"/>
    the son of Maath, the son of Mattathias, the son of Semein, the son of Josech, the son of Joda,
    <verse eid="LUK 3:26"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 3:27"/>
    the son of Joanan, the son of Rhesa, the son of Zerubbabel, the son of Shealtiel, the son of Neri,
    <verse eid="LUK 3:27"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 3:28"/>
    the son of Melchi, the son of Addi, the son of Cosam, the son of Elmadam, the son of Er,
    <verse eid="LUK 3:28"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 3:29"/>
    the son of Joshua, the son of Eliezer, the son of Jorim, the son of Matthat, the son of Levi,
    <verse eid="LUK 3:29"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 3:30"/>
    the son of Simeon, the son of Judah, the son of Joseph, the son of Jonam, the son of Eliakim,
    <verse eid="LUK 3:30"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 3:31"/>
    the son of Melea, the son of Menna, the son of Mattatha, the son of Nathan, the son of David,
    <verse eid="LUK 3:31"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 3:32"/>
    the son of Jesse, the son of Obed, the son of Boaz, the son of Sala, the son of Nahshon,
    <verse eid="LUK 3:32"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 3:33"/>
    the son of Amminadab, the son of Admin, the son of Arni, the son of Hezron, the son of Perez, the son of Judah,
    <verse eid="LUK 3:33"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 3:34"/>
    the son of Jacob, the son of Isaac, the son of Abraham, the son of Terah, the son of Nahor,
    <verse eid="LUK 3:34"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 3:35"/>
    the son of Serug, the son of Reu, the son of Peleg, the son of Eber, the son of Shelah,
    <verse eid="LUK 3:35"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 3:36"/>
    the son of Cainan, the son of Arphaxad, the son of Shem, the son of Noah, the son of Lamech,
    <verse eid="LUK 3:36"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 3:37"/>
    the son of Methuselah, the son of Enoch, the son of Jared, the son of Mahalaleel, the son of Cainan,
    <verse eid="LUK 3:37"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 3:38"/>
    the son of Enos, the son of Seth, the son of Adam, the son of God. 
    <verse eid="LUK 3:38"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Notes </para>
  <para style="p">6 Quoted from Is. 40:3-5</para>
  <chapter eid="LUK 3"/>
  <chapter number="4" style="c" sid="LUK 4"/>
  <para style="s1">The Temptation of Jesus</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 4:1-11; Mk. 1:12-13</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 4:1"/>
    Jesus, full of the Holy Spirit, returned from the Jordan River. He was led by the Spirit into the wilderness
    <verse eid="LUK 4:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 4:2"/>
    for forty days, where He was being tempted by the devil. He ate nothing during those days, and when they came to an end, He was hungry and physically weak.
    <verse eid="LUK 4:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 4:3"/>
    The devil said to Him, “If You are the Son of God, command this stone to become bread.”
    <verse eid="LUK 4:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 4:4"/>
    But Jesus answered him, “It is written in Scripture: ‘Man shall not live by bread alone.’ ”* 
    <verse eid="LUK 4:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 4:5"/>
    Then the devil led Him up to a high place and showed Him all the kingdoms of the world in a single instant of time.
    <verse eid="LUK 4:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 4:6"/>
    The devil said to Him, “I will give You all this authority and glory, for it has been handed over to me, and I give it to whomever I wish.
    <verse eid="LUK 4:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 4:7"/>
    Therefore, if You will worship before me, all this will be Yours.”
    <verse eid="LUK 4:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 4:8"/>
    But Jesus answered him, “It is written: 
    <verse eid="LUK 4:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">‘You shall worship the Lord your God, </para>
  <para style="p">and Him alone you shall serve.’ ”*</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 4:9"/>
    Then the devil brought Jesus to Jerusalem and set Him on the highest point of the temple. He said to Him, “If You are the Son of God, throw Yourself down from here,
    <verse eid="LUK 4:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 4:10"/>
    for it is written: ‘He will command His angels concerning You, 
    <verse eid="LUK 4:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">to guard You,’</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 4:11"/>
    and ‘On their hands they will bear You up, 
    <verse eid="LUK 4:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">so that You will not strike Your foot against a stone.’ ”</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 4:12"/>
    Jesus answered him, “It has been said: ‘You shall not put the Lord your God to the test.’ ”* 
    <verse eid="LUK 4:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 4:13"/>
    When the devil had finished every form and type of temptation, he withdrew from Jesus to wait for a more opportune time.
    <verse eid="LUK 4:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Beginning of Jesus’ Galilean Ministry</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 4:12-17; Mk. 1:14-15</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 4:14"/>
    Jesus returned to Galilee in the power of the Spirit. News and reports about Him spread throughout all the surrounding region.
    <verse eid="LUK 4:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 4:15"/>
    He was teaching in their synagogues, and He was being praised and glorified by everyone.
    <verse eid="LUK 4:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Rejection of Jesus in Nazareth</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 13:53-58; Mk. 6:1-6</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 4:16"/>
    Jesus came to Nazareth, where He had been brought up as a child. According to His custom on the Sabbath day, He went into the synagogue and stood up to read from Scripture.
    <verse eid="LUK 4:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 4:17"/>
    The scroll of the prophet Isaiah was handed to Him. He unrolled the scroll and found the place where it was written:
    <verse eid="LUK 4:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 4:18"/>
    “The Spirit of the Lord is upon Me, 
    <verse eid="LUK 4:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">because He has anointed Me to preach good news to the poor. </para>
  <para style="p">He has sent Me to proclaim freedom to the captives, </para>
  <para style="p">and recovery of sight to the blind, </para>
  <para style="p">to set free those who are oppressed,</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 4:19"/>
    to proclaim the favorable year of the Lord’s grace.”* 
    <verse eid="LUK 4:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 4:20"/>
    He rolled up the scroll, gave it back to the attendant, and sat down to teach. The eyes of everyone in the synagogue were fixed intently on Him, waiting to hear what He would say.
    <verse eid="LUK 4:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 4:21"/>
    Jesus began to say to them, “Today this Scripture has been fulfilled in your hearing—I am the One this prophecy speaks of.”
    <verse eid="LUK 4:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 4:22"/>
    All were bearing witness to Him and were marveling at the gracious words that were coming from His mouth. Yet they were also saying with skepticism, “Is this not Joseph’s son? How can He claim such things?”
    <verse eid="LUK 4:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 4:23"/>
    Jesus said to them, “Surely you will quote to Me this proverb: ‘Physician, heal yourself.’ You are thinking, ‘Whatever we heard happened in Capernaum, do here in Your hometown as well.’ ”
    <verse eid="LUK 4:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 4:24"/>
    He continued, “Truly, I tell you that no prophet is accepted and honored in his own hometown.
    <verse eid="LUK 4:24"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 4:25"/>
    But I tell you the truth: There were many widows in Israel during the time of Elijah, when the sky was shut up for three years and six months and a great famine came over the whole land.*
    <verse eid="LUK 4:25"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 4:26"/>
    Yet Elijah was not sent to any of them, but only to a widow in Zarephath, in the region of Sidon—a Gentile woman.*
    <verse eid="LUK 4:26"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 4:27"/>
    There were also many lepers in Israel during the time of Elisha the prophet, yet none of them was cleansed except Naaman the Syrian—another Gentile.”* 
    <verse eid="LUK 4:27"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 4:28"/>
    When they heard these things, all in the synagogue were filled with rage.
    <verse eid="LUK 4:28"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 4:29"/>
    They rose up, drove Him out of the city, and led Him to the brow of the hill on which their city was built, intending to throw Him down the cliff to His death.
    <verse eid="LUK 4:29"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 4:30"/>
    But passing through the midst of them supernaturally, He went on His way.
    <verse eid="LUK 4:30"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Healing of a Man with a Spirit of an Unclean Demon</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Mk. 1:21-28</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 4:31"/>
    Jesus went down to Capernaum, a city in Galilee. He was teaching the people on the Sabbath day,
    <verse eid="LUK 4:31"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 4:32"/>
    and they were utterly astonished at His teaching, because His word carried divine authority.
    <verse eid="LUK 4:32"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 4:33"/>
    In the synagogue there was a man who had the spirit of an unclean demon. He cried out in a loud voice,
    <verse eid="LUK 4:33"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 4:34"/>
    “Ha! What do You have to do with us, Jesus of Nazareth? Have You come to destroy us before our time? I know who You are—the Holy One of God!”
    <verse eid="LUK 4:34"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 4:35"/>
    Jesus rebuked the demon, commanding, “Be silent and come out of him!” After the demon had thrown the man down violently in their midst, it came out of him without doing him any lasting injury.
    <verse eid="LUK 4:35"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 4:36"/>
    Amazement came over all the people, and they were saying to one another, “What kind of word is this? He commands the unclean spirits with authority and power, and they come out!”
    <verse eid="LUK 4:36"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 4:37"/>
    Reports about Jesus were spreading rapidly into every place in the surrounding region.
    <verse eid="LUK 4:37"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Healing of Many</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 8:14-17; Mk. 1:29-34</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 4:38"/>
    Jesus left the synagogue and went into Simon Peter’s house. Simon’s mother-in-law was suffering from a high fever, and they appealed to Jesus on her behalf.
    <verse eid="LUK 4:38"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="39" style="v" sid="LUK 4:39"/>
    Standing over her, Jesus rebuked the fever with authority, and it left her immediately. She got up at once and began to serve them.
    <verse eid="LUK 4:39"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="40" style="v" sid="LUK 4:40"/>
    As the sun was setting and the Sabbath was ending, all those who had people sick with various diseases brought them to Jesus. He laid His hands on each one of them individually and healed them.
    <verse eid="LUK 4:40"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="41" style="v" sid="LUK 4:41"/>
    Demons also were coming out from many people, crying out and saying, “You are the Son of God!” But Jesus rebuked them sternly and would not allow them to speak, because they knew Him to be the Christ.
    <verse eid="LUK 4:41"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Extension of Jesus’ Preaching Ministry</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Mk. 1:35-39</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="42" style="v" sid="LUK 4:42"/>
    When daylight came, Jesus left the house and went to a deserted place to pray. The crowds were looking for Him eagerly, and when they found Him, they tried to prevent Him from leaving them.
    <verse eid="LUK 4:42"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="43" style="v" sid="LUK 4:43"/>
    But He said to them, “It is necessary for Me to preach the gospel of the kingdom of God to the other cities as well, because this is the purpose for which I was sent by the Father.”
    <verse eid="LUK 4:43"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="44" style="v" sid="LUK 4:44"/>
    He continued preaching in the synagogues throughout Judea, proclaiming the good news of God’s kingdom.
    <verse eid="LUK 4:44"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Notes</para>
  <para style="p">4 Quote from Deut. 8:3</para>
  <para style="p">8 Quoted from Deut. 6:13</para>
  <para style="p">12 Quoted from Deut. 6:16</para>
  <para style="p">18-19 Quoted from Is. 61:1-2</para>
  <para style="p">25 This is a reference to 1 Kgs. 17:1; 18:1</para>
  <para style="p">26 This is a reference to 1 Kgs. </para>
  <para style="p">17:9</para>
  <para style="p">27 This is a reference to 2 Kgs. 5:1-14</para>
  <chapter eid="LUK 4"/>
  <chapter number="5" style="c" sid="LUK 5"/>
  <para style="s1">The First Disciples of Jesus</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 4:18-22; Mk. 1:16-20</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 5:1"/>
    On one occasion, as the crowd was pressing in on Jesus to hear the word of God, He was standing beside the lake of Gennesaret—also known as the Sea of Galilee.
    <verse eid="LUK 5:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 5:2"/>
    He saw two boats lying at the water’s edge. The fishermen had gotten out of them and were washing their nets after a night of fishing.
    <verse eid="LUK 5:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 5:3"/>
    Jesus got into one of the boats, which belonged to Simon Peter. He asked Simon to put out a little distance from the shore. Then Jesus sat down and began to teach the crowds from the boat.
    <verse eid="LUK 5:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 5:4"/>
    When He had finished speaking, He said to Simon, “Put out into the deep water, and let down your nets for a catch.”
    <verse eid="LUK 5:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 5:5"/>
    Simon answered and said, “Master, we worked hard throughout the entire night and caught nothing at all. But because You say so, I will let down the nets.”
    <verse eid="LUK 5:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 5:6"/>
    When they did this, they enclosed such a very large number of fish that their nets began to tear from the weight.
    <verse eid="LUK 5:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 5:7"/>
    They signaled frantically to their partners in the other boat to come and help them. They came and filled both boats so full that the boats began to sink.
    <verse eid="LUK 5:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 5:8"/>
    When Simon Peter saw this miraculous catch, he fell down at Jesus’ knees in awe, saying, “Go away from me, Lord, for I am a sinful man!” 
    <verse eid="LUK 5:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 5:9"/>
    Amazement had seized him and all those with him at the overwhelming catch of fish they had taken.
    <verse eid="LUK 5:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 5:10"/>
    James and John, the sons of Zebedee, who were Simon’s partners, were similarly amazed. But Jesus said to Simon, “Do not be afraid. From now on you will be catching men for the kingdom of God.”
    <verse eid="LUK 5:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 5:11"/>
    When they had brought their boats to shore, they left everything behind—their livelihood, their security—and followed Jesus.
    <verse eid="LUK 5:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Cleansing of a Leper</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 8:1-4; Mk. 1:40-45</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 5:12"/>
    While Jesus was in one of the cities, a man appeared who was covered with leprosy. When he saw Jesus, he fell on his face and begged Him, saying, “Lord, if You are willing, You have the power to make me clean.”
    <verse eid="LUK 5:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 5:13"/>
    Jesus stretched out His hand and touched him—an act that would normally make someone ceremonially unclean. He said, “I am willing. Be made clean.” Immediately the leprosy left him.
    <verse eid="LUK 5:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 5:14"/>
    Jesus commanded him, “Tell no one about this. But go and show yourself to the priest for examination, and make an offering for your cleansing just as Moses commanded in the Law. This will serve as evidence to them of your healing.”
    <verse eid="LUK 5:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Jesus’ Habitual Ministry and Prayer Life</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 5:15"/>
    But the news about Jesus kept spreading even more widely, and large crowds would gather to hear Him teach and to be healed from their sicknesses.
    <verse eid="LUK 5:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 5:16"/>
    Yet Jesus Himself would often withdraw quietly to deserted places to pray. 
    <verse eid="LUK 5:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Healing of a Paralytic</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 9:1-8; Mk. 2:1-12</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 5:17"/>
    On one of those days, while Jesus was teaching, Pharisees and teachers of the Law were sitting there. They had come from every village of Galilee and Judea, and from Jerusalem, to investigate Him. The power of the Lord was present for Jesus to perform healings.
    <verse eid="LUK 5:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 5:18"/>
    Some men came carrying on a stretcher a man who was paralyzed. They were trying to bring him inside and set him before Jesus.
    <verse eid="LUK 5:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 5:19"/>
    When they could not find a way to bring him in because of the dense crowd, they went up on the roof. They removed the tiles and lowered him on his mat down through the opening into the middle of the crowd, right in front of Jesus.
    <verse eid="LUK 5:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 5:20"/>
    When Jesus saw their faith—demonstrated by their persistent effort—He said, “Man, your sins are forgiven you.”
    <verse eid="LUK 5:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 5:21"/>
    The scribes and Pharisees began to question and reason, saying, “Who is this man who speaks blasphemies? Who has the authority to forgive sins except God alone?”
    <verse eid="LUK 5:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 5:22"/>
    But Jesus, knowing their thoughts and reasoning, answered them, “Why are you questioning these things in your hearts?
    <verse eid="LUK 5:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 5:23"/>
    Which is easier to say: ‘Your sins are forgiven you,’ or to say, ‘Get up and walk’? 
    <verse eid="LUK 5:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 5:24"/>
    But so that you may know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins”—He said to the paralyzed man—“I say to you, get up, take up your mat, and go to your house.”
    <verse eid="LUK 5:24"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 5:25"/>
    Immediately the man stood up before them, picked up what he had been lying on, and went home glorifying God.
    <verse eid="LUK 5:25"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 5:26"/>
    Astonishment seized everyone present, and they began to glorify God. They were filled with awe, saying, &quot;We have seen extraordinary and remarkable things today.”
    <verse eid="LUK 5:26"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Calling of Levi</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 9:9-13; Mk. 2:13-17</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 5:27"/>
    After these events, Jesus went out and saw a tax collector named Levi sitting at the tax collection booth. Jesus said to him, “Follow Me.”
    <verse eid="LUK 5:27"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 5:28"/>
    Levi left everything behind, got up immediately, and began to follow Jesus, abandoning his lucrative but despised occupation.
    <verse eid="LUK 5:28"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 5:29"/>
    Levi held a great banquet for Jesus in his house. A large crowd of tax collectors and others were reclining at the table with them.
    <verse eid="LUK 5:29"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 5:30"/>
    The Pharisees and their scribes began to complain to Jesus’ disciples, saying, “Why do you eat and drink with tax collectors and sinners?”
    <verse eid="LUK 5:30"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 5:31"/>
    Jesus answered them, “Those who are in good health have no need of a physician, but those who are sick do.
    <verse eid="LUK 5:31"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 5:32"/>
    I have not come to call the righteous who think they have no need, but sinners to repentance.”
    <verse eid="LUK 5:32"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Question Regarding Fasting</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 9:14-17; Mk. 2:18-22</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 5:33"/>
    They said to Jesus, “The disciples of John the Baptist fast frequently and offer prayers regularly. Likewise, the disciples of the Pharisees do the same. But Your disciples are eating and drinking freely.”
    <verse eid="LUK 5:33"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 5:34"/>
    Jesus responded, “Can you make the wedding guests fast while the bridegroom is with them?
    <verse eid="LUK 5:34"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 5:35"/>
    But days will come when the bridegroom will be taken away from them. Then they will fast in those days when I am gone.”
    <verse eid="LUK 5:35"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 5:36"/>
    Jesus also told them a parable: “No one tears a patch from a new garment and sews it on an old garment. If he does, he will ruin the new garment, and the patch from the new will not match the old.
    <verse eid="LUK 5:36"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 5:37"/>
    No one puts new wine into old wineskins. If he does, the new wine will burst the wineskins as it ferments. The wine will be spilled, and the wineskins will be ruined.
    <verse eid="LUK 5:37"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 5:38"/>
    Instead, new wine must be put into fresh wineskins so both are preserved.
    <verse eid="LUK 5:38"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="39" style="v" sid="LUK 5:39"/>
    No one who has been drinking old wine immediately desires the new, for he says, ‘The old is better.’ ”
    <verse eid="LUK 5:39"/>
  </para>
  <chapter eid="LUK 5"/>
  <chapter number="6" style="c" sid="LUK 6"/>
  <para style="s1">Plucking and Eating Heads of Grain on the Sabbath</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 12:1-8; Mk. 2:23-28</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 6:1"/>
    On a Sabbath day, Jesus was passing through grainfields. His disciples were plucking and eating the heads of grain, rubbing them in their hands to remove the husks.
    <verse eid="LUK 6:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 6:2"/>
    Some of the Pharisees challenged them, saying, “Why are you doing what is not lawful on the Sabbath?”
    <verse eid="LUK 6:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 6:3"/>
    Jesus answered them, “Have you not read in Scripture what David did when he and those with him were hungry?*
    <verse eid="LUK 6:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 6:4"/>
    He entered the house of God and took the consecrated bread of the Presence, ate it, and gave some to those with him. This bread was not lawful for anyone to eat except the priests alone.”
    <verse eid="LUK 6:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 6:5"/>
    Jesus said to them, “The Son of Man is Lord even of the Sabbath.” He has authority over the Sabbath and its proper observance.
    <verse eid="LUK 6:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Jesus Heals a Man with a Withered Hand</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 12:9-14; Mk. 3:1-6</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 6:6"/>
    On another Sabbath, Jesus entered the synagogue and began to teach. A man was there whose right hand was withered.
    <verse eid="LUK 6:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 6:7"/>
    The scribes and Pharisees were watching Jesus carefully to see if He would heal on the Sabbath, so that they might find grounds to accuse Him.
    <verse eid="LUK 6:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 6:8"/>
    But Jesus knew their thoughts and intentions. He said to the man with the withered hand, “Get up and stand here in the middle where everyone can see.” The man rose and stood there.
    <verse eid="LUK 6:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 6:9"/>
    Jesus said to them, “I ask you: Is it lawful on the Sabbath to do good or to do evil? To save a life or to destroy it?”
    <verse eid="LUK 6:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 6:10"/>
    He looked around at all of them, then said to the man, “Stretch out your hand.” He did so, and his hand was completely restored.
    <verse eid="LUK 6:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 6:11"/>
    But the Pharisees were filled with fury. They began discussing among themselves what they might do to Jesus.
    <verse eid="LUK 6:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Choosing of the Twelve</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 10:1-4; Mk. 3:13-19</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 6:12"/>
    In those days, Jesus went out to the mountain to pray. He spent the entire night in prayer to God—seeking the Father’s will.
    <verse eid="LUK 6:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 6:13"/>
    When day came, He summoned His disciples to Himself and chose twelve from among them, whom He also named apostles—meaning “sent ones”:
    <verse eid="LUK 6:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 6:14"/>
    Simon, whom He also named Peter, and Andrew his brother, James and John, Philip and Bartholomew,
    <verse eid="LUK 6:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 6:15"/>
    Matthew and Thomas, James the son of Alphaeus, and Simon who was called the Zealot,
    <verse eid="LUK 6:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 6:16"/>
    Judas the son of James, and Judas Iscariot, who became the traitor.
    <verse eid="LUK 6:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Jesus Before a Great Multitude</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 4:23-25</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 6:17"/>
    Jesus descended from the mountain with the twelve and stood on a level place. A large crowd of His disciples was there, along with a great multitude of people from all Judea and Jerusalem and the coastal region of Tyre and Sidon.
    <verse eid="LUK 6:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 6:18"/>
    They had come to hear Him teach and to be healed of their diseases. Those who were troubled and tormented by unclean spirits were also being healed. 
    <verse eid="LUK 6:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 6:19"/>
    The whole crowd was trying to touch Him, because power was flowing out from Him and healing everyone.
    <verse eid="LUK 6:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Sermon on the Plain: Blessings and Woes</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 5:1-12</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 6:20"/>
    Jesus lifted up His eyes toward His disciples and began to teach, saying: “Blessed are you who are poor, for yours is the kingdom of God.
    <verse eid="LUK 6:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 6:21"/>
    Blessed are you who are hungry now for righteousness, for you will be satisfied. Blessed are you who weep now, for you will laugh with joy.
    <verse eid="LUK 6:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 6:22"/>
    Blessed are you when people hate you, when they exclude you from their fellowship, when they insult and defame you, and when they reject your name as evil on account of the Son of Man.
    <verse eid="LUK 6:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 6:23"/>
    Rejoice in that day and leap for joy, for your reward is great in heaven. Their fathers treated the prophets in the same way.
    <verse eid="LUK 6:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 6:24"/>
    But woe to you who are rich and comfortable, for you have already received your consolation and comfort.
    <verse eid="LUK 6:24"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 6:25"/>
    Woe to you who are well fed now, for you will be hungry. Woe to you who laugh now, for you will mourn and weep.
    <verse eid="LUK 6:25"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 6:26"/>
    Woe to you when all people speak well of you, for their fathers treated the false prophets in the same way.”
    <verse eid="LUK 6:26"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Sermon on the Plain: Love for Enemies</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 5:38-48; 7:12a</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 6:27"/>
    “But to you who are listening, I say: Love your enemies, do good to those who hate you,
    <verse eid="LUK 6:27"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 6:28"/>
    bless those who curse you, pray for those who mistreat and insult you.
    <verse eid="LUK 6:28"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 6:29"/>
    When someone strikes you, refuse retaliation and be willing to endure further injustice without seeking revenge. And if someone forcefully takes your outer cloak, be ready to surrender even your inner garment rather than respond with resistance rooted in personal vengeance.
    <verse eid="LUK 6:29"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 6:30"/>
    Be generous toward those who ask for help, and when someone takes what is yours, do not insist on reclaiming it in a vindictive or grasping spirit. 
    <verse eid="LUK 6:30"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 6:31"/>
    Treat others just as you desire that people would treat you. 
    <verse eid="LUK 6:31"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 6:32"/>
    If you love only those who love you, what credit is that to you? Even sinners love those who love them in return.
    <verse eid="LUK 6:32"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 6:33"/>
    If you do good only to those who do good to you, what credit is that? Even sinners do the same.
    <verse eid="LUK 6:33"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 6:34"/>
    If you lend only to those from whom you expect to be repaid, what credit is that? Even sinners lend to sinners, expecting to receive back the same amount.
    <verse eid="LUK 6:34"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 6:35"/>
    But love your enemies, do good to them, and lend, expecting nothing in return. Then your reward will be great, and you will be sons of the Most High, for He is kind to the ungrateful and evil.
    <verse eid="LUK 6:35"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 6:36"/>
    Be merciful and compassionate, just as your Father is merciful.”
    <verse eid="LUK 6:36"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Sermon on the Plain: Judging and Condemning Others and Forgiveness</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 7:1-5</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 6:37"/>
    “Do not judge others, and you will not be judged. Do not condemn, and you will not be condemned. Forgive, and you will be forgiven.
    <verse eid="LUK 6:37"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 6:38"/>
    Give generously, and it will be given to you. A good measure—pressed down, shaken together, and running over—will be poured into your lap. For with the measure you use to measure others, it will be measured back to you.”
    <verse eid="LUK 6:38"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="39" style="v" sid="LUK 6:39"/>
    Jesus also spoke a parable to them: “Can a blind person guide another blind person? Will they not both fall into a pit?
    <verse eid="LUK 6:39"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="40" style="v" sid="LUK 6:40"/>
    A disciple is not above his teacher, but everyone who is fully trained will be like his teacher.
    <verse eid="LUK 6:40"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="41" style="v" sid="LUK 6:41"/>
    Why do you look at the speck of sawdust in your brother’s eye but fail to notice the log in your own eye?
    <verse eid="LUK 6:41"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="42" style="v" sid="LUK 6:42"/>
    How can you say to your brother, ‘Brother, let me remove the speck from your eye,’ when you yourself do not see the log in your own eye? You hypocrite! First remove the log from your own eye, and then you will see clearly to remove the speck from your brother’s eye.”
    <verse eid="LUK 6:42"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Sermon on the Plain: The Good Man and the Evil Person</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 7:17-20; 12:34b-35</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="43" style="v" sid="LUK 6:43"/>
    “No good tree bears bad fruit, nor does a bad tree bear good fruit.
    <verse eid="LUK 6:43"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="44" style="v" sid="LUK 6:44"/>
    Each tree is known and recognized by its own fruit. People do not gather figs from thornbushes, nor do they pick grapes from brambles.
    <verse eid="LUK 6:44"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="45" style="v" sid="LUK 6:45"/>
    The good person out of the good treasure stored in his heart brings forth good. The evil person out of his evil treasure brings forth evil. For out of the abundance of the heart, the mouth speaks.”
    <verse eid="LUK 6:45"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Sermon on the Plain: The Two Foundations</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 7:24-27</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="46" style="v" sid="LUK 6:46"/>
    “Why do you call Me ‘Lord, Lord,’ and yet do not do what I say? 
    <verse eid="LUK 6:46"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="47" style="v" sid="LUK 6:47"/>
    Everyone who comes to Me and hears My words and acts on them—I will show you what he is like:
    <verse eid="LUK 6:47"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="48" style="v" sid="LUK 6:48"/>
    He is like a man building a house who dug down deep and laid the foundation on solid rock. When a flood came, the torrent crashed against that house but could not shake it, because it had been well built on a firm foundation.
    <verse eid="LUK 6:48"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="49" style="v" sid="LUK 6:49"/>
    But the one who hears My words and does not put them into practice is like a man who built a house on the ground without digging a foundation. When the torrent burst against it, the house immediately collapsed, and the destruction of that house was complete and catastrophic.”
    <verse eid="LUK 6:49"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Notes</para>
  <para style="p">3 This is a reference to I Sam. 21:1-6</para>
  <chapter eid="LUK 6"/>
  <chapter number="7" style="c" sid="LUK 7"/>
  <para style="s1">The Healing of a Centurion’s Servant</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 8:5-13; Jn. 4:43-54</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 7:1"/>
    When Jesus had completed delivering all His teachings within hearing of the people, He entered the city of Capernaum.
    <verse eid="LUK 7:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 7:2"/>
    A certain Roman centurion had a servant who was highly valued and precious to him. This servant was gravely ill and at the point of death.
    <verse eid="LUK 7:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 7:3"/>
    When the centurion heard about Jesus and His healing power, he sent Jewish elders to Him, earnestly requesting that Jesus would come and heal his servant.
    <verse eid="LUK 7:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 7:4"/>
    When they came to Jesus, they began to plead with Him urgently, saying, “This centurion is worthy that You should grant him this favor.
    <verse eid="LUK 7:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 7:5"/>
    He loves our nation, and he himself built the synagogue for us at his own expense.” The centurion was a Gentile who honored the God of Israel.
    <verse eid="LUK 7:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 7:6"/>
    So Jesus went with them. When He was not far from the house, the centurion sent friends to meet Him with a message: “Lord, do not trouble Yourself further, for I am not worthy that You should enter under my roof.
    <verse eid="LUK 7:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 7:7"/>
    For this reason, I did not even consider myself worthy to come to You in person. But simply speak a word, and let my servant be healed.
    <verse eid="LUK 7:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 7:8"/>
    For I myself am a man placed under authority, having soldiers under my command. I say to one, ‘Go,’ and he goes; to another, ‘Come,’ and he comes; and to my servant, ‘Do this,’ and he does it. If I have such authority, how much more do You?”
    <verse eid="LUK 7:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 7:9"/>
    When Jesus heard these words, He marveled at the centurion’s faith. Turning to the crowd following Him, He said, “I tell you, not even in Israel have I found such remarkable faith as this Gentile has shown.”
    <verse eid="LUK 7:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 7:10"/>
    When those who had been sent returned to the house, they found the servant in perfect health, completely restored.
    <verse eid="LUK 7:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Raising of a Widow’s Son</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 7:11"/>
    On the following day, Jesus traveled to a town called Nain. His disciples and a large crowd accompanied Him on the journey.
    <verse eid="LUK 7:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 7:12"/>
    As He approached the gate of the city, a funeral procession was coming out. A dead man was being carried out—the only son of his mother, and she was a widow. A substantial crowd from the city was with her in mourning.
    <verse eid="LUK 7:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 7:13"/>
    When the Lord saw her, He was moved with deep compassion for her situation. He said to her gently, “Do not weep.”
    <verse eid="LUK 7:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 7:14"/>
    Jesus came up and touched the burial bier, and those carrying it stopped. He said with authority, “Young man, I say to you, arise!”
    <verse eid="LUK 7:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 7:15"/>
    The dead man sat up and began to speak. Jesus gave him back to his mother, alive and well.
    <verse eid="LUK 7:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 7:16"/>
    Fear and awe seized everyone present, and they began to glorify God, saying, “A great prophet has risen among us!” and “God has visited and looked upon His people with favor!”
    <verse eid="LUK 7:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 7:17"/>
    This report concerning Jesus spread throughout all of Judea and into all the surrounding region.
    <verse eid="LUK 7:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Delegates from John the Baptist</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 11:2-19</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 7:18"/>
    John’s disciples brought him a full report about all these remarkable things Jesus was doing.
    <verse eid="LUK 7:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 7:19"/>
    John summoned two of his disciples and sent them to the Lord with a question: “Are You the Coming One we have been expecting, or should we look for someone else?”
    <verse eid="LUK 7:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 7:20"/>
    When the men came to Jesus, they said, “John the Baptist sent us to You to ask, ‘Are You the Coming One, or should we look for another?’ ”
    <verse eid="LUK 7:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 7:21"/>
    At that very hour, Jesus was healing many people of diseases, afflictions, and evil spirits. He was also freely granting sight to many who were blind.
    <verse eid="LUK 7:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 7:22"/>
    Jesus answered the messengers, “Go back and report to John what you have seen and heard: The blind receive sight, the lame walk, those with leprosy are cleansed, the deaf hear, the dead are raised to life, and the good news is being proclaimed to the poor.
    <verse eid="LUK 7:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 7:23"/>
    Blessed is the one who does not stumble in faith or take offense at Me because I do not meet his expectations.”
    <verse eid="LUK 7:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Concerning John the Baptist</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 7:24"/>
    After John’s messengers had departed, Jesus began to speak to the crowds about John: “What did you go out into the wilderness to see? A reed shaken and swayed by the wind—someone weak and changeable?
    <verse eid="LUK 7:24"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 7:25"/>
    What then did you go out to see? A man dressed in soft, luxurious garments? No! Those who wear splendid clothing and live in luxury are found in royal palaces, not in the wilderness.
    <verse eid="LUK 7:25"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 7:26"/>
    What then did you go out to see? A prophet? Yes, I tell you, and far more than a prophet.
    <verse eid="LUK 7:26"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 7:27"/>
    This is the one about whom it is written in Scripture: 
    <verse eid="LUK 7:27"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">‘Behold, I am sending My messenger ahead of You, </para>
  <para style="p">who will prepare Your way before You.’*</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 7:28"/>
    I tell you, among those born of women, there is no one greater than John. Yet the one who is least in the kingdom of God is greater than he.” 
    <verse eid="LUK 7:28"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 7:29"/>
    Luke adds parenthetically: When all the people heard this—even the despised tax collectors—they acknowledged God's way as just and right, having been baptized with John’s baptism.
    <verse eid="LUK 7:29"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 7:30"/>
    But the Pharisees and experts in the Law rejected God’s purpose for themselves, refusing to be baptized by John.
    <verse eid="LUK 7:30"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 7:31"/>
    Jesus continued, “To what then shall I compare the people of this generation? What are they like?
    <verse eid="LUK 7:31"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 7:32"/>
    They are like children sitting in the marketplace, calling out to one another and saying, 
    <verse eid="LUK 7:32"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">‘We played wedding music on the flute for you, but you did not dance. </para>
  <para style="p">We sang funeral songs, but you did not mourn and wail.’</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 7:33"/>
    For John the Baptist came neither eating bread nor drinking wine, and you say, ‘He has a demon and is possessed.’
    <verse eid="LUK 7:33"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 7:34"/>
    The Son of Man has come eating and drinking normally, and you say, ‘Look at Him! A glutton and a drunkard, a friend of tax collectors and sinners!’ You cannot be pleased no matter what we do.
    <verse eid="LUK 7:34"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 7:35"/>
    Yet wisdom is vindicated and proven right by all her children—by those who respond rightly to God’s message.”
    <verse eid="LUK 7:35"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Forgiveness of a Sinful Woman</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 7:36"/>
    One of the Pharisees invited Jesus to dine with him. Jesus entered the Pharisee’s house and reclined at the table for the meal.
    <verse eid="LUK 7:36"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 7:37"/>
    A woman in the city who was known as a sinner learned that Jesus was reclining at the table in the Pharisee’s house. She brought an alabaster jar filled with expensive fragrant oil.
    <verse eid="LUK 7:37"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 7:38"/>
    Standing behind Jesus at His feet, weeping with repentance, she began to wet His feet with her tears. She wiped them with her hair, kissed His feet repeatedly, and anointed them with the fragrant oil.
    <verse eid="LUK 7:38"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="39" style="v" sid="LUK 7:39"/>
    When the Pharisee who had invited Jesus saw this, he said to himself, “If this man were truly a prophet, He would know who and what kind of woman this is who is touching Him—that she is a notorious sinner.”
    <verse eid="LUK 7:39"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="40" style="v" sid="LUK 7:40"/>
    Jesus responded to his unspoken thoughts, saying, “Simon, I have something to say to you.” Simon replied, “Speak, Teacher.”
    <verse eid="LUK 7:40"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="41" style="v" sid="LUK 7:41"/>
    Jesus said, “There were two debtors who owed money to a certain creditor. One owed five hundred denarii—nearly two years’ wages—and the other owed fifty.
    <verse eid="LUK 7:41"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="42" style="v" sid="LUK 7:42"/>
    When neither of them had the means to repay their debts, the creditor graciously pardoned and forgave the debts of both. Now which of them will love him more?”
    <verse eid="LUK 7:42"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="43" style="v" sid="LUK 7:43"/>
    Simon answered, “I assume it would be the one to whom he forgave the larger debt.” Jesus said to him, “You have judged correctly.”
    <verse eid="LUK 7:43"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="44" style="v" sid="LUK 7:44"/>
    Then turning toward the woman, Jesus said to Simon, “Do you see this woman? When I entered your house, you did not provide Me with water for My feet—a basic courtesy. But she has wet My feet with her tears and wiped them with her hair.
    <verse eid="LUK 7:44"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="45" style="v" sid="LUK 7:45"/>
    You did not give Me a customary kiss of greeting. But from the time I entered, she has not stopped kissing My feet.
    <verse eid="LUK 7:45"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="46" style="v" sid="LUK 7:46"/>
    You did not anoint My head with ordinary olive oil. But she has anointed My feet with expensive fragrant oil.
    <verse eid="LUK 7:46"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="47" style="v" sid="LUK 7:47"/>
    Therefore, I tell you, her many sins have been forgiven, as demonstrated by the fact that she loved much. But the one to whom little is forgiven loves only a little.”
    <verse eid="LUK 7:47"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="48" style="v" sid="LUK 7:48"/>
    Jesus said to the woman, “Your sins have been forgiven.”
    <verse eid="LUK 7:48"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="49" style="v" sid="LUK 7:49"/>
    Those reclining at the table with Jesus began to say among themselves, “Who is this man who even forgives sins?”
    <verse eid="LUK 7:49"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="50" style="v" sid="LUK 7:50"/>
    Jesus said to the woman, “Your faith has saved you. Go in peace.”
    <verse eid="LUK 7:50"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Notes</para>
  <para style="p">27 Quoted from Mal. 3:1</para>
  <chapter eid="LUK 7"/>
  <chapter number="8" style="c" sid="LUK 8"/>
  <para style="s1">Women Who Assisted Jesus and His Disciples</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 8:1"/>
    Soon afterward, Jesus was traveling from one city and village to another, preaching and proclaiming the good news of the kingdom of God. The twelve apostles were with Him.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 8:2"/>
    Certain women who had been healed from evil spirits and diseases were also traveling with them: Mary, called Magdalene, from whom seven demons had been cast out;
    <verse eid="LUK 8:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 8:3"/>
    Joanna, the wife of Chuza who was Herod’s household manager; Susanna; and many other women who were providing for their material needs from their own financial resources.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Parable of the Sower</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 13:1-9; Mk. 4:1-9</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 8:4"/>
    As a large crowd was gathering and people were coming to Jesus from one city after another, He spoke to them by way of a parable:
    <verse eid="LUK 8:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 8:5"/>
    “A farmer went out to sow his seed. As he was scattering the seed, some fell along the pathway and was trampled underfoot. The birds of the sky came and devoured it.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 8:6"/>
    Other seed fell on rocky ground. When it sprouted and grew, it quickly withered away because it had no moisture.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 8:7"/>
    Other seed fell in the midst of thorns. The thorns grew up with the plants and choked them out.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 8:8"/>
    Still other seed fell into good, fertile soil. It grew and produced a crop yielding a hundred times what was sown.” After saying these things, Jesus called out, “The one who has ears to hear, let him hear and understand!”
    <verse eid="LUK 8:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Purpose of the Parables</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 13:10-17; Mk. 4:10-12</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 8:9"/>
    His disciples began to ask Him what this parable meant and signified.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 8:10"/>
    Jesus said, “To you it has been granted to know the mysteries and secrets of the kingdom of God. But to the rest, these truths come in parables, so that ‘seeing they may not perceive, and hearing they may not understand.’
    <verse eid="LUK 8:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Interpretation of the Parable of the Sower</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 13:18-23; Mk. 4:13-20</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 8:11"/>
    Jesus explained: “Now this is what the parable means: The seed represents the word of God.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 8:12"/>
    Those along the pathway are the ones who hear the word, but then the devil comes and snatches away the word from their hearts, so that they may not believe and be saved.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 8:13"/>
    Those on the rocky ground are the ones who, when they hear the word, receive it with joy and enthusiasm. But these people have no root. They believe for a while, but in a time of testing and trial, they fall away.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 8:14"/>
    As for what fell among the thorns—these are the ones who hear the word, but as they go along through life, they are choked by the cares, riches, and pleasures of this life. They do not bring fruit to maturity.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 8:15"/>
    As for the seed that fell on good soil—these are the ones who, upon hearing the word with a noble and good heart, hold fast to it firmly. They bear fruit through perseverance and patient endurance.”
    <verse eid="LUK 8:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">A Lamp on a Stand</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Mk. 4:21-25</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 8:16"/>
    Jesus continued His teaching: “No one lights a lamp and then covers it with a clay jar or puts it under a bed. Instead, he sets it on a lampstand so that those who enter the room may see the light.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 8:17"/>
    For nothing is concealed that will not eventually become manifest. Nothing is kept secret that will not be brought to light.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 8:18"/>
    Therefore, pay careful attention to how you listen and hear. For whoever has spiritual understanding, more will be given to him. But whoever does not have understanding, even what he thinks he has will be taken away from him.”
    <verse eid="LUK 8:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Jesus’ Mother and Brothers</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 12:46-50; Mk. 3:31-35</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 8:19"/>
    Jesus’ mother and brothers came to see Him, but they were not able to reach Him because of the dense crowd.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 8:20"/>
    Someone reported to Him, “Your mother and Your brothers are standing outside, wanting to see You.”
    <verse eid="LUK 8:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 8:21"/>
    But Jesus responded, “My mother and My brothers are these—the ones who hear the word of God and put it into practice.”
    <verse eid="LUK 8:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Jesus Calms a Storm</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 8:23-27; Mk. 4:35-41</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 8:22"/>
    One day Jesus got into a boat with His disciples and said to them, “Let us cross over to the other side of the lake.” So they set sail.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 8:23"/>
    As they were sailing across, Jesus fell asleep. A violent storm of wind descended upon the lake. The boat was being swamped with water, and they were in serious danger of sinking.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 8:24"/>
    The disciples came to Jesus and woke Him urgently, saying, “Master, Master, we are perishing!” Jesus awoke, rebuked the wind and the raging water, and they ceased. There was a great calm.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:24"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 8:25"/>
    Jesus said to His disciples, “Where is your faith?” But they were afraid and filled with awe, saying to one another, “Who then is this man, that He commands even the winds and the water, and they obey Him?”
    <verse eid="LUK 8:25"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Healing of the Gerasene Demon-Possessed Man</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 8:28-34; Mk. 5:1-20</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 8:26"/>
    They sailed across to the region of the Gerasenes, which is on the opposite side of the lake from Galilee.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:26"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 8:27"/>
    When Jesus stepped out onto the land, a certain man from the city met Him. This man had demons and had not worn clothes for a considerable time. He was not living in a house but among the tombs.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:27"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 8:28"/>
    When he saw Jesus, he cried out, fell down before Him, and said in a loud voice, “What do I have to do with You, Jesus, Son of the Most High God? I beg You, do not torment me!”
    <verse eid="LUK 8:28"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 8:29"/>
    Jesus had been commanding the unclean spirit to come out of the man. Many times it had seized him. Though he was bound with chains and shackles and kept under guard, he would break the bonds. The demon would drive him into solitary places.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:29"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 8:30"/>
    Jesus asked him, “What is your name?” He answered, “Legion”—because many demons had entered into him.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:30"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 8:31"/>
    The demons kept begging Jesus not to order them to depart into the abyss—the place of eternal confinement.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:31"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 8:32"/>
    A large herd of pigs was feeding there on the hillside. The demons implored Jesus to permit them to enter the pigs. Jesus gave them permission.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:32"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 8:33"/>
    The demons came out of the man and entered the pigs. The herd rushed down the steep bank into the lake and drowned.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:33"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 8:34"/>
    When those tending the pigs saw what had happened, they fled and reported it in the city and throughout the countryside.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:34"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 8:35"/>
    People went out to see what had taken place. They came to Jesus and found the man from whom the demons had gone out. He was sitting at Jesus’ feet, clothed and in his right mind. They were frightened at the transformation.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:35"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 8:36"/>
    Those who had witnessed the event reported to the others how the demon-possessed man had been healed and delivered.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:36"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 8:37"/>
    All the people from the surrounding region of the Gerasenes requested Jesus to depart from them, for they were seized with great fear. So Jesus got into the boat and returned across the lake.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:37"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 8:38"/>
    The man from whom the demons had gone out kept begging Jesus to let him accompany Him as a disciple.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:38"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="39" style="v" sid="LUK 8:39"/>
    But Jesus sent him away with different instructions, saying, “Return to your home and tell what great things God has done for you.” The man went away, proclaiming throughout the whole city all that Jesus had done for him.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:39"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Healing of a Woman with the Flow of Blood and the Raising of Jairus’ Daughter</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 9:18-26; Mk. 5:21-43</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="40" style="v" sid="LUK 8:40"/>
    When Jesus returned to the other side, the crowd welcomed Him, for they had all been waiting for Him.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:40"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="41" style="v" sid="LUK 8:41"/>
    A man named Jairus came to Jesus. He was a ruler of the synagogue. Falling down at Jesus’ feet, he began to beg Him earnestly to come to his house,
    <verse eid="LUK 8:41"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="42" style="v" sid="LUK 8:42"/>
    because his only daughter, about twelve years old, was dying.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:42"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">As Jesus was going, the crowds were pressing against Him from all sides.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="43" style="v" sid="LUK 8:43"/>
    A woman was there who had suffered from a discharge of blood for twelve years. She had spent all her living on physicians but had not been healed by anyone.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:43"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="44" style="v" sid="LUK 8:44"/>
    She came up behind Jesus in the crowd and touched the fringe of His garment. Immediately her flow of blood stopped.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:44"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="45" style="v" sid="LUK 8:45"/>
    Jesus said, “Who is the one who touched Me?” When everyone denied it, Peter said, “Master, the crowds are pressing and thronging against You from every direction.”
    <verse eid="LUK 8:45"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="46" style="v" sid="LUK 8:46"/>
    But Jesus insisted, “Someone deliberately touched Me, for I know that power has gone out from Me.”
    <verse eid="LUK 8:46"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="47" style="v" sid="LUK 8:47"/>
    When the woman saw that she had not escaped notice, she came trembling. She fell down before Him and declared before all the people why she had touched Him and how she had been immediately healed.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:47"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="48" style="v" sid="LUK 8:48"/>
    Jesus said to her, “Daughter, your faith has made you well. Go in peace.”
    <verse eid="LUK 8:48"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="49" style="v" sid="LUK 8:49"/>
    While Jesus was still speaking, someone came from the house of the synagogue ruler with devastating news: “Your daughter is dead. Do not trouble the Teacher any longer.”
    <verse eid="LUK 8:49"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="50" style="v" sid="LUK 8:50"/>
    But when Jesus heard this, He responded, “Do not be afraid. Only believe, and she will be made well.”
    <verse eid="LUK 8:50"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="51" style="v" sid="LUK 8:51"/>
    When Jesus arrived at the house, He did not permit anyone to enter with Him except Peter, John, James, and the child’s father and mother.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:51"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="52" style="v" sid="LUK 8:52"/>
    Everyone was weeping and mourning for the girl. But Jesus said, “Do not weep. She is not dead but sleeping.” 
    <verse eid="LUK 8:52"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="53" style="v" sid="LUK 8:53"/>
    They began ridiculing Jesus, knowing with certainty that she was dead.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:53"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="54" style="v" sid="LUK 8:54"/>
    But Jesus took her by the hand and called out, “Child, arise!”
    <verse eid="LUK 8:54"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="55" style="v" sid="LUK 8:55"/>
    Her spirit returned to her body, and she stood up immediately. Jesus ordered that something should be given to her to eat, demonstrating that she was truly alive.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:55"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="56" style="v" sid="LUK 8:56"/>
    Her parents were utterly astonished. But Jesus commanded them not to tell anyone what had taken place.
    <verse eid="LUK 8:56"/>
  </para>
  <chapter eid="LUK 8"/>
  <chapter number="9" style="c" sid="LUK 9"/>
  <para style="s1">Jesus Commissioned the Twelve</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 10:5-15; Mk. 6:7-13</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 9:1"/>
    Jesus called the twelve apostles together and gave them power and authority over all demons and to heal diseases.
    <verse eid="LUK 9:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 9:2"/>
    He sent them out to preach the kingdom of God and to heal the sick.
    <verse eid="LUK 9:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 9:3"/>
    Jesus said to them, “Take nothing for your journey—no staff, no bag, no bread, no money. Do not even take two tunics apiece.
    <verse eid="LUK 9:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 9:4"/>
    Whatever house you enter, stay there until you depart from that town.
    <verse eid="LUK 9:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 9:5"/>
    Wherever people do not receive you, when you leave that city, shake the dust from your feet as a testimony against them.”
    <verse eid="LUK 9:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 9:6"/>
    So the apostles departed and traveled throughout the villages, preaching the gospel and healing people everywhere.
    <verse eid="LUK 9:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Thorough Perplexity of Herod</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 14:1-12; Mk. 6:14-29</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 9:7"/>
    Herod the tetrarch heard about all that was happening through Jesus’ ministry. He was thoroughly confused, because some people were saying that John the Baptist had been raised from the dead,
    <verse eid="LUK 9:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 9:8"/>
    while others claimed that Elijah had appeared, and still others that one of the ancient prophets had risen.
    <verse eid="LUK 9:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 9:9"/>
    Herod said, “I beheaded John myself. But who is this about whom I hear such things?” He kept seeking an opportunity to see Jesus.
    <verse eid="LUK 9:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Miraculous Feeding of the Five Thousand</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 14:13-21; Mk. 6:30-44; Jn. 6:1-14</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 9:10"/>
    When the apostles returned from their mission, they gave Jesus a full report of all they had done. Jesus took them and withdrew privately to a town called Bethsaida.
    <verse eid="LUK 9:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 9:11"/>
    But when the crowds learned where He had gone, they followed Him. Jesus welcomed them and began speaking to them about the kingdom of God. He continued healing those who needed healing.
    <verse eid="LUK 9:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 9:12"/>
    As the day began to draw to a close, the twelve came to Jesus and said, “Dismiss the crowd so they can go into the surrounding villages and countryside to find lodging and get food, because we are in a deserted place here.”
    <verse eid="LUK 9:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 9:13"/>
    But Jesus said to them, “You give them something to eat.” They protested, “We have no more than five loaves and two fish—unless we go and buy food for all these people.”
    <verse eid="LUK 9:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 9:14"/>
    There were approximately five thousand men present, not counting women and children. Jesus said to His disciples, “Have them sit down in groups of about fifty each.”
    <verse eid="LUK 9:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 9:15"/>
    The disciples did as Jesus instructed and made everyone sit down.
    <verse eid="LUK 9:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 9:16"/>
    Jesus took the five loaves and the two fish, looked up to heaven, blessed and gave thanks, then broke them. He kept giving them to the disciples to distribute before the crowd.
    <verse eid="LUK 9:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 9:17"/>
    They all ate and were satisfied. What was left over was picked up—twelve baskets full of broken pieces.
    <verse eid="LUK 9:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Jesus is the Christ of God</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 16:13-19; Mk. 8:27-29</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 9:18"/>
    On one occasion while Jesus was praying in solitude, the disciples were with Him nearby. He questioned them, saying, “Who do the crowds say that I am?”
    <verse eid="LUK 9:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 9:19"/>
    They answered, “Some say John the Baptist; others say Elijah; and still others say that one of the ancient prophets has risen.”
    <verse eid="LUK 9:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 9:20"/>
    Jesus asked them, “But you—who do you say that I am?” Peter answered, “The Christ of God—the Messiah.”
    <verse eid="LUK 9:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Jesus Speaks of His Death and Resurrection the First Time</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 16:20-23; Mk. 8:33</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 9:21"/>
    Jesus warned them sternly and commanded them to tell this to no one.
    <verse eid="LUK 9:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 9:22"/>
    He said, “It is necessary for the Son of Man to suffer many things, to be rejected by the elders, chief priests, and scribes, to be killed, and on the third day to be raised.”
    <verse eid="LUK 9:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Following Jesus</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 16:24-28; Mk. 8:34-9:1</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 9:23"/>
    Jesus began to say to everyone, “If anyone desires to come after Me, he must deny himself, take up his cross daily, and follow Me.
    <verse eid="LUK 9:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 9:24"/>
    For whoever desires to save his life will lose it, but whoever loses his life for My sake—this person will save it.
    <verse eid="LUK 9:24"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 9:25"/>
    For what does it profit a person if he gains the whole world yet loses or forfeits his own soul?
    <verse eid="LUK 9:25"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 9:26"/>
    For whoever is ashamed of Me and of My words, the Son of Man will be ashamed of this one when He comes in His glory and the glory of the Father and of the holy angels.
    <verse eid="LUK 9:26"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 9:27"/>
    But I tell you with certainty: There are some standing here who will not taste death until they see the kingdom of God.”
    <verse eid="LUK 9:27"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Transfiguration</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 17:1-8; Mk. 9:2-8</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 9:28"/>
    About eight days after saying these things, Jesus took Peter, John, and James and went up on the mountain to pray.
    <verse eid="LUK 9:28"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 9:29"/>
    As He was praying, the appearance of His face was dramatically altered, and His clothing became white and flashing like lightning.
    <verse eid="LUK 9:29"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 9:30"/>
    Suddenly two men were talking with Him—Moses and Elijah.
    <verse eid="LUK 9:30"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 9:31"/>
    They appeared in glorious splendor and were speaking about His departure, which He was about to accomplish in Jerusalem—referring to His death, resurrection, and ascension.
    <verse eid="LUK 9:31"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 9:32"/>
    Peter and those with him were heavy with sleep. But when they became fully awake, they saw Jesus’ glory and the two men standing with Him.
    <verse eid="LUK 9:32"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 9:33"/>
    As Moses and Elijah were parting from Jesus, Peter said, “Master, it is wonderful for us to be here. Let us make three shelters—one for You, one for Moses, and one for Elijah.” He did not know what he was saying.
    <verse eid="LUK 9:33"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 9:34"/>
    While Peter was speaking, a cloud appeared and began to overshadow them. They became afraid as they entered the cloud.
    <verse eid="LUK 9:34"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 9:35"/>
    A voice came from the cloud, declaring, “This is My Son, the One whom I have chosen. Listen to Him!”
    <verse eid="LUK 9:35"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 9:36"/>
    When the voice had spoken, Jesus was found alone. The disciples kept silent about this and told no one in those days anything of what they had seen.
    <verse eid="LUK 9:36"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Healing of a Boy with an Unclean Spirit</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 17:14-18; Mk. 9:14-27</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 9:37"/>
    On the next day, when they had come down from the mountain, a large crowd met Jesus.
    <verse eid="LUK 9:37"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 9:38"/>
    A man from the crowd cried out desperately, “Teacher, I beg You to look with compassion on my son, because he is my only child.
    <verse eid="LUK 9:38"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="39" style="v" sid="LUK 9:39"/>
    A spirit seizes him, and suddenly he cries out. It convulses him with foaming at the mouth. It departs from him only with great difficulty, crushing and bruising him severely as it leaves.
    <verse eid="LUK 9:39"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="40" style="v" sid="LUK 9:40"/>
    I begged Your disciples to cast it out, but they were not able to do so.”
    <verse eid="LUK 9:40"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="41" style="v" sid="LUK 9:41"/>
    Jesus responded, “O unbelieving and perverse generation! How long must I remain with you and endure you? Bring your son here.”
    <verse eid="LUK 9:41"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="42" style="v" sid="LUK 9:42"/>
    Even as the boy was approaching, the demon threw him to the ground and convulsed him violently. But Jesus rebuked the unclean spirit, healed the boy completely, and gave him back to his father.
    <verse eid="LUK 9:42"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="43" style="v" sid="LUK 9:43"/>
    Everyone was struck with amazement at the majesty and greatness of God.
    <verse eid="LUK 9:43"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Jesus Speaks of His Death the Second Time</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 17:22-23; Mk. 9:30-32</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">While everyone was marveling at all that Jesus was doing, He said to His disciples,</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="44" style="v" sid="LUK 9:44"/>
    “Let these words sink deeply into your ears: The Son of Man is about to be betrayed into the hands of men.”
    <verse eid="LUK 9:44"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="45" style="v" sid="LUK 9:45"/>
    But the disciples did not understand this saying. It was hidden from them so that they could not grasp it. They were afraid to ask Him about this statement.
    <verse eid="LUK 9:45"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Argument on Who is Greatest Among the Disciples</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 18:1-5; Mk. 9:33-37</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="46" style="v" sid="LUK 9:46"/>
    An argument arose among the disciples regarding which of them might be the greatest.
    <verse eid="LUK 9:46"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="47" style="v" sid="LUK 9:47"/>
    Jesus, knowing the reasoning of their hearts, took a child and had him stand beside Him.
    <verse eid="LUK 9:47"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="48" style="v" sid="LUK 9:48"/>
    He said to them, “Whoever receives this child in My name receives Me. And whoever receives Me receives the One who sent Me. For the one who is least among all of you—this is the one who is truly great.”
    <verse eid="LUK 9:48"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Whoever Is Not Against You Is for You</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Mk. 9:38-40</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="49" style="v" sid="LUK 9:49"/>
    John responded, “Master, we saw someone casting out demons in Your name, and we kept trying to prevent him because he does not follow with us in our group.”
    <verse eid="LUK 9:49"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="50" style="v" sid="LUK 9:50"/>
    But Jesus said to him, “Do not prevent him, for whoever is not against you is for you.” 
    <verse eid="LUK 9:50"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Rejection of Jesus in a Samaritan Village</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="51" style="v" sid="LUK 9:51"/>
    As the time approached for Jesus to be taken up to heaven, He resolutely set His face to go to Jerusalem.
    <verse eid="LUK 9:51"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="52" style="v" sid="LUK 9:52"/>
    He sent messengers ahead of Him. They went and entered a Samaritan village to make preparations for Him.
    <verse eid="LUK 9:52"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="53" style="v" sid="LUK 9:53"/>
    But the people did not receive Him because His destination was Jerusalem. The Samaritans were hostile to Jews going to worship there.
    <verse eid="LUK 9:53"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="54" style="v" sid="LUK 9:54"/>
    When the disciples James and John saw this rejection, they said, “Lord, do You want us to command fire to come down from heaven and consume them?”
    <verse eid="LUK 9:54"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="55" style="v" sid="LUK 9:55"/>
    But Jesus turned and rebuked them for their vindictive spirit.
    <verse eid="LUK 9:55"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="56" style="v" sid="LUK 9:56"/>
    They went to another village instead.
    <verse eid="LUK 9:56"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Demands of Following Jesus</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 8:19-22</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="57" style="v" sid="LUK 9:57"/>
    As they were traveling along the road, someone said to Jesus, “I will follow You wherever You go.”
    <verse eid="LUK 9:57"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="58" style="v" sid="LUK 9:58"/>
    Jesus said to him, “Foxes have dens, and birds of the sky have nests, but the Son of Man has no place to lay His head.” 
    <verse eid="LUK 9:58"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="59" style="v" sid="LUK 9:59"/>
    Jesus said to another person, “Follow Me.” But that person said, “Lord, permit me first to go and bury my father.”
    <verse eid="LUK 9:59"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="60" style="v" sid="LUK 9:60"/>
    Jesus said to him, “Let the dead bury their own dead. But you go and proclaim the kingdom of God.”
    <verse eid="LUK 9:60"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="61" style="v" sid="LUK 9:61"/>
    Yet another person said, “I will follow You, Lord, but first let me say farewell to those at my home.”
    <verse eid="LUK 9:61"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="62" style="v" sid="LUK 9:62"/>
    But Jesus said, “No one who puts his hand to the plow and looks back is fit for service in the kingdom of God.”
    <verse eid="LUK 9:62"/>
  </para>
  <chapter eid="LUK 9"/>
  <chapter number="10" style="c" sid="LUK 10"/>
  <para style="s1">The Appointment of the Seventy-two Others</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 10:1"/>
    Following these events, the Lord commissioned seventy additional disciples, dispatching them in pairs ahead of Him to every town and location He intended to visit personally.
    <verse eid="LUK 10:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 10:2"/>
    He instructed them: “The spiritual harvest is abundant, yet laborers are scarce. Therefore, entreat the Lord who oversees this harvest to dispatch additional workers into His harvest fields.
    <verse eid="LUK 10:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 10:3"/>
    Proceed with this mission; understand that I am sending you forth as vulnerable lambs among predatory wolves.
    <verse eid="LUK 10:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 10:4"/>
    Travel without carrying a money pouch, supply bag, or extra sandals, and refrain from stopping to exchange greetings along your journey.
    <verse eid="LUK 10:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 10:5"/>
    Upon entering any household, immediately pronounce: ‘May peace rest upon this dwelling.’
    <verse eid="LUK 10:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 10:6"/>
    If a person receptive to peace resides there, your blessing will settle upon them. Otherwise, your blessing will return to you unfulfilled.
    <verse eid="LUK 10:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 10:7"/>
    Remain in that same household, accepting whatever food and drink they offer, for laborers deserve compensation. Do not relocate between different houses. 
    <verse eid="LUK 10:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 10:8"/>
    When you enter a town that welcomes you, consume whatever meals are provided for you,
    <verse eid="LUK 10:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 10:9"/>
    heal those who are sick within it, and proclaim to them, ‘God’s kingdom has drawn near to your community.’
    <verse eid="LUK 10:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 10:10"/>
    However, when you enter a town that rejects you, proceed into its public streets and declare,
    <verse eid="LUK 10:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 10:11"/>
    ‘We are removing even the dust from your town that adheres to our feet as a testimony against you. Nevertheless, understand this: God’s kingdom has approached your community.’
    <verse eid="LUK 10:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 10:12"/>
    I assure you that on the day of judgment, Sodom will receive more tolerable treatment than that town which rejects the gospel message.
    <verse eid="LUK 10:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Pronouncement of Woes on the Unrepentant Cities</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 11:20-24</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 10:13"/>
    Woe upon you, Chorazin! Woe upon you, Bethsaida! Had the miraculous demonstrations performed among you occurred in the pagan cities of Tyre and Sidon, those cities would have turned from their sins long ago, adopting mourning attire and ashes as outward expressions of genuine repentance.
    <verse eid="LUK 10:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 10:14"/>
    Nevertheless, Tyre and Sidon will experience more bearable judgment than you will endure.
    <verse eid="LUK 10:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 10:15"/>
    And you, Capernaum—do you presume you will be elevated to heaven’s glory? No; you will descend to Hades in judgment.
    <verse eid="LUK 10:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 10:16"/>
    Whoever listens to you listens to Me; whoever dismisses you dismisses Me; and whoever dismisses Me dismisses the Father who commissioned Me.”
    <verse eid="LUK 10:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Return of the Seventy-two</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 10:17"/>
    The seventy disciples returned filled with joy, reporting, “Lord, even demonic spirits submit to us when we invoke Your name.”
    <verse eid="LUK 10:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 10:18"/>
    He responded to them, “I observed Satan plummeting from heaven like a lightning bolt.
    <verse eid="LUK 10:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 10:19"/>
    Listen carefully: I have granted you authority to trample upon serpents and scorpions, and authority over all the enemy’s power, ensuring nothing will harm you.
    <verse eid="LUK 10:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 10:20"/>
    Nevertheless, do not rejoice primarily because spirits submit to your authority, but rejoice because your names have been written in heaven’s register.”
    <verse eid="LUK 10:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Jesus Rejoices in the Holy Spirit</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 11:25-27; 13:16-17</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 10:21"/>
    At that precise moment, Jesus rejoiced greatly through the Holy Spirit and declared, “I offer You praise, Father, sovereign Lord over heaven and earth, because You have concealed these truths from those who consider themselves wise and intellectually sophisticated, yet revealed them to those who approach with childlike humility. Yes, Father, this approach pleased You.
    <verse eid="LUK 10:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 10:22"/>
    My Father has entrusted all authority to Me, and no one truly comprehends the Son’s identity except the Father, nor does anyone truly comprehend the Father’s identity except the Son, along with anyone to whom the Son chooses to reveal Him.”
    <verse eid="LUK 10:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 10:23"/>
    Turning privately toward His disciples, He said, “How privileged are the eyes that witness what you are witnessing.
    <verse eid="LUK 10:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 10:24"/>
    For I tell you that numerous prophets and monarchs longed to observe what you are observing but never saw it; they desired to hear what you are hearing but never heard it.”
    <verse eid="LUK 10:24"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Parable of the Good </para>
  <para style="s1">Samaritan</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 10:25"/>
    Then a legal expert in Mosaic Law stood to test Jesus, inquiring, “Teacher, what actions must I perform to inherit eternal life?”
    <verse eid="LUK 10:25"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 10:26"/>
    Jesus replied, “What does the Law state? How do you interpret it?” 
    <verse eid="LUK 10:26"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 10:27"/>
    The man answered, “You shall love the Lord your God from your entire heart, with your complete soul, with your full strength, and with your whole mind; and love your neighbor as yourself.”
    <verse eid="LUK 10:27"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 10:28"/>
    Jesus affirmed, “Your response is correct. Live according to this principle, and you will obtain life.”
    <verse eid="LUK 10:28"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 10:29"/>
    But the man, attempting to justify himself, pressed Jesus, “And who qualifies as my neighbor?”
    <verse eid="LUK 10:29"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 10:30"/>
    Jesus continued with a parable: “A certain individual was traveling down from Jerusalem to Jericho when bandits attacked him. They stripped his clothing, assaulted him severely, and abandoned him barely alive.
    <verse eid="LUK 10:30"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 10:31"/>
    By coincidence, a priest was traveling down that same road. Upon seeing the injured man, he deliberately passed by on the opposite side.
    <verse eid="LUK 10:31"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 10:32"/>
    Similarly, a Levite arrived at that location, observed the victim, and likewise passed by on the opposite side.
    <verse eid="LUK 10:32"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 10:33"/>
    However, a certain Samaritan who was journeying came upon him, and upon seeing him, felt deep compassion.
    <verse eid="LUK 10:33"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 10:34"/>
    He approached the victim, bandaged his wounds while pouring on olive oil and wine as medicinal treatment. Then he placed him upon his own animal, transported him to an inn, and provided care for him.
    <verse eid="LUK 10:34"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 10:35"/>
    The following day, he produced two denarii and gave them to the innkeeper, instructing, ‘Care for him, and whatever additional expenses you incur, I will reimburse you upon my return.’
    <verse eid="LUK 10:35"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 10:36"/>
    Which of these three individuals, in your judgment, proved to be a neighbor to the man assaulted by bandits?”
    <verse eid="LUK 10:36"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 10:37"/>
    The legal expert replied, “The one who demonstrated compassion toward him.” Jesus instructed him, “Proceed and conduct yourself likewise.”
    <verse eid="LUK 10:37"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Martha and Mary</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 10:38"/>
    As they continued their journey, Jesus entered a particular village where a woman named Martha welcomed Him into her home.
    <verse eid="LUK 10:38"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="39" style="v" sid="LUK 10:39"/>
    She had a sister named Mary, who seated herself at Jesus’ feet and listened intently to His teaching.
    <verse eid="LUK 10:39"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="40" style="v" sid="LUK 10:40"/>
    Martha, however, was distracted by extensive hospitality preparations. She approached Jesus and protested, “Lord, does it not concern You that my sister has abandoned me to serve alone? Instruct her to assist me.”
    <verse eid="LUK 10:40"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="41" style="v" sid="LUK 10:41"/>
    The Lord responded to her, “Martha, Martha, you are worried and agitated about numerous matters.
    <verse eid="LUK 10:41"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="42" style="v" sid="LUK 10:42"/>
    Yet only one thing is truly necessary. Mary has chosen the superior portion, which will not be removed from her.”
    <verse eid="LUK 10:42"/>
  </para>
  <chapter eid="LUK 10"/>
  <chapter number="11" style="c" sid="LUK 11"/>
  <para style="s1">The Lord’s Prayer</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 6:9-15; 7:7-11</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 11:1"/>
    On one occasion, Jesus was praying at a certain location. When He concluded, one of His disciples requested, “Lord, teach us to pray, just as John instructed his disciples.”
    <verse eid="LUK 11:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 11:2"/>
    He instructed them, “When you pray, say: 
    <verse eid="LUK 11:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">‘Father, may Your name be revered as holy. </para>
  <para style="p">May Your kingdom come.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 11:3"/>
    Provide us each day with our daily sustenance.
    <verse eid="LUK 11:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 11:4"/>
    And forgive us our sins, for we ourselves forgive everyone indebted to us. 
    <verse eid="LUK 11:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">And do not lead us into temptation.’ ”</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 11:5"/>
    Then He said to them, “Suppose one of you has a friend, and you go to him at midnight, saying to him, ‘Friend, lend me three loaves of bread,
    <verse eid="LUK 11:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 11:6"/>
    because a friend of mine has arrived from a journey, and I have nothing to offer him.’
    <verse eid="LUK 11:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 11:7"/>
    And that friend answering from inside responds, ‘Do not trouble me. The door is already locked and my children are in bed with me. I cannot get up to give you anything.’
    <verse eid="LUK 11:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 11:8"/>
    I tell you, even though he will not arise and provide anything because of friendship, yet because of your shameless persistence, he will get up and give you whatever you need.
    <verse eid="LUK 11:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 11:9"/>
    Therefore, I tell you: Ask persistently and it will be given to you; seek diligently and you will discover; knock continually and the door will be opened to you.
    <verse eid="LUK 11:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 11:10"/>
    For everyone who asks receives; the one who seeks discovers; and to the one who knocks, the door will be opened.
    <verse eid="LUK 11:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 11:11"/>
    What father among you, if his son requests a fish, will give him a serpent instead of a fish?
    <verse eid="LUK 11:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 11:12"/>
    Or if he requests an egg, will he give him a scorpion?
    <verse eid="LUK 11:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 11:13"/>
    If you then, despite being sinful by nature, understand how to give good gifts to your children, how much more will the Father from heaven give the Holy Spirit to those who ask Him!”
    <verse eid="LUK 11:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Jesus and Beelzebul</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 12:12-30; Mk. 3:20-27</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 11:14"/>
    Jesus was expelling a demon that rendered someone mute. When the demon departed, the previously mute person spoke, and the crowds marveled.
    <verse eid="LUK 11:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 11:15"/>
    But certain individuals declared, “He expels demons by Beelzebul, the ruler of demons.”
    <verse eid="LUK 11:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 11:16"/>
    Others, testing Him, persisted in demanding a miraculous sign from heaven.
    <verse eid="LUK 11:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 11:17"/>
    But Jesus, perceiving their thoughts, said to them, “Every kingdom experiencing internal division becomes devastated, and a household divided against itself collapses.
    <verse eid="LUK 11:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 11:18"/>
    If Satan also experiences internal division, how will his kingdom endure? For you claim I expel demons by Beelzebul.
    <verse eid="LUK 11:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 11:19"/>
    But if I expel demons by Beelzebul, by whose power do your followers expel them? Therefore, they will serve as your judges.
    <verse eid="LUK 11:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 11:20"/>
    However, if I expel demons by God’s finger, then God’s kingdom has arrived among you.
    <verse eid="LUK 11:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 11:21"/>
    When a strong man, fully armed, guards his own estate, his possessions remain secure.
    <verse eid="LUK 11:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 11:22"/>
    But when someone stronger than he attacks and overpowers him, he confiscates all the armor in which he trusted and distributes his plundered goods.
    <verse eid="LUK 11:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 11:23"/>
    “Whoever does not align with Me opposes Me; and whoever does not gather with Me scatters.
    <verse eid="LUK 11:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Return of the Unclean Spirit</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 12:43-45</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 11:24"/>
    “When an unclean spirit departs from a person, it travels through waterless regions seeking rest but discovers none. Then it declares, ‘I will return to my dwelling from which I departed.’
    <verse eid="LUK 11:24"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 11:25"/>
    Upon arriving, it discovers the house swept clean and orderly.
    <verse eid="LUK 11:25"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 11:26"/>
    Then it departs and brings seven other spirits more wicked than itself, and after entering, they inhabit that person. Consequently, that person’s final condition becomes worse than the initial state.”
    <verse eid="LUK 11:26"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">True Blessedness</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 11:27"/>
    As He was speaking these words, a certain woman from the crowd raised her voice and declared to Him, “Blessed is the womb that carried You, and the breasts that nursed You!”
    <verse eid="LUK 11:27"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 11:28"/>
    But He replied, “On the contrary, blessed are those who hear God’s word and obey it!”
    <verse eid="LUK 11:28"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Seeking After a Sign</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 12:38-42; Mk. 8:12</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 11:29"/>
    As the crowds increased, He began saying, “This generation is a wicked generation. It seeks a miraculous sign, but no sign will be provided to it except the sign of Jonah.
    <verse eid="LUK 11:29"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 11:30"/>
    For just as Jonah became a sign to the Ninevites, so too the Son of Man will be to this generation.
    <verse eid="LUK 11:30"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 11:31"/>
    The queen of the South will arise at the judgment alongside the people of this generation and will condemn them, because she traveled from earth’s distant regions to hear Solomon’s wisdom; and consider this, someone infinitely greater than Solomon is here.
    <verse eid="LUK 11:31"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 11:32"/>
    The people of Nineveh will stand at the judgment with this generation and condemn it, because they repented at Jonah’s preaching; and consider this, someone infinitely greater than Jonah is here.
    <verse eid="LUK 11:32"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Light of the Body</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 5:15; 6:22-23</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 11:33"/>
    “No one, after lighting a lamp, conceals it in a cellar or under a basket, but places it on a lampstand, so that those who enter can perceive the light.
    <verse eid="LUK 11:33"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 11:34"/>
    Your eye serves as the body’s lamp. When your eye is healthy, your entire body is illuminated. But when it is diseased, your body is also darkened.
    <verse eid="LUK 11:34"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 11:35"/>
    Therefore, be careful lest the light within you is actually darkness.
    <verse eid="LUK 11:35"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 11:36"/>
    If then your whole body is illuminated, having no darkened part, it will be completely bright, as when a lamp illuminates you with its radiance.”
    <verse eid="LUK 11:36"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Pronouncement of Woes on the Pharisees and Legal Experts</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 23:1-36; Mk. 12:38-40; Lk. 20:45-47</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 11:37"/>
    When He finished speaking, a Pharisee invited Him to dine with him, and He entered and reclined at the table.
    <verse eid="LUK 11:37"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 11:38"/>
    The Pharisee, observing this, was astonished that He did not perform the ceremonial washing before the meal.
    <verse eid="LUK 11:38"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="39" style="v" sid="LUK 11:39"/>
    But the Lord said to him, “Now you Pharisees cleanse the cup’s and dish’s exterior, but your interior is filled with greed and wickedness.
    <verse eid="LUK 11:39"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="40" style="v" sid="LUK 11:40"/>
    Foolish people! Did not the One who created the exterior also create the interior?
    <verse eid="LUK 11:40"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="41" style="v" sid="LUK 11:41"/>
    Rather, give as charity what is within; and observe, everything becomes clean for you.
    <verse eid="LUK 11:41"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="42" style="v" sid="LUK 11:42"/>
    “But woe to you, Pharisees, because you tithe mint and rue and every garden herb, yet neglect justice and God’s love. These you ought to have practiced without neglecting those other matters.
    <verse eid="LUK 11:42"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="43" style="v" sid="LUK 11:43"/>
    Woe to you, Pharisees, because you desire the most prominent seats in the synagogues and respectful greetings in the marketplaces.
    <verse eid="LUK 11:43"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="44" style="v" sid="LUK 11:44"/>
    Woe to you, because you resemble unmarked graves, and people who walk over them remain unaware.
    <verse eid="LUK 11:44"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="45" style="v" sid="LUK 11:45"/>
    Then one of the legal experts responded to Him, “Teacher, by saying these things, you insult us also.”
    <verse eid="LUK 11:45"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="46" style="v" sid="LUK 11:46"/>
    He replied, “Woe to you also, legal experts, because you burden people with oppressive loads, yet you yourselves refuse to touch the burdens with even one finger.
    <verse eid="LUK 11:46"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="47" style="v" sid="LUK 11:47"/>
    Woe to you, because you construct monuments for the prophets, yet your ancestors murdered them.
    <verse eid="LUK 11:47"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="48" style="v" sid="LUK 11:48"/>
    Consequently, you serve as witnesses and approve of your ancestors’ deeds, because they murdered them and you build their monuments.
    <verse eid="LUK 11:48"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="49" style="v" sid="LUK 11:49"/>
    Indeed, for this reason, God’s Wisdom declared, ‘I will send them prophets and apostles, and some they will kill and persecute,’
    <verse eid="LUK 11:49"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="50" style="v" sid="LUK 11:50"/>
    so that the blood of all prophets, shed from the world’s foundation, may be charged against this generation,
    <verse eid="LUK 11:50"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="51" style="v" sid="LUK 11:51"/>
    from Abel’s blood to Zechariah’s blood, who perished between the altar and the sanctuary. Yes, I tell you, it will be charged against this generation.
    <verse eid="LUK 11:51"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="52" style="v" sid="LUK 11:52"/>
    Woe to you, legal experts, you have removed the key of knowledge. You did not enter yourselves, and you prevented those who were attempting to enter.”
    <verse eid="LUK 11:52"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="53" style="v" sid="LUK 11:53"/>
    When He departed from there, the scribes and Pharisees became extremely hostile and interrogated Him about numerous matters,
    <verse eid="LUK 11:53"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="54" style="v" sid="LUK 11:54"/>
    plotting to trap Him in something He might say.
    <verse eid="LUK 11:54"/>
  </para>
  <chapter eid="LUK 11"/>
  <chapter number="12" style="c" sid="LUK 12"/>
  <para style="s1">Warning Against the Leaven of </para>
  <para style="s1">the Pharisees</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 12:1"/>
    During this time, an enormous crowd of many thousands assembled, so packed together that people were trampling one another. Jesus began speaking first to His disciples: “Be vigilant and guard yourselves against the corrupting influence of the Pharisees, which is hypocrisy. 
    <verse eid="LUK 12:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 12:2"/>
    There is nothing covered up that will not eventually be uncovered, nothing hidden that will not ultimately be brought into the open.
    <verse eid="LUK 12:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 12:3"/>
    Therefore, whatever you have whispered in the darkness will be proclaimed in broad daylight, and what you have spoken privately behind closed doors will be announced publicly from the housetops.
    <verse eid="LUK 12:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Fear Only God</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 10:28-31</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 12:4"/>
    “I say to you who are My friends: Do not be afraid of those who can kill the physical body but afterward possess no further power to harm you.
    <verse eid="LUK 12:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 12:5"/>
    Let Me show you whom you should fear: Fear the One who, after taking your life, has the authority to cast your soul into hell. Yes, I tell you emphatically, fear Him alone!
    <verse eid="LUK 12:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 12:6"/>
    Are not five small sparrows sold for merely two pennies in the marketplace? Yet not a single one of them is forgotten or overlooked in God’s sight.
    <verse eid="LUK 12:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 12:7"/>
    In fact, even the individual hairs on your head are all numbered and known by God. Stop being afraid—you are worth far more than many sparrows.
    <verse eid="LUK 12:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Acknowledging Christ Before Men</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 10:32-33; 12:32; 10:19-20</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 12:8"/>
    “I tell you this truth: Everyone who openly acknowledges and confesses Me before other people, the Son of Man will likewise acknowledge him before the angels of God.
    <verse eid="LUK 12:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 12:9"/>
    But whoever denies Me before people will be denied and disowned before the angels of God.
    <verse eid="LUK 12:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 12:10"/>
    Everyone who speaks a word against the Son of Man may receive forgiveness. But the person who blasphemes against the Holy Spirit—attributing God’s work to Satan—will not receive forgiveness.
    <verse eid="LUK 12:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 12:11"/>
    When they bring you before synagogues, rulers, and authorities for trial, do not become anxious about how you should defend yourself or what you should say in your defense.
    <verse eid="LUK 12:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 12:12"/>
    The Holy Spirit will teach and instruct you in that very hour what you must say.”
    <verse eid="LUK 12:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Parable of the Rich Fool</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 12:13"/>
    Someone in the crowd spoke up and said to Jesus, “Teacher, command my brother to divide the family inheritance with me.”
    <verse eid="LUK 12:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 12:14"/>
    But Jesus replied to him, “Man, who appointed or authorized Me to be a judge or arbitrator to settle disputes between you?”
    <verse eid="LUK 12:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 12:15"/>
    Then Jesus warned the people, “Keep watch against every form of greed and covetousness, because a person’s life does not consist in or depend on the abundance of material possessions he owns.”
    <verse eid="LUK 12:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 12:16"/>
    Jesus told them this parable: “The land belonging to a certain wealthy man produced an exceptionally abundant harvest.
    <verse eid="LUK 12:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 12:17"/>
    He began reasoning to himself, saying, ‘What should I do? I do not have sufficient space to store all my crops.’
    <verse eid="LUK 12:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 12:18"/>
    Then he said, ‘Here is what I will do: I will tear down my existing barns and build much larger ones, and there I will store all my grain and my goods.
    <verse eid="LUK 12:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 12:19"/>
    And I will say to my soul, “Soul, you have plenty of goods laid up for many years to come. Take your ease, relax, eat, drink, and enjoy yourself.’ ”
    <verse eid="LUK 12:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 12:20"/>
    But God said to him, ‘You fool! This very night your soul is being demanded of you. And the things you have prepared and accumulated—whose will they be after you die?’
    <verse eid="LUK 12:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 12:21"/>
    This is how it will be for anyone who stores up treasure for himself but is not rich toward God—who accumulates earthly wealth but has no spiritual riches.”
    <verse eid="LUK 12:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Do Not Be Anxious</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 6:25-34; 19:21</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 12:22"/>
    Then Jesus said to His disciples, “For this reason I tell you: Stop worrying about your life—what you will eat, or about your body—what you will wear.
    <verse eid="LUK 12:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 12:23"/>
    Life is far more important than food, and the body is far more important than clothing.
    <verse eid="LUK 12:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 12:24"/>
    Consider and observe the ravens: they do not sow seed or reap harvest; they have no storeroom or barn, yet God feeds them. How much more valuable and precious you are than birds!
    <verse eid="LUK 12:24"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 12:25"/>
    Which of you by worrying and being anxious can add a single hour to your lifespan or a single inch to your height?
    <verse eid="LUK 12:25"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 12:26"/>
    If you cannot accomplish even this smallest thing, why do you worry about all the other matters?
    <verse eid="LUK 12:26"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 12:27"/>
    Consider how the lilies grow: they do not toil or spin thread to make clothing, yet I tell you that not even Solomon in all his royal splendor was dressed as beautifully as one of these flowers.
    <verse eid="LUK 12:27"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 12:28"/>
    Now if God so clothes the grass of the field—which is alive today but tomorrow is thrown into the oven as fuel—how much more will He clothe you, O you people of little faith!
    <verse eid="LUK 12:28"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 12:29"/>
    As for you, do not keep seeking what you will eat and what you will drink, and do not have a worried, anxious mind.
    <verse eid="LUK 12:29"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 12:30"/>
    For all the pagan nations of the world eagerly seek after all these things, but your Father knows that you need these things.
    <verse eid="LUK 12:30"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 12:31"/>
    Instead, seek His kingdom first and foremost, and these material necessities will be provided to you.
    <verse eid="LUK 12:31"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 12:32"/>
    Do not be afraid, little flock, for your Father has chosen in His good pleasure to give you the kingdom.
    <verse eid="LUK 12:32"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 12:33"/>
    Sell your possessions and give to the poor. Make for yourselves money bags that do not wear out or become old, an unfailing treasure in heaven where no thief can come near and no moth can destroy.
    <verse eid="LUK 12:33"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 12:34"/>
    For wherever your treasure is located, there your heart will be focused also.”
    <verse eid="LUK 12:34"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Be Prepared for Action</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 24:45-51</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 12:35"/>
    “Keep your waists girded and ready for action, and keep your lamps burning brightly.
    <verse eid="LUK 12:35"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 12:36"/>
    Be like servants who are waiting expectantly for their master to return from the wedding banquet, so that when he comes and knocks, they can immediately open the door for him.
    <verse eid="LUK 12:36"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 12:37"/>
    Blessed are those servants whom the master will find alert and watching when he arrives. Truly, I tell you that he will dress himself for service, have them recline at the table, and will personally come and serve them.
    <verse eid="LUK 12:37"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 12:38"/>
    Even if he comes back in the second watch of the night (about 9.00 p.m. to midnight) or the third watch (about midnight to 3.00 a.m.) and finds them watching faithfully, blessed are those servants.
    <verse eid="LUK 12:38"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="39" style="v" sid="LUK 12:39"/>
    But understand this: If the homeowner had known at what hour the thief was coming, he would have stayed awake and not allowed his house to be broken into.
    <verse eid="LUK 12:39"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="40" style="v" sid="LUK 12:40"/>
    You also must be ready and prepared, because the Son of Man is coming at an hour when you do not expect Him.”
    <verse eid="LUK 12:40"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="41" style="v" sid="LUK 12:41"/>
    Peter asked, “Lord, are You telling this parable for us apostles specifically, or for everyone in general?”
    <verse eid="LUK 12:41"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="42" style="v" sid="LUK 12:42"/>
    The Lord answered, “Who then is the faithful and wise manager whom the master will put in charge over his household servants to give them their food allowance at the proper time?
    <verse eid="LUK 12:42"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="43" style="v" sid="LUK 12:43"/>
    Blessed is that servant whom his master finds doing so when he returns.
    <verse eid="LUK 12:43"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="44" style="v" sid="LUK 12:44"/>
    Truly, I tell you that the master will put him in charge of all his possessions.
    <verse eid="LUK 12:44"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="45" style="v" sid="LUK 12:45"/>
    But suppose that servant says in his heart, ‘My master is taking a long time in coming,’ and he begins to beat the male and female servants, and to eat and drink and get drunk.
    <verse eid="LUK 12:45"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="46" style="v" sid="LUK 12:46"/>
    The master of that servant will come on a day when he does not expect him and at an hour he does not know. He will cut him in pieces and assign him a place with the unfaithful.
    <verse eid="LUK 12:46"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="47" style="v" sid="LUK 12:47"/>
    That servant who knew his master’s will but did not get ready or act according to his will shall be beaten with many blows.
    <verse eid="LUK 12:47"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="48" style="v" sid="LUK 12:48"/>
    But the one who did not know his master’s will and did things worthy of punishment will be beaten with a few blows. From everyone to whom much has been given, much will be required. To whom much was entrusted, they will ask even more from him.”
    <verse eid="LUK 12:48"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Jesus’ Mission to Cast Fire on the Earth</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 10:34-36</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="49" style="v" sid="LUK 12:49"/>
    Jesus declared, “I came to cast fire upon the earth—to bring judgment and division—and how I wish it were already kindled and burning!
    <verse eid="LUK 12:49"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="50" style="v" sid="LUK 12:50"/>
    But I have a baptism of suffering to undergo, and how greatly distressed I am until it is accomplished!
    <verse eid="LUK 12:50"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="51" style="v" sid="LUK 12:51"/>
    Do you think that I came to bring peace on the earth? No, I tell you, but rather division and separation!
    <verse eid="LUK 12:51"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="52" style="v" sid="LUK 12:52"/>
    For from now on, five people in one household will be divided against each other: three against two and two against three.
    <verse eid="LUK 12:52"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="53" style="v" sid="LUK 12:53"/>
    They will be divided: Father against son and son against father, mother against daughter and daughter against mother, mother-in-law against her daughter-in-law and daughter-in-law against mother-in-law.”
    <verse eid="LUK 12:53"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Interpreting the Time</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 16:2-3</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="54" style="v" sid="LUK 12:54"/>
    Jesus also said to the crowds: “When you see a cloud rising in the west, you immediately say, ‘A rainstorm is coming,’ and so it happens.
    <verse eid="LUK 12:54"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="55" style="v" sid="LUK 12:55"/>
    And when the south wind is blowing, you say, ‘There will be scorching heat,’ and it comes to pass.
    <verse eid="LUK 12:55"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="56" style="v" sid="LUK 12:56"/>
    You hypocrites! You know how to interpret and analyze the appearance of the earth and sky, but how is it that you do not know how to interpret this present time and recognize the signs of the Messiah’s presence?
    <verse eid="LUK 12:56"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Settling with Your Opponent</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 5:25-26</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="57" style="v" sid="LUK 12:57"/>
    Why do you not judge for yourselves what is right and just?
    <verse eid="LUK 12:57"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="58" style="v" sid="LUK 12:58"/>
    As you are going with your adversary to appear before the magistrate, make every effort to settle the matter with him along the way, lest he drag you to the judge, and the judge hand you over to the officer, and the officer throw you into prison.
    <verse eid="LUK 12:58"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="59" style="v" sid="LUK 12:59"/>
    I tell you, you will never get out of there until you have paid back every last penny.”
    <verse eid="LUK 12:59"/>
  </para>
  <chapter eid="LUK 12"/>
  <chapter number="13" style="c" sid="LUK 13"/>
  <para style="s1">Repent or Perish</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 13:1"/>
    At that very time some people arrived who brought Jesus news about certain Galileans whose blood Pilate had mixed with their sacrifices—killing them while they were worshipping.
    <verse eid="LUK 13:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 13:2"/>
    Jesus responded to them, “Do you think that these Galileans were worse sinners than all the other Galileans because they suffered this fate?
    <verse eid="LUK 13:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 13:3"/>
    No, I tell you emphatically. But unless you repent and turn from your sins, you will all likewise perish.
    <verse eid="LUK 13:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 13:4"/>
    Or those eighteen people on whom the tower in Siloam fell and killed them—do you think they were more guilty sinners than all the other people living in Jerusalem?
    <verse eid="LUK 13:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 13:5"/>
    No, I tell you. But unless you repent, you will all perish in the same way.
    <verse eid="LUK 13:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Parable of the Fig Tree</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 13:6"/>
    Then Jesus told this parable: “A certain man had a fig tree that was planted in his vineyard. He came looking for fruit on it but found none.
    <verse eid="LUK 13:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 13:7"/>
    So he said to the vineyard worker, ‘Look, for three years now I have been coming to look for fruit on this fig tree and have found none. Cut it down! Why should it continue using up the soil and depleting its nutrients?’
    <verse eid="LUK 13:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 13:8"/>
    But the worker replied to him, ‘Sir, leave it alone for one more year. Let me dig around it and put fertilizer on it.
    <verse eid="LUK 13:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 13:9"/>
    If it bears fruit next year, excellent. But if not, then you can cut it down.’ ”
    <verse eid="LUK 13:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">A Woman with a Spirit of Disability</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 13:10"/>
    Jesus was teaching in one of the synagogues on the Sabbath day.
    <verse eid="LUK 13:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 13:11"/>
    A woman was there who had been afflicted by a spirit of disability for eighteen years. She was bent over and completely unable to straighten herself up.
    <verse eid="LUK 13:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 13:12"/>
    When Jesus saw her, He called her over and said to her, “Woman, you are set free from your disability.”
    <verse eid="LUK 13:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 13:13"/>
    He laid His hands on her, and immediately she straightened up and began glorifying and praising God.
    <verse eid="LUK 13:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 13:14"/>
    But the synagogue ruler was indignant because Jesus had healed on the Sabbath. He said to the crowd, “There are six days in which work should be done. Come on those days and be healed, not on the Sabbath day.”
    <verse eid="LUK 13:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 13:15"/>
    But the Lord answered him, “You hypocrites! Does not each one of you on the Sabbath untie his ox or donkey from the stall and lead it away to give it water?
    <verse eid="LUK 13:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 13:16"/>
    This woman is a daughter of Abraham whom Satan has kept bound for eighteen long years. Should she not be released from this bondage on the Sabbath day?”
    <verse eid="LUK 13:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 13:17"/>
    When Jesus said these things, all those who opposed Him were put to shame and humiliated. But the entire crowd was rejoicing over all the glorious things being done by Him.
    <verse eid="LUK 13:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Parables of the Mustard Seed and the Leaven</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 13:31-33; Mk. 4:30-32</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 13:18"/>
    Therefore Jesus said, “What is the kingdom of God like? To what can I compare it?
    <verse eid="LUK 13:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 13:19"/>
    It is like a mustard seed that a man took and planted in his garden. It grew and became a large tree, and the birds of the sky nested in its branches.”
    <verse eid="LUK 13:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 13:20"/>
    Again Jesus asked, “To what shall I compare the kingdom of God?
    <verse eid="LUK 13:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 13:21"/>
    It is like yeast that a woman took and mixed into three measures of flour until the entire batch of dough was leavened.” 
    <verse eid="LUK 13:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Narrow Door</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 7:13-14, 21-23</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 13:22"/>
    Jesus was traveling through towns and villages, teaching as He made His way toward Jerusalem.
    <verse eid="LUK 13:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 13:23"/>
    Someone asked Him, “Lord, are only a few people going to be saved?” He said to them,
    <verse eid="LUK 13:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 13:24"/>
    “Strive and exert yourselves to the utmost to enter through the narrow door, because many, I tell you, will try to enter but will not be able.
    <verse eid="LUK 13:24"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 13:25"/>
    Once the master of the house has gotten up and shut the door, you will stand outside knocking on the door, saying, ‘Lord, open the door for us.’ But He will answer you, ‘I do not know where you come from.’
    <verse eid="LUK 13:25"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 13:26"/>
    Then you will begin to say, ‘We ate and drank in Your presence, and You taught in our streets.’
    <verse eid="LUK 13:26"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 13:27"/>
    But He will reply, ‘I tell you, I do not know where you come from. Depart from Me, all you workers of unrighteousness and evildoing!’
    <verse eid="LUK 13:27"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 13:28"/>
    There will be weeping and gnashing of teeth when you see Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, and all the prophets in the kingdom of God, but yourselves thrown outside.
    <verse eid="LUK 13:28"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 13:29"/>
    People will come from east and west, from north and south, and will recline at the table in the kingdom of God.
    <verse eid="LUK 13:29"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 13:30"/>
    And take note: Some who are last will be first, and some who are first will be last.”
    <verse eid="LUK 13:30"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Jesus Laments Over Jerusalem</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 23:37-39</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 13:31"/>
    At that very hour some Pharisees came and said to Jesus, “Leave this place and go away, because Herod wants to kill You.”
    <verse eid="LUK 13:31"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 13:32"/>
    Jesus replied, “Go tell that fox, ‘Listen: I am casting out demons and performing healings today and tomorrow, and on the third day I will complete My work.’
    <verse eid="LUK 13:32"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 13:33"/>
    Nevertheless, I must keep going on My way today and tomorrow and the day following, because it is unthinkable that a prophet should perish outside Jerusalem.
    <verse eid="LUK 13:33"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 13:34"/>
    O Jerusalem, Jerusalem, the city that kills the prophets and stones those who are sent to her! How often I wanted to gather your children together as a hen gathers her chicks under her wings, but you were not willing!
    <verse eid="LUK 13:34"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 13:35"/>
    Look, your house is left to you desolate and abandoned. I tell you, you will not see Me again until the time comes when you say, ‘Blessed is He who comes in the name of the Lord!’ ”
    <verse eid="LUK 13:35"/>
  </para>
  <chapter eid="LUK 13"/>
  <chapter number="14" style="c" sid="LUK 14"/>
  <para style="s1">The Healing of a Man Suffering from Dropsy</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 14:1"/>
    One Sabbath day, when Jesus went to eat a meal at the house of a prominent Pharisee, they were watching Him closely with hostile intent.
    <verse eid="LUK 14:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 14:2"/>
    Right there in front of Him was a man suffering from dropsy.
    <verse eid="LUK 14:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 14:3"/>
    Jesus asked the lawyers and Pharisees, “Is it lawful to heal on the Sabbath, or not?”
    <verse eid="LUK 14:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 14:4"/>
    But they remained silent, refusing to answer. Jesus took hold of the man, healed him, and sent him on his way.
    <verse eid="LUK 14:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 14:5"/>
    Then Jesus asked them, “Which of you, if your son or ox falls into a well on the Sabbath day, will not immediately pull him out?”
    <verse eid="LUK 14:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 14:6"/>
    They could give no answer to refute these arguments.
    <verse eid="LUK 14:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Parable of the Guests and a Host at a Wedding Feast</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 14:7"/>
    When Jesus noticed how the invited guests were choosing the places of honor for themselves, He told them this parable:
    <verse eid="LUK 14:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 14:8"/>
    “When someone invites you to a wedding feast, do not take the place of honor, lest someone more honored than you has been invited by the host.
    <verse eid="LUK 14:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 14:9"/>
    Then the one who invited both of you will come and say to you, ‘Give this person your place.’ And you will begin with shame to take the lowest and least honorable place.
    <verse eid="LUK 14:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 14:10"/>
    Instead, when you are invited, go and sit in the lowest place, so that when your host comes, he will say to you, ‘Friend, move up to a better place.’ Then you will be honored in the presence of all the other guests.
    <verse eid="LUK 14:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 14:11"/>
    For everyone who exalts himself will be humbled, and the one who humbles himself will be exalted.”
    <verse eid="LUK 14:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 14:12"/>
    Then Jesus addressed His host, saying, “When you host a luncheon or dinner, do not call your friends, your siblings, your relatives, or your prosperous neighbors. If you do this, they may reciprocate by inviting you, and in this way you will receive your recompense.
    <verse eid="LUK 14:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 14:13"/>
    Rather, when you host a feast, extend invitations to the poor, the crippled, the lame, and the blind.
    <verse eid="LUK 14:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 14:14"/>
    Then you will experience blessing, because they lack the means to repay you. You will receive your reward at the resurrection of the righteous.’’
    <verse eid="LUK 14:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Parable of the Great Dinner</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 22:1-10</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 14:15"/>
    When one of those sharing the meal with Jesus heard these words, he declared to Him, “How blessed is the person who will share a meal in God’s kingdom!”
    <verse eid="LUK 14:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 14:16"/>
    Jesus replied to him with a parable: “A certain man was hosting a magnificent banquet and sent out invitations to many people.
    <verse eid="LUK 14:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 14:17"/>
    At the appointed hour for the dinner, he dispatched his servant to inform those who had received invitations, ‘Come now, because everything stands prepared.’
    <verse eid="LUK 14:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 14:18"/>
    But one after another they all started offering excuses. The first declared to him, ‘I have purchased a piece of land, and it is necessary for me to go examine it. I ask you to excuse me.’
    <verse eid="LUK 14:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 14:19"/>
    And another stated, ‘I have purchased five pairs of oxen, and I am going to test them. I ask you to excuse me.’
    <verse eid="LUK 14:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 14:20"/>
    Yet another responded, ‘I have just married a wife, and for this reason I am unable to come.’
    <verse eid="LUK 14:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 14:21"/>
    The servant returned and conveyed all these responses to his master. Then the master of the house grew angry and commanded his servant, ‘Go out at once into the city’s streets and narrow lanes and bring here the poor, the crippled, the blind, and the lame.’
    <verse eid="LUK 14:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 14:22"/>
    The servant reported, ‘Master, what you commanded has been accomplished, and still there is available space.’
    <verse eid="LUK 14:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 14:23"/>
    Then the master instructed the servant, ‘Go out to the highways and along the hedgerows and urge people to enter, so that my house may be filled.
    <verse eid="LUK 14:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 14:24"/>
    I declare to you, not one of those individuals who received invitations will have a taste of my banquet.’ ”
    <verse eid="LUK 14:24"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Cost of Discipleship</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 10:37-38</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 14:25"/>
    Great crowds were journeying alongside Jesus. Turning toward them, He declared,
    <verse eid="LUK 14:25"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 14:26"/>
    “If anyone comes to Me and does not hate his own father and mother, and wife and children, and brothers and sisters, and indeed even his own life, he is unable to be My disciple.
    <verse eid="LUK 14:26"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 14:27"/>
    Whoever does not bear his own cross and come after Me is unable to be My disciple.
    <verse eid="LUK 14:27"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 14:28"/>
    For which among you, intending to construct a tower, does not first sit down and compute the expense to determine whether he has sufficient resources to finish it?
    <verse eid="LUK 14:28"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 14:29"/>
    Otherwise, having laid the foundation and proving unable to complete the project, all who observe will start ridiculing him,
    <verse eid="LUK 14:29"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 14:30"/>
    declaring, ‘This person initiated construction but lacked the ability to bring it to completion.’
    <verse eid="LUK 14:30"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 14:31"/>
    Or what king, marching out to engage another king in battle, does not first sit down and deliberate whether he has the capability with ten thousand soldiers to confront the one advancing against him with twenty thousand?
    <verse eid="LUK 14:31"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 14:32"/>
    And if he cannot, while the other king is still at a distance, he dispatches an embassy and requests conditions for peace.
    <verse eid="LUK 14:32"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 14:33"/>
    So in the same manner, any among you who does not renounce all his possessions is unable to be My disciple.”
    <verse eid="LUK 14:33"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Uselessness of Salt Without Taste</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 5:13; Mk. 9:50</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 14:34"/>
    Salt is excellent and beneficial, but if the salt becomes tasteless, by what means can its flavor be restored?
    <verse eid="LUK 14:34"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 14:35"/>
    It proves suitable neither for the ground nor for the compost heap. People discard it. Let the one who has ears for hearing, hear.”
    <verse eid="LUK 14:35"/>
  </para>
  <chapter eid="LUK 14"/>
  <chapter number="15" style="c" sid="LUK 15"/>
  <para style="s1">The Parable of the Lost Sheep</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 18:12-14</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 15:1"/>
    Now all the tax collectors and sinners were approaching to hear Jesus.
    <verse eid="LUK 15:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 15:2"/>
    But the Pharisees and the scribes were complaining, saying, “This person receives sinners and shares meals with them.”
    <verse eid="LUK 15:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 15:3"/>
    So He communicated this parable to them:
    <verse eid="LUK 15:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 15:4"/>
    “What person among you, possessing a hundred sheep and losing one of them, does not abandon the ninety-nine in the wilderness and pursue the lost one until he locates it?
    <verse eid="LUK 15:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 15:5"/>
    And having found it, he lays it on his shoulders with joy.
    <verse eid="LUK 15:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 15:6"/>
    And arriving at his home, he summons his friends and neighbors, declaring to them, 'Share in my joy, because I have located my sheep that was lost!’
    <verse eid="LUK 15:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 15:7"/>
    I declare to you that in this same manner, there will be greater joy in heaven over one sinner who repents than over ninety-nine righteous individuals who have no need of repentance.”
    <verse eid="LUK 15:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Parable of the Lost Coin</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 15:8"/>
    “Or what woman, possessing ten silver coins and losing one coin, does not light a lamp, sweep the house, and conduct a thorough search until she discovers it?
    <verse eid="LUK 15:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 15:9"/>
    And having discovered it, she gathers her friends and neighbors, declaring, ‘Share in my joy, because I have discovered the coin I had lost!’
    <verse eid="LUK 15:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 15:10"/>
    In this same manner, I declare to you, joy occurs in the presence of God’s angels over one sinner who repents.”
    <verse eid="LUK 15:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Parable of the Prodigal Son</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 15:11"/>
    Jesus went on to say: “There was a man who had two sons.
    <verse eid="LUK 15:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 15:12"/>
    The younger of them said to his father, ‘Father, give me the portion of the inheritance that belongs to me.’ So the father apportioned his estate between them.
    <verse eid="LUK 15:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 15:13"/>
    A short time later, the younger son gathered everything he owned and left for a far-off land. There he wasted his wealth through irresponsible and self-indulgent living.
    <verse eid="LUK 15:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 15:14"/>
    When he had exhausted all his resources, a severe famine swept through that region, and he found himself in desperate need.
    <verse eid="LUK 15:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 15:15"/>
    He went and attached himself to a citizen of that country, who sent him out to his fields to tend pigs.
    <verse eid="LUK 15:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 15:16"/>
    He was so hungry that he wished he could eat the food given to the pigs, but no one offered him anything.
    <verse eid="LUK 15:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 15:17"/>
    When he finally came to his right mind, he said, ‘How many of my father’s hired workers have food to spare, while I am perishing here from hunger!
    <verse eid="LUK 15:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 15:18"/>
    I will rise, return to my father, and say to him, “Father, I have sinned against heaven and before you.
    <verse eid="LUK 15:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 15:19"/>
    I am no longer deserving to be called your son. Treat me as one of your hired servants.” ’
    <verse eid="LUK 15:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 15:20"/>
    He got up and went back to his father. While he was still far away, his father saw him and was moved with deep compassion. He ran to him, embraced him, and kissed him warmly.
    <verse eid="LUK 15:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 15:21"/>
    The son said, ‘Father, I have sinned against heaven and before you. I am no longer worthy to be called your son.’
    <verse eid="LUK 15:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 15:22"/>
    But the father said to his servants, ‘Hurry! Bring the finest robe and put it on him. Place a ring on his hand and sandals on his feet.
    <verse eid="LUK 15:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 15:23"/>
    Bring the fattened calf and slaughter it. Let us celebrate with a feast,
    <verse eid="LUK 15:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 15:24"/>
    because this son of mine was dead and has come back to life; he was lost and has been found.’ And they began to celebrate.
    <verse eid="LUK 15:24"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 15:25"/>
    Meanwhile, the older son was working in the field. As he came near the house, he heard music and dancing.
    <verse eid="LUK 15:25"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 15:26"/>
    He called one of the servants and asked what all this meant.
    <verse eid="LUK 15:26"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 15:27"/>
    The servant said to him, ‘Your brother has returned, and your father has killed the fattened calf because he has received him back safe and well.’
    <verse eid="LUK 15:27"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 15:28"/>
    The older son became angry and refused to enter the house. His father came out and urged him to come in.
    <verse eid="LUK 15:28"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 15:29"/>
    But he answered his father, ‘Look, I have served you all these years and never ignored your commands. Yet you never gave me even a young goat so that I could celebrate with my friends.
    <verse eid="LUK 15:29"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 15:30"/>
    But when this son of yours returned—after wasting your property with immoral living—you slaughtered the fattened calf for him!’
    <verse eid="LUK 15:30"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 15:31"/>
    The father said to him, ‘My son, you are always with me, and everything I have belongs to you.
    <verse eid="LUK 15:31"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 15:32"/>
    But it was right for us to celebrate and rejoice, because this brother of yours was dead and has come back to life; he was lost and has now been found.’ ”
    <verse eid="LUK 15:32"/>
  </para>
  <chapter eid="LUK 15"/>
  <chapter number="16" style="c" sid="LUK 16"/>
  <para style="s1">The Parable of the Unrighteous Manager</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 16:1"/>
    Jesus also spoke to His disciples and said: “There was a wealthy man who employed a steward to manage his estate. Charges were brought to him that this steward was mismanaging and squandering his property.
    <verse eid="LUK 16:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 16:2"/>
    So the master summoned him and said, ‘What is this report I hear about you? Turn in a full account of your administration, because you can no longer remain in charge.’
    <verse eid="LUK 16:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 16:3"/>
    The steward began reasoning within himself, ‘What am I going to do now that my master is removing me from my position? I do not have the strength to do manual labor, and I am too ashamed to survive by begging.
    <verse eid="LUK 16:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 16:4"/>
    I know what course of action I will take, so that after I am dismissed from my stewardship, people will receive me into their homes.’
    <verse eid="LUK 16:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 16:5"/>
    So he called each one of his master’s debtors to him individually. He asked the first, ‘How much do you owe my master?’
    <verse eid="LUK 16:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 16:6"/>
    He replied, ‘Eight hundred gallons of olive oil.’ The steward said to him, ‘Take your bill, sit down immediately, and revise it to four hundred.’
    <verse eid="LUK 16:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 16:7"/>
    Then he said to another, ‘And how much do you owe?’ He answered, ‘A thousand measures of wheat.’ He said to him, ‘Take your bill and change it to eight hundred.’
    <verse eid="LUK 16:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 16:8"/>
    The master praised the dishonest steward, not for his corruption, but because he had acted with shrewd foresight. For the sons of this present age show greater practical wisdom in dealing with their own affairs than the sons of light do.
    <verse eid="LUK 16:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 16:9"/>
    And I tell you, make use of earthly wealth to cultivate relationships for eternal purposes, so that when such wealth comes to an end, you will be welcomed into everlasting dwellings.
    <verse eid="LUK 16:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 16:10"/>
    The one who proves faithful in very small matters will also be faithful in great matters, and the one who is dishonest in small things will also be dishonest in much.
    <verse eid="LUK 16:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 16:11"/>
    Therefore, if you have not shown yourselves trustworthy in handling unrighteous wealth, who will entrust to you the true and lasting riches?
    <verse eid="LUK 16:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 16:12"/>
    And if you have not been faithful with what belongs to another, who will give you what is truly your own?
    <verse eid="LUK 16:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 16:13"/>
    No servant can remain loyal to two masters. Either he will hate the one and love the other, or he will cling to the one and despise the other. You cannot serve God and wealth.”
    <verse eid="LUK 16:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Pharisaic Love of Money and Hypocrisy</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 16:14"/>
    The Pharisees, who were lovers of money, were listening to all these things and were mocking Him with contempt.
    <verse eid="LUK 16:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 16:15"/>
    Jesus said to them, “You are the ones who present yourselves as righteous in the sight of men, but God has full knowledge of your hearts. What is highly esteemed among men is repulsive in the sight of God.”
    <verse eid="LUK 16:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Law and the Kingdom of God</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 11:12–13</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 16:16"/>
    “The Law and the Prophets were proclaimed up until the time of John. From that point on, the good news of the kingdom of God is being announced, and people are pressing into it with urgent determination.
    <verse eid="LUK 16:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 16:17"/>
    But it would be easier for the entire created order to collapse than for the smallest mark of the Law to lose its authority or fail to be fulfilled.
    <verse eid="LUK 16:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 16:18"/>
    Everyone who divorces his wife and marries another commits adultery, and the one who marries a woman divorced from her husband also commits adultery.”
    <verse eid="LUK 16:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Rich Man and Lazarus</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 16:19"/>
    “There was a rich man who clothed himself in purple garments and fine linen and lived in continual luxury and self-indulgence.
    <verse eid="LUK 16:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 16:20"/>
    And laid at his gate was a poor man named Lazarus, whose body was covered with open sores.
    <verse eid="LUK 16:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 16:21"/>
    He longed to be fed with what fell from the rich man’s table. Even the dogs came and licked his wounds.
    <verse eid="LUK 16:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 16:22"/>
    The day came when the poor man died, and he was carried by angels to Abraham’s side. The rich man also died and was buried.
    <verse eid="LUK 16:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 16:23"/>
    While he was in Hades, suffering torment, he lifted up his eyes and saw Abraham from a great distance, with Lazarus resting at his side.
    <verse eid="LUK 16:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 16:24"/>
    He cried out and said, ‘Father Abraham, have mercy on me, and send Lazarus so that he may dip the tip of his finger in water and cool my tongue, because I am suffering terribly in this flame.’
    <verse eid="LUK 16:24"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 16:25"/>
    But Abraham said, ‘Son, remember that during your lifetime you received what you considered good, while Lazarus received what was painful and harmful. Now he is being comforted here, and you are in anguish.
    <verse eid="LUK 16:25"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 16:26"/>
    And beyond all this, a vast chasm has been permanently fixed between us and you, so that those who desire to pass from here to you are unable to do so, and no one can cross from there to us.’
    <verse eid="LUK 16:26"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 16:27"/>
    Then he said, ‘I beg you, father, send Lazarus to my father’s household,
    <verse eid="LUK 16:27"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 16:28"/>
    for I have five brothers. Let him warn them, so that they will not also come to this place of torment.’
    <verse eid="LUK 16:28"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 16:29"/>
    But Abraham said, ‘They have Moses and the Prophets; they must listen to them.’
    <verse eid="LUK 16:29"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 16:30"/>
    He replied, ‘No, father Abraham, but if someone goes to them from the dead, they will repent.’
    <verse eid="LUK 16:30"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 16:31"/>
    Abraham said to him, ‘If they do not listen to Moses and the Prophets, they will not be persuaded even if someone rises from the dead.’ ”
    <verse eid="LUK 16:31"/>
  </para>
  <chapter eid="LUK 16"/>
  <chapter number="17" style="c" sid="LUK 17"/>
  <para style="s1">Some Sayings of Jesus: Causes of Sin</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 18:6-7; 21-22; Mk. 9:42</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 17:1"/>
    Jesus told His disciples, “It is inevitable that temptations and occasions to sin will come into the world. But woe and judgment await the person who becomes the instrument through which they come!
    <verse eid="LUK 17:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 17:2"/>
    It would be far better for that person to have a heavy millstone fastened around his neck and be hurled into the sea than to cause even one of these little believers to stumble and fall into sin.
    <verse eid="LUK 17:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 17:3"/>
    Pay careful attention to your own conduct! If your brother commits a sin, confront him directly about it. If he repents and turns from it, forgive him.
    <verse eid="LUK 17:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 17:4"/>
    Even if he sins against you seven times in a single day and returns to you seven times saying, ‘I repent,’ you must grant him forgiveness.”
    <verse eid="LUK 17:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Augmenting Faith</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 17:5"/>
    The apostles said to the Lord, “Grant us greater faith! Increase our capacity to believe and trust!”
    <verse eid="LUK 17:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 17:6"/>
    The Lord replied, “If you possessed faith even as tiny as a mustard seed—the smallest of all seeds—you could command this mulberry tree, ‘Be uprooted and transplanted into the sea,’ and it would obey you.” 
    <verse eid="LUK 17:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Unmeritorious Servants</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 17:7"/>
    Jesus continued His teaching: “Suppose one of you has a servant who has been plowing the field or shepherding the sheep. When he returns from the field, would you immediately say to him, ‘Come at once and recline at the table to eat’?
    <verse eid="LUK 17:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 17:8"/>
    Would you not rather tell him, ‘Prepare my supper, dress yourself properly, and serve me while I eat and drink. Afterward you may eat and drink your own meal’?
    <verse eid="LUK 17:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 17:9"/>
    Does the master thank the servant simply for doing what was commanded of him? Certainly not.
    <verse eid="LUK 17:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 17:10"/>
    In the same way, when you have done everything you were commanded to do, you should say, ‘We are unworthy servants who deserve no special recognition. We have merely done our duty and what was expected of us.’ ”
    <verse eid="LUK 17:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Healing of the Ten Lepers</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 17:11"/>
    While Jesus was traveling toward Jerusalem, He passed through the border region between Samaria and Galilee.
    <verse eid="LUK 17:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 17:12"/>
    As He was entering a certain village, ten men afflicted with leprosy approached Him. Because of their ceremonial uncleanness, they stood at a distance as required by Law.
    <verse eid="LUK 17:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 17:13"/>
    They raised their voices and called out, “Jesus, Master, have mercy on us and heal us!”
    <verse eid="LUK 17:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 17:14"/>
    When Jesus saw them, He said, “Go and show yourselves to the priests for examination and certification of your healing.” As they were going on their way in obedience, they were miraculously cleansed.
    <verse eid="LUK 17:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 17:15"/>
    One of them, when he realized that he had been healed, turned back to Jesus, glorifying God with a loud voice.
    <verse eid="LUK 17:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 17:16"/>
    He threw himself down at Jesus’ feet in gratitude and worship, thanking Him profusely. And this man was a Samaritan—a despised foreigner.
    <verse eid="LUK 17:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 17:17"/>
    Jesus responded by asking, “Were not all ten cleansed from their leprosy? Where are the other nine?
    <verse eid="LUK 17:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 17:18"/>
    Has no one returned to give glory and praise to God except this foreigner?”
    <verse eid="LUK 17:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 17:19"/>
    Then Jesus said to him, “Stand up and go on your way. Your faith has made you completely well.”
    <verse eid="LUK 17:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Coming of God’s Kingdom</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 24:23-28; 37-41</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 17:20"/>
    When the Pharisees asked Jesus when the kingdom of God would come, He answered them, “The kingdom of God does not come with careful observation of signs or with visible display.
    <verse eid="LUK 17:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 17:21"/>
    People will not be able to say, ‘Look, here it is!’ or 'There it is!’ For the kingdom of God is already in your midst through My presence.”
    <verse eid="LUK 17:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 17:22"/>
    Then Jesus said to His disciples, “The time is coming when you will long to see one of the days of the Son of Man—to experience My presence again—but you will not see it.
    <verse eid="LUK 17:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 17:23"/>
    People will say to you, ‘Look, there He is!’ or ‘Here He is!’ Do not go running after them or follow them.
    <verse eid="LUK 17:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 17:24"/>
    For when the Son of Man comes in His day, He will be like lightning that flashes and illuminates the sky from one end to the other—unmistakable and visible to all.
    <verse eid="LUK 17:24"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 17:25"/>
    But first He must suffer many things and be rejected by this generation.
    <verse eid="LUK 17:25"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 17:26"/>
    Just as it was in the days of Noah, so it will be in the days of the Son of Man.
    <verse eid="LUK 17:26"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 17:27"/>
    People were eating and drinking, marrying and being given in marriage, right up until the day Noah entered the ark. Then the flood came and destroyed them all without warning.
    <verse eid="LUK 17:27"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 17:28"/>
    It was exactly the same in the days of Lot: People were eating and drinking, buying and selling, planting and building—carrying on with normal life.
    <verse eid="LUK 17:28"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 17:29"/>
    But on the day Lot departed from Sodom, fire and burning sulfur rained down from heaven and destroyed them all.
    <verse eid="LUK 17:29"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 17:30"/>
    It will be precisely like this on the day when the Son of Man is revealed to the world.
    <verse eid="LUK 17:30"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 17:31"/>
    On that day, no one who is on the housetop should go down into the house to retrieve their belongings. Likewise, no one in the field should turn back toward home.
    <verse eid="LUK 17:31"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 17:32"/>
    Remember what happened to Lot’s wife when she looked back longingly.”
    <verse eid="LUK 17:32"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 17:33"/>
    Whoever attempts to preserve and save his life will lose it, but whoever is willing to lose his life will preserve it for eternity.
    <verse eid="LUK 17:33"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 17:34"/>
    I tell you, on that night two people will be lying in one bed together. One will be taken and the other will be left.
    <verse eid="LUK 17:34"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 17:35"/>
    Two women will be grinding grain together at the mill. One will be taken and the other left.”*
    <verse eid="LUK 17:35"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 17:37"/>
    The disciples asked Him, “Where will this happen, Lord?” Jesus replied, “Where the dead body is, there the vultures will naturally gather.”
    <verse eid="LUK 17:37"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Notes</para>
  <para style="p">
    35 A textual variant appears in some ancient manuscripts, inserting a verse 36. Its content—
    <char style="it">“Two [men] will be in the field. The one will be taken and the other will be left”</char>
    —serves to harmonize the narrative with its parallel in the Gospel of Matthew 24:40
  </para>
  <chapter eid="LUK 17"/>
  <chapter number="18" style="c" sid="LUK 18"/>
  <para style="s1">The Parable of the Persistent Widow and the Judge</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 18:1"/>
    Jesus told His disciples a parable to teach them that they should always pray and never lose heart or give up.
    <verse eid="LUK 18:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 18:2"/>
    He said: “In a certain town there was a judge who had no fear of God and no respect or regard for people.
    <verse eid="LUK 18:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 18:3"/>
    There was a widow in that same town who kept coming to him repeatedly, pleading, ‘Grant me justice against my adversary.’
    <verse eid="LUK 18:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 18:4"/>
    For a long time the judge refused to help her. But eventually he said to himself, ‘Even though I have no fear of God and no concern for people,
    <verse eid="LUK 18:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 18:5"/>
    yet because this widow keeps bothering me persistently, I will grant her justice, so that she will not eventually wear me down completely with her continual coming.’ ”
    <verse eid="LUK 18:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 18:6"/>
    The Lord said, “Pay attention to what this unjust judge says.
    <verse eid="LUK 18:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 18:7"/>
    Will not God bring about justice for His chosen ones who cry out to Him day and night? Will He delay long in helping them?
    <verse eid="LUK 18:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 18:8"/>
    I tell you, He will see that they receive justice, and He will do it quickly. However, when the Son of Man comes, will He find such faith and persistent, faithful prayer on the earth?”
    <verse eid="LUK 18:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Parable of the Pharisee and the Tax Collector</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 18:9"/>
    Jesus also told this parable to some people who were confident in their own righteousness and looked down with contempt on everyone else:
    <verse eid="LUK 18:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 18:10"/>
    “Two men went up to the temple to pray. One was a Pharisee and the other was a tax collector.
    <verse eid="LUK 18:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 18:11"/>
    The Pharisee stood apart by himself and prayed about himself: ‘God, I thank You that I am not like other people—robbers, evildoers, adulterers—or even like this tax collector over there.
    <verse eid="LUK 18:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 18:12"/>
    I fast twice a week and give a tenth of everything I earn and acquire.’
    <verse eid="LUK 18:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 18:13"/>
    But the tax collector stood at a distance, far from the holy place. He would not even lift his eyes toward heaven, but beat his breast in contrition and said, ‘God, be merciful to me, a sinner!’
    <verse eid="LUK 18:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 18:14"/>
    I tell you, this man went home justified before God rather than the other. For everyone who exalts himself will be humbled, and the one who humbles himself will be exalted by God.”
    <verse eid="LUK 18:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Receiving the Kingdom of God Like a Child</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 19:13-15; Mk. 10:13-16</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 18:15"/>
    People were bringing even their babies and infants to Jesus so that He might place His hands on them and bless them. When the disciples saw this, they rebuked the people for bothering Jesus.
    <verse eid="LUK 18:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 18:16"/>
    But Jesus called the children to Himself and said, “Let the little children come to Me, and do not prevent them, for the kingdom of God belongs to such as these.
    <verse eid="LUK 18:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 18:17"/>
    Truly I tell you, anyone who does not receive the kingdom of God like a little child—with simple trust and humility—will never enter it.”
    <verse eid="LUK 18:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Rich Ruler</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 19:16-30; Mk. 10:17-31</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 18:18"/>
    A certain ruler, a man of authority and wealth, asked Jesus, “Good Teacher, what must I do to inherit eternal life?”
    <verse eid="LUK 18:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 18:19"/>
    Jesus answered him, &quot;Why do you call Me good? No one is good in an absolute sense except God alone.
    <verse eid="LUK 18:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 18:20"/>
    You know the commandments: ‘Do not commit adultery, do not murder, do not steal, do not give false testimony, honor your father and mother.’ ”*
    <verse eid="LUK 18:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 18:21"/>
    The man replied confidently, “I have faithfully kept all these commandments since I was young.”
    <verse eid="LUK 18:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 18:22"/>
    When Jesus heard this, He said to him, “You still lack one thing. Sell everything you possess and distribute the proceeds to the poor, and you will have treasure in heaven. Then come and follow Me.”
    <verse eid="LUK 18:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 18:23"/>
    When the ruler heard this, he became extremely sad, for he was extraordinarily wealthy and could not bring himself to part with his possessions.
    <verse eid="LUK 18:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 18:24"/>
    Jesus looked at him and said, “How hard it is for those who have riches to enter the kingdom of God!
    <verse eid="LUK 18:24"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 18:25"/>
    Indeed, it is easier for a camel to pass through the eye of a sewing needle than for a rich person to enter the kingdom of God!”
    <verse eid="LUK 18:25"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 18:26"/>
    Those who heard this asked in amazement, “Who then can be saved?”
    <verse eid="LUK 18:26"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 18:27"/>
    Jesus replied, “What is impossible with human beings is possible with God.”
    <verse eid="LUK 18:27"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 18:28"/>
    Peter said, “Look, we have left everything we had—our homes, our occupations, our security—to follow You!”
    <verse eid="LUK 18:28"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 18:29"/>
    Jesus said to them, “Truly I tell you, there is no one who has left home or wife or brothers or sisters or parents or children for the sake of the kingdom of God
    <verse eid="LUK 18:29"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 18:30"/>
    who will not receive many times as much in this present age, and in the age to come, eternal life.”
    <verse eid="LUK 18:30"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Jesus Speaks of His Death and Resurrection the Third Time</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 20:17-19; Mk. 10:32-34</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 18:31"/>
    Jesus took the Twelve aside privately and told them, “Listen carefully: We are going up to Jerusalem, and everything that has been written by the prophets about the Son of Man will be fulfilled and accomplished.
    <verse eid="LUK 18:31"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 18:32"/>
    He will be handed over to the Gentiles. They will mock Him, insult Him, and spit on Him in contempt.
    <verse eid="LUK 18:32"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 18:33"/>
    They will flog Him mercilessly and kill Him. But on the third day He will rise again from the dead.”
    <verse eid="LUK 18:33"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 18:34"/>
    The disciples did not understand any of this teaching. Its meaning was completely hidden from them, and they could not grasp what Jesus was talking about.
    <verse eid="LUK 18:34"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Healing of a Blind Beggar Near Jericho</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 20:29-34; Mk. 10:46-52</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 18:35"/>
    As Jesus was approaching the city of Jericho, a blind man was sitting beside the road begging for alms.
    <verse eid="LUK 18:35"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 18:36"/>
    When he heard the crowd passing by, he inquired what was happening.
    <verse eid="LUK 18:36"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 18:37"/>
    They informed him, “Jesus of Nazareth is passing by this way.”
    <verse eid="LUK 18:37"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 18:38"/>
    He called out loudly, “Jesus, Son of David, have mercy on me!”
    <verse eid="LUK 18:38"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="39" style="v" sid="LUK 18:39"/>
    Those who were leading the procession rebuked him and told him to be quiet. But he shouted all the more insistently, “Son of David, have mercy on me!”
    <verse eid="LUK 18:39"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="40" style="v" sid="LUK 18:40"/>
    Jesus stopped and ordered that the man be brought to Him. When he came near, Jesus asked him,
    <verse eid="LUK 18:40"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="41" style="v" sid="LUK 18:41"/>
    “What do you want Me to do for you?” The blind man replied, “Lord, I want to receive my sight.”
    <verse eid="LUK 18:41"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="42" style="v" sid="LUK 18:42"/>
    Jesus said to him, “Receive your sight. Your faith has healed you.”
    <verse eid="LUK 18:42"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="43" style="v" sid="LUK 18:43"/>
    Immediately he received his sight and began following Jesus, praising God. When all the people witnessed this miracle, they also gave praise to God.
    <verse eid="LUK 18:43"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Notes</para>
  <para style="p">20 Quoted from Ex. 20:12-16; Deut. 5:16-20</para>
  <chapter eid="LUK 18"/>
  <chapter number="19" style="c" sid="LUK 19"/>
  <para style="s1">Zacchaeus Seeks Jesus</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 19:1"/>
    Jesus entered the city of Jericho and was passing through it.
    <verse eid="LUK 19:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 19:2"/>
    A man named Zacchaeus was there. He was a chief tax collector—a supervisor over other tax collectors—and he was very wealthy.
    <verse eid="LUK 19:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 19:3"/>
    Zacchaeus wanted to see who Jesus was, but he could not see over the crowd because he was short in stature.
    <verse eid="LUK 19:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 19:4"/>
    So he ran ahead of the crowd and climbed up into a sycamore-fig tree to see Jesus, since Jesus was going to pass that way.
    <verse eid="LUK 19:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 19:5"/>
    When Jesus reached that spot, He looked up and said to him, “Zacchaeus, come down immediately. I must stay at your house today.” 
    <verse eid="LUK 19:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 19:6"/>
    So Zacchaeus came down quickly and welcomed Jesus gladly into his home.
    <verse eid="LUK 19:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 19:7"/>
    All the people who saw this began muttering, &quot;He has gone to be the guest of a notorious sinner.&quot;
    <verse eid="LUK 19:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 19:8"/>
    But Zacchaeus stood before the Lord and said, “Look, Lord! Here and now I am giving half of my possessions to the poor. And if I have cheated anyone out of anything through false accusations, I will pay back four times the amount.”
    <verse eid="LUK 19:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 19:9"/>
    Jesus said to him, “Today salvation has come to this household, because this man, too, is a true son of Abraham by faith.
    <verse eid="LUK 19:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 19:10"/>
    For the Son of Man came to seek and to save those who are lost.”
    <verse eid="LUK 19:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Parable of the Ten Minas</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 25:14-30</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 19:11"/>
    While the people were listening to this encounter, Jesus went on to tell them a parable. He did this because He was near Jerusalem and the people thought that the kingdom of God was going to appear immediately.
    <verse eid="LUK 19:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 19:12"/>
    Jesus said: “A man of noble birth traveled to a distant country to be appointed as king and then to return home.
    <verse eid="LUK 19:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 19:13"/>
    Before leaving, he called ten of his servants and gave them ten minas—a substantial sum. He instructed them, ‘Put this money to work in business until I return.’
    <verse eid="LUK 19:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 19:14"/>
    But his subjects hated him and sent a delegation after him to declare, ‘We do not want this man to reign as king over us.’
    <verse eid="LUK 19:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 19:15"/>
    Nevertheless, he was appointed king and returned home. Then he summoned the servants to whom he had given the money, in order to find out what profit they had gained through trading.
    <verse eid="LUK 19:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 19:16"/>
    The first servant came and reported, ‘Sir, your mina has earned ten more minas.’
    <verse eid="LUK 19:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 19:17"/>
    His master commended him: ‘Well done, my good servant! Because you have been trustworthy and faithful in a very small matter, I am putting you in charge of ten cities.’
    <verse eid="LUK 19:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 19:18"/>
    The second servant came and said, ‘Sir, your mina has earned five more minas.’
    <verse eid="LUK 19:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 19:19"/>
    His master told him, ‘You are to be in charge of five cities.’
    <verse eid="LUK 19:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 19:20"/>
    Then another servant came and said, ‘Sir, here is your mina. I have kept it safely laid away in a piece of cloth.
    <verse eid="LUK 19:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 19:21"/>
    I was afraid of you, because you are a hard and demanding man. You take out what you did not deposit and reap what you did not sow.’
    <verse eid="LUK 19:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 19:22"/>
    His master replied, ‘I will judge you by your own words, you wicked servant! You knew that I am a hard man, taking out what I did not put in and reaping what I did not sow?
    <verse eid="LUK 19:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 19:23"/>
    Then why did you not put my money on deposit with the bankers, so that when I returned, I could have collected it with interest?’
    <verse eid="LUK 19:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 19:24"/>
    Then he said to those standing nearby, ‘Take his mina away from him and give it to the one who has ten minas.’
    <verse eid="LUK 19:24"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 19:25"/>
    They protested, ‘Sir, he already has ten!’
    <verse eid="LUK 19:25"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 19:26"/>
    The master replied, ‘I tell you that to everyone who has been faithful, more will be given. But from the one who has nothing—who has been unfaithful—even what he has will be taken away.
    <verse eid="LUK 19:26"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 19:27"/>
    But those enemies of mine who did not want me to reign as king over them, bring them here and execute them in my presence.’ ”
    <verse eid="LUK 19:27"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Triumphal Entry into Jerusalem</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 21:1-11; Mk. 11:1-11; Jn. 12:12-19</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 19:28"/>
    After Jesus had said these things, He went on ahead of the group, going up toward Jerusalem.
    <verse eid="LUK 19:28"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 19:29"/>
    As He approached Bethphage and Bethany at the Mount of Olives, He sent two of His disciples ahead,
    <verse eid="LUK 19:29"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 19:30"/>
    saying to them, “Go to the village ahead of you. As you enter it, you will find a colt tied there that no one has ever ridden. Untie it and bring it here to Me.
    <verse eid="LUK 19:30"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 19:31"/>
    If anyone asks you, ‘Why are you untying it?' simply say, 'The Lord needs it.’ ”
    <verse eid="LUK 19:31"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 19:32"/>
    Those who were sent went ahead and found everything just as Jesus had told them.
    <verse eid="LUK 19:32"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 19:33"/>
    As they were untying the colt, its owners asked them, “Why are you untying the colt?”
    <verse eid="LUK 19:33"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 19:34"/>
    They answered, “The Lord needs it.”
    <verse eid="LUK 19:34"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 19:35"/>
    They brought the colt to Jesus, threw their cloaks on it, and set Jesus on it.
    <verse eid="LUK 19:35"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 19:36"/>
    As Jesus rode along, people spread their cloaks on the road as a carpet of honor.
    <verse eid="LUK 19:36"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 19:37"/>
    When He came near the place where the road descends from the Mount of Olives, the whole crowd of disciples began joyfully praising God in loud voices for all the miracles they had witnessed:
    <verse eid="LUK 19:37"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 19:38"/>
    “Blessed is the King who comes in the name of the Lord! Peace in heaven and glory in the highest!”
    <verse eid="LUK 19:38"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="39" style="v" sid="LUK 19:39"/>
    Some Pharisees in the crowd said to Jesus, “Teacher, rebuke Your disciples and make them stop!”
    <verse eid="LUK 19:39"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="40" style="v" sid="LUK 19:40"/>
    Jesus replied, “I tell you, if they kept silent, the very stones would cry out in proclamation.”
    <verse eid="LUK 19:40"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Jesus Weeps Over Jerusalem</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="41" style="v" sid="LUK 19:41"/>
    As Jesus approached Jerusalem and saw the city spread before Him, He wept over it
    <verse eid="LUK 19:41"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="42" style="v" sid="LUK 19:42"/>
    and said, “If only you had known on this day what would bring you peace! But now it is hidden from your eyes and you cannot see it.
    <verse eid="LUK 19:42"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="43" style="v" sid="LUK 19:43"/>
    The days will come upon you when your enemies will build siege walls against you. They will encircle you and hem you in on every side.
    <verse eid="LUK 19:43"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="44" style="v" sid="LUK 19:44"/>
    They will dash you to the ground—you and your children within your walls. They will not leave one stone standing on another, because you did not recognize the time when God visited you.”
    <verse eid="LUK 19:44"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Cleansing of the Temple</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 21:12-17; Mk. 11:15-19; Jn. 2:13-22</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="45" style="v" sid="LUK 19:45"/>
    When Jesus entered the temple courts, He began driving out those who were selling sacrificial animals and exchanging money.
    <verse eid="LUK 19:45"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="46" style="v" sid="LUK 19:46"/>
    He declared to them, “It is written in Scripture, ‘My house shall be a house of prayer,’ but you have made it ‘a den of robbers.’ ”
    <verse eid="LUK 19:46"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="47" style="v" sid="LUK 19:47"/>
    Every day Jesus was teaching in the temple courts. But the chief priests, the teachers of the Law, and the leaders among the people were actively seeking to kill Him.
    <verse eid="LUK 19:47"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="48" style="v" sid="LUK 19:48"/>
    Yet they could not find any way to accomplish this, because all the people hung on His words and listened to Him intently.
    <verse eid="LUK 19:48"/>
  </para>
  <chapter eid="LUK 19"/>
  <chapter number="20" style="c" sid="LUK 20"/>
  <para style="s1">Jesus’ Authority Questioned</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 21:23-27; Mk. 11:27-33</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 20:1"/>
    One day as Jesus was teaching the people in the temple courts and proclaiming the good news, the chief priests and teachers of the Law, together with the elders, confronted Him.
    <verse eid="LUK 20:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 20:2"/>
    They challenged Him, “Tell us by what authority You are doing these things. Who gave You this authority to teach and act as You do?”
    <verse eid="LUK 20:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 20:3"/>
    Jesus replied, “I will also ask you a question. Tell Me:
    <verse eid="LUK 20:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 20:4"/>
    Was John’s baptism from heaven—from divine authority—or from human origin?”
    <verse eid="LUK 20:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 20:5"/>
    They discussed this among themselves and reasoned, “If we say, ‘From heaven,’ He will ask us, ‘Why did you not believe him?’
    <verse eid="LUK 20:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 20:6"/>
    But if we say, ‘From human origin,’ all the people will stone us, because they are firmly convinced that John was a prophet.”
    <verse eid="LUK 20:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 20:7"/>
    So they responded that they could not tell where John’s authority originated.
    <verse eid="LUK 20:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 20:8"/>
    And Jesus declared to them, “Neither will I inform you by what authority I perform these actions.”
    <verse eid="LUK 20:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Parable of the Wicked Farmers</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 21:33-46; Mk. 12:1-12</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 20:9"/>
    Then He began communicating this parable to the people: “A certain man established a vineyard, rented it out to farmers, and departed to a distant region for an extended period.
    <verse eid="LUK 20:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 20:10"/>
    When the season dispatched a servant to the farmers so they might give him his portion of the vineyard’s produce. But the farmers struck the servant and sent him back without anything.
    <verse eid="LUK 20:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 20:11"/>
    He proceeded to send another servant, but they beat him as well, subjected him to shameful treatment, and sent him back empty-handed.
    <verse eid="LUK 20:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 20:12"/>
    He went on to send a third, and they wounded this one and cast him out.
    <verse eid="LUK 20:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 20:13"/>
    Then the vineyard’s owner said, ‘What course of action should I take? I will send my son whom I love. Surely they will show reverence to him.’
    <verse eid="LUK 20:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 20:14"/>
    But upon seeing the son, the farmers reasoned among themselves, saying, ‘This is the heir. Let us kill him so that the inheritance will transfer to us.’
    <verse eid="LUK 20:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 20:15"/>
    So they expelled him from the vineyard and killed him. What action, then, will the vineyard’s owner take against them?
    <verse eid="LUK 20:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 20:16"/>
    He will come and eliminate those farmers and entrust the vineyard to different people.” Upon hearing this, they responded, “May such a thing never occur!”
    <verse eid="LUK 20:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 20:17"/>
    But He looked intently at them and asked, “What then is the significance of this written passage:
    <verse eid="LUK 20:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">‘The stone that the builders discarded has become the chief cornerstone’?*</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 20:18"/>
    Everyone falling upon that stone will be shattered to pieces,
    <verse eid="LUK 20:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">and the person upon whom it falls will be pulverized.”*</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 20:19"/>
    The scribes and the chief priests sought to seize Him at that very moment, for they understood that He had directed this parable against them. Yet they feared the people’s response.
    <verse eid="LUK 20:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Paying Taxes to Caesar</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 22:15-22; Mk. 12:13-17</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 20:20"/>
    So they watched Him closely and sent agents who posed as righteous men, hoping to trap Him in His speech so they might deliver Him to the governor's jurisdiction and authority.
    <verse eid="LUK 20:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 20:21"/>
    They questioned Him, saying, “Teacher, we recognize that You communicate and instruct rightly and without error. You demonstrate no favoritism but teach God’s way according to truth.
    <verse eid="LUK 20:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 20:22"/>
    Is it lawful for us to pay tribute to Caesar, or not?”
    <verse eid="LUK 20:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 20:23"/>
    But perceiving their craftiness, He said to them,
    <verse eid="LUK 20:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 20:24"/>
    “Display a denarius for Me. Whose likeness and inscription does it bear?” They replied, “Caesar's.”
    <verse eid="LUK 20:24"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 20:25"/>
    He declared to them, “Then render to Caesar the things that are Caesar’s, and to God the things that are God’s.”
    <verse eid="LUK 20:25"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 20:26"/>
    They proved unable to entrap Him in His statement in the people’s presence. Marveling at His response, they became silent.
    <verse eid="LUK 20:26"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Concerning Marriage and the Resurrection</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 22:23-33; Mk. 12:18-27</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 20:27"/>
    Some of the Sadducees—who deny that there is a resurrection—came to Jesus with a hypothetical question.
    <verse eid="LUK 20:27"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 20:28"/>
    They said, “Teacher, Moses wrote for us in the Law that if a man’s brother dies leaving a wife but no children, the man must marry the widow and raise up offspring for his brother.
    <verse eid="LUK 20:28"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 20:29"/>
    Now there existed seven brothers. The first took a wife and died without children.
    <verse eid="LUK 20:29"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 20:30"/>
    The second married her,
    <verse eid="LUK 20:30"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 20:31"/>
    and the third took her. In this same manner, all seven married her and died without leaving children.
    <verse eid="LUK 20:31"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 20:32"/>
    Afterward, the woman died as well.
    <verse eid="LUK 20:32"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 20:33"/>
    In the resurrection, therefore, whose wife does she become, since all seven had her as wife?”
    <verse eid="LUK 20:33"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 20:34"/>
    Jesus responded to them, “The sons of this age marry and are given in marriage.
    <verse eid="LUK 20:34"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 20:35"/>
    But those judged worthy to attain that age and the resurrection from the dead neither marry nor are given in marriage.
    <verse eid="LUK 20:35"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 20:36"/>
    For they are no longer able to die, being equal to angels. And they are sons of God, being sons of the resurrection.
    <verse eid="LUK 20:36"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 20:37"/>
    But that the dead are raised—even Moses indicated this at the passage about the bush, when he designates the Lord as ‘the God of Abraham, and the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob.’
    <verse eid="LUK 20:37"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 20:38"/>
    Now He is not God of the dead but of the living, for to Him all are alive.”
    <verse eid="LUK 20:38"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="39" style="v" sid="LUK 20:39"/>
    Some of the scribes replied, “Teacher, You have spoken well!”
    <verse eid="LUK 20:39"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="40" style="v" sid="LUK 20:40"/>
    And they no longer had courage to question Him about anything.
    <verse eid="LUK 20:40"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Question about David’s Son</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 22:41-46; Mk. 12:35-37</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="41" style="v" sid="LUK 20:41"/>
    Then Jesus said to them, “Why do people say that the Christ is the son of David?
    <verse eid="LUK 20:41"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="42" style="v" sid="LUK 20:42"/>
    David himself declares in the Book of Psalms: 
    <verse eid="LUK 20:42"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">‘The Lord said to my Lord: </para>
  <para style="p">“Sit at My right hand</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="43" style="v" sid="LUK 20:43"/>
    until I make Your enemies a footstool for Your feet.” ’
    <verse eid="LUK 20:43"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="44" style="v" sid="LUK 20:44"/>
    David calls Him ‘Lord.’ How then can the Christ be his son?”
    <verse eid="LUK 20:44"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Beware of the Scribes</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 23:1-36; Mk. 12:38-40; Lk. 11:37-54</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="45" style="v" sid="LUK 20:45"/>
    While all the people were listening, Jesus said to His disciples,
    <verse eid="LUK 20:45"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="46" style="v" sid="LUK 20:46"/>
    “Beware of the teachers of the Law. They love to walk around in flowing robes and love to receive respectful greetings in the marketplaces. They desire the most important seats in the synagogues and the places of honour at banquets.
    <verse eid="LUK 20:46"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="47" style="v" sid="LUK 20:47"/>
    They devour widows’ houses, and for show they make lengthy prayers. These men will receive the most severe condemnation and punishment.”
    <verse eid="LUK 20:47"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Notes</para>
  <para style="p">17 Quoted from Ps. 118:22</para>
  <para style="p">18 Referring to Is. 8:14-15</para>
  <chapter eid="LUK 20"/>
  <chapter number="21" style="c" sid="LUK 21"/>
  <para style="s1">The Poor Widow’s Offering</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 12:41-44</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 21:1"/>
    Jesus looked up and observed the wealthy people depositing their generous gifts into the temple treasury.
    <verse eid="LUK 21:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 21:2"/>
    He also noticed a certain poor widow placing two small copper coins into the offering—the smallest denomination of currency.
    <verse eid="LUK 21:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 21:3"/>
    Jesus said to His disciples, “Truly, I tell you that this poor widow has given more than all the others combined.
    <verse eid="LUK 21:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 21:4"/>
    For all these wealthy people gave their gifts out of their abundance and surplus. But she, out of her poverty and need, has given all the means of livelihood she possessed.”
    <verse eid="LUK 21:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Prediction of the Destruction of the Temple</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 24:1-2; Mk. 13:1-2</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 21:5"/>
    As some of the disciples were commenting on the temple—how it was adorned with beautiful stones and dedicated offerings—Jesus made a startling prediction.
    <verse eid="LUK 21:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 21:6"/>
    He said, “Regarding these things you are admiring—the days will come when not one stone will be left upon another. Everything will be completely demolished.”
    <verse eid="LUK 21:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Eschatological Discourse: The Beginning of Woes</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 24:3-14; Mk. 13:3-13</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 21:7"/>
    The disciples asked Him, “Teacher, when will these things happen? What will be the sign indicating when these events are about to occur?”
    <verse eid="LUK 21:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 21:8"/>
    Jesus answered, “Be careful that you are not led astray. Many will come claiming My authority, saying, ‘I am the Christ,’ and ‘The time of the end has drawn near!’ Do not follow after them.
    <verse eid="LUK 21:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 21:9"/>
    When you hear reports of wars and political upheavals, do not be terrified or panic-stricken. These things must take place first, but the end of the age will not come immediately.”
    <verse eid="LUK 21:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 21:10"/>
    Jesus continued His instruction: “Nation will rise up in conflict against nation, and kingdom will war against kingdom.
    <verse eid="LUK 21:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 21:11"/>
    There will be massive earthquakes, and in various places famines and devastating plagues. There will be terrifying events and great miraculous signs appearing from heaven.
    <verse eid="LUK 21:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 21:12"/>
    But before all these things happen, they will arrest you and persecute you severely. They will hand you over to the synagogues for punishment and throw you into prisons. You will be brought before kings and governors because of your association with My name.
    <verse eid="LUK 21:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 21:13"/>
    This persecution will result in opportunities for you to bear witness and testify about Me.
    <verse eid="LUK 21:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 21:14"/>
    Therefore, settle it firmly in your hearts not to worry in advance about how you will defend yourselves or what you will say.
    <verse eid="LUK 21:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 21:15"/>
    For I will give you a mouth to speak and wisdom that none of your adversaries will be able to resist or contradict.
    <verse eid="LUK 21:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 21:16"/>
    You will be betrayed even by parents, brothers, relatives, and friends. Some of you will be put to death for your faith.
    <verse eid="LUK 21:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 21:17"/>
    You will be hated by everyone because of your identification with My name.
    <verse eid="LUK 21:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 21:18"/>
    Yet not a single hair of your head will perish.
    <verse eid="LUK 21:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 21:19"/>
    By your patient endurance and steadfast faithfulness, you will preserve your souls and gain eternal life.”
    <verse eid="LUK 21:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Eschatological Discourse: Prediction of the Destruction of Jerusalem</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 24:15-21; Mk. 13:14-19</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 21:20"/>
    Jesus continued, “When you see Jerusalem surrounded by armies, then understand that its desolation has come near.
    <verse eid="LUK 21:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 21:21"/>
    At that time, let those in Judea flee immediately to the mountains. Let those inside the city depart quickly. Let those in the countryside not enter the city.
    <verse eid="LUK 21:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 21:22"/>
    For these will be days of divine vengeance, when all the things written in Scripture will be fulfilled.
    <verse eid="LUK 21:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 21:23"/>
    How terrible it will be for women who are pregnant and for those nursing infants in those days! There will be great distress upon the land and divine wrath against this people.
    <verse eid="LUK 21:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 21:24"/>
    They will fall by the edge of the sword and be taken captive into all the nations. Jerusalem will be trampled down and controlled by Gentiles until the times of the Gentiles are completed and fulfilled.”
    <verse eid="LUK 21:24"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Eschatological Discourse: The Coming of the Son of Man</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 24:29-31; Mk. 13:24-27</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 21:25"/>
    Jesus also spoke about more distant events: “There will be observable signs in the sun, the moon, and the stars. On the earth, the nations will be overwhelmed with anxiety and confusion as they face the violent roaring and upheaval of the sea and its waves.
    <verse eid="LUK 21:25"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 21:26"/>
    People will faint from terror and apprehension about what is coming upon the world. The powers of the heavens will be shaken and destabilized.
    <verse eid="LUK 21:26"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 21:27"/>
    Then they will see the Son of Man coming in a cloud with power and great glory to establish His kingdom.
    <verse eid="LUK 21:27"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 21:28"/>
    When these things begin to take place, stand up straight and lift up your heads in confidence, because your redemption and deliverance is drawing near!”
    <verse eid="LUK 21:28"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Eschatological Discourse: The Lesson of the Fig Tree</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 24:32-35; Mk. 13:28-31</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 21:29"/>
    Jesus spoke a parable to them: “Observe the fig tree and all the other trees.
    <verse eid="LUK 21:29"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 21:30"/>
    As soon as they sprout leaves, you see for yourselves and know that summer is already near.
    <verse eid="LUK 21:30"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 21:31"/>
    In the same way, when you see these prophesied events occurring, know with certainty that the kingdom of God is near.
    <verse eid="LUK 21:31"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 21:32"/>
    Truly, I tell you that this generation will not pass away until all these things have taken place. Some of these events will occur within the lifetime of those listening.
    <verse eid="LUK 21:32"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 21:33"/>
    Heaven and earth will pass away and cease to exist, but My words will never pass away.”
    <verse eid="LUK 21:33"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Eschatological Discourse: The Imperatives to Pay Attention to Oneself and to Keep Watching Always</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 21:34"/>
    Jesus warned, “Pay careful attention to yourselves. Be on guard lest your hearts become weighed down and dulled by dissipation, drunkenness, and the anxious worries of this life. If you are not vigilant, that day will come upon you suddenly and unexpectedly like a trap. 
    <verse eid="LUK 21:34"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 21:35"/>
    For it will come upon all those who live on the face of the whole earth.
    <verse eid="LUK 21:35"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 21:36"/>
    Stay awake at all times. Pray constantly that you may have strength to escape all these things that will happen, and to stand with confidence before the Son of Man when He returns.”
    <verse eid="LUK 21:36"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Jesus Teaches in the Temple</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 21:37"/>
    During the day, Jesus was teaching in the temple courts. But at night He would leave the city and spend the night on the hill called the Mount of Olives.
    <verse eid="LUK 21:37"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 21:38"/>
    All the people would come to Him at daybreak in the temple to listen to His teaching.
    <verse eid="LUK 21:38"/>
  </para>
  <chapter eid="LUK 21"/>
  <chapter number="22" style="c" sid="LUK 22"/>
  <para style="s1">The Conspiracy to Kill Jesus</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 26:1-5, 14-16; Mk. 14:1-2, 10-11; Jn. 11:45-53</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 22:1"/>
    The Feast of Unleavened Bread, which is called the Passover, was drawing near.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 22:2"/>
    The chief priests and the scribes were actively seeking how they might put Jesus to death, but they feared the reaction of the people who honored Jesus.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Judas Will Betray Jesus at the Instigation of Satan</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 22:3"/>
    Then Satan himself entered into Judas, the one called Iscariot, who was numbered among the twelve apostles.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 22:4"/>
    Judas went away and held discussions with the chief priests and the officers of the temple guard about how he might betray Jesus to them.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 22:5"/>
    They were delighted and agreed to pay him money for his treachery.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 22:6"/>
    Judas consented to their proposal and began looking for a favorable opportunity to betray Jesus to them when no crowd was present.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Jesus Celebrates the Passover with the Disciples</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 26:17-25; Mk. 14:12-21; Jn. 13:21-30</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 22:7"/>
    Then came the day of the Feast of Unleavened Bread, on which the Passover lamb had to be sacrificed according to the Law.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 22:8"/>
    Jesus sent Peter and John with instructions, saying, “Go and make preparations for us to eat the Passover meal together.”
    <verse eid="LUK 22:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 22:9"/>
    They asked Him, “Where do You want us to make preparations?”
    <verse eid="LUK 22:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 22:10"/>
    Jesus said to them, “Listen carefully: When you enter the city, a man carrying a jar of water will meet you. Follow him into whatever house he enters.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 22:11"/>
    Say to the owner of that house, ‘The Teacher asks you: Where is the guest room where I may eat the Passover with My disciples?’
    <verse eid="LUK 22:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 22:12"/>
    He will show you a large, furnished upper room. Make preparations there.”
    <verse eid="LUK 22:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 22:13"/>
    Peter and John departed and found everything exactly as Jesus had told them. They prepared the Passover meal.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Lord’s Supper Instituted</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 26:26-30; Mk. 14:22-26; 1 Cor. 11:23-25</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 22:14"/>
    When the hour arrived, Jesus reclined at the table with the twelve apostles.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 22:15"/>
    He said to them, “I have earnestly and intensely desired to eat this Passover meal with you before I suffer.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 22:16"/>
    For I tell you, I will not eat it again until it finds its fulfillment in the kingdom of God.”
    <verse eid="LUK 22:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 22:17"/>
    Jesus took the cup, gave thanks to God, and said, “Take this and divide it among yourselves.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 22:18"/>
    For I tell you, from this moment forward, I will not drink of the fruit of the vine until the kingdom of God comes in its fullness.”
    <verse eid="LUK 22:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 22:19"/>
    Then Jesus took bread, gave thanks, broke it, and gave it to them, saying, “This is My body, which is given for you as a sacrifice. Do this in remembrance of Me.”
    <verse eid="LUK 22:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 22:20"/>
    In the same way, He took the cup after they had eaten, saying, “This cup represents the new covenant in My blood, which is poured out for you.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 22:21"/>
    But understand this: The hand of the one who is betraying Me is with Me right now on this table.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 22:22"/>
    The Son of Man will go to His death according to what has been predetermined and decreed by God. But woe to that man by whom He is betrayed!”
    <verse eid="LUK 22:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 22:23"/>
    The disciples began discussing among themselves which one of them it might possibly be who would do such a thing.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Dispute About Who Seems to be the Greatest</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 22:24"/>
    A serious argument arose among the disciples concerning which one of them should be regarded as the most prominent.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:24"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 22:25"/>
    Jesus addressed them, saying, “The rulers of the nations dominate their peoples, and those who exercise authority expect recognition as benefactors.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:25"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 22:26"/>
    This must not define life among you. Rather, the one who is regarded as greatest must take the place of the least, and the one who governs must live as a servant.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:26"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 22:27"/>
    Consider this: who is regarded as greater, the one reclining at the table or the one attending to him? Is it not the one reclining? Yet here I am among you, living as one who serves.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:27"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 22:28"/>
    You are the ones who have remained with Me during My trials and hardships.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:28"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 22:29"/>
    Just as My Father entrusted royal authority to Me, I now entrust a kingdom to you,
    <verse eid="LUK 22:29"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 22:30"/>
    so that you may eat and drink at My table in My kingdom, and you will sit on thrones exercising judgment over the twelve tribes of Israel.”
    <verse eid="LUK 22:30"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Peter’s Denial Foretold</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 26:31–35; Mk. 14:27–31; Jn. 13:36–38</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 22:31"/>
    Jesus then said, “Simon, Simon, listen carefully. Satan has demanded permission to test all of you, shaking you as grain is shaken in a sieve.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:31"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 22:32"/>
    But I have interceded for you in particular, that your faith may not collapse. When you have turned back, strengthen your brothers.”
    <verse eid="LUK 22:32"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 22:33"/>
    Peter responded, “Lord, I am prepared to go with You both to imprisonment and to death.”
    <verse eid="LUK 22:33"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 22:34"/>
    Jesus replied, “I tell you, Peter, the rooster will not crow today until you have denied three times that you know Me.”
    <verse eid="LUK 22:34"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Scripture Must Be Fulfilled</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 22:35"/>
    Jesus asked them, “When I sent you out without money, supplies, or sandals, did you lack anything?” They answered, “Nothing.”
    <verse eid="LUK 22:35"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 22:36"/>
    He said, “But now, whoever has money should take it, and likewise supplies. And whoever does not have a sword should sell his outer garment and acquire one.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:36"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 22:37"/>
    For I tell you that what is written must be fulfilled in Me: ‘He was numbered among the lawless.’ Everything written about Me is now approaching its completion.”
    <verse eid="LUK 22:37"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 22:38"/>
    They said, “Lord, here are two swords.” He answered, “That is sufficient.”
    <verse eid="LUK 22:38"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Jesus Prays on the Mount of Olives</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 26:36–46; Mk. 14:32–42</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="39" style="v" sid="LUK 22:39"/>
    Jesus departed and went, according to His established practice, to the Mount of Olives, and the disciples followed Him.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:39"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="40" style="v" sid="LUK 22:40"/>
    When He arrived at the place, He said to them, “Pray that you may not enter into temptation.”
    <verse eid="LUK 22:40"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="41" style="v" sid="LUK 22:41"/>
    He withdrew from them about a stone’s throw away, knelt down, and began to pray.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:41"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="42" style="v" sid="LUK 22:42"/>
    He said, “Father, if You are willing, remove this cup from Me. Yet not My will, but Yours, must be done.”
    <verse eid="LUK 22:42"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="43" style="v" sid="LUK 22:43"/>
    Then an angel from heaven appeared to Him, strengthening Him.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:43"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="44" style="v" sid="LUK 22:44"/>
    Being in deep anguish, He prayed with greater intensity, and His sweat became like heavy drops of blood falling to the ground.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:44"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="45" style="v" sid="LUK 22:45"/>
    When He rose from prayer, He came to the disciples and found them asleep, exhausted by sorrow.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:45"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="46" style="v" sid="LUK 22:46"/>
    He said to them, “Why are you sleeping? Rise and pray, so that you do not enter into temptation.”
    <verse eid="LUK 22:46"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Arrest of Jesus</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 26:47–56; Mk. 14:43–50; Jn. 18:3–11</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="47" style="v" sid="LUK 22:47"/>
    While He was still speaking, a crowd appeared, led by Judas, one of the twelve. He approached Jesus to greet Him with a kiss.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:47"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="48" style="v" sid="LUK 22:48"/>
    Jesus said to him, “Judas, are you betraying the Son of Man with a kiss?”
    <verse eid="LUK 22:48"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="49" style="v" sid="LUK 22:49"/>
    When those around Jesus saw what was unfolding, they asked, “Lord, should we strike with the sword?”
    <verse eid="LUK 22:49"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="50" style="v" sid="LUK 22:50"/>
    One of them struck the servant of the high priest and cut off his right ear.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:50"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="51" style="v" sid="LUK 22:51"/>
    Jesus said, “Enough. Stop this.” And He touched the man’s ear and healed him.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:51"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="52" style="v" sid="LUK 22:52"/>
    Then Jesus said to the chief priests, officers of the temple guard, and elders who had come for Him, “Have you come with swords and clubs as though I were a violent criminal?
    <verse eid="LUK 22:52"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="53" style="v" sid="LUK 22:53"/>
    I was with you daily in the temple teaching, and you did not seize Me. But this is your hour, when the authority of darkness is permitted to act.”
    <verse eid="LUK 22:53"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Peter Denies Jesus</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 26:57–58, 69–75; Mk. 14:53–54, 66–72; Jn. 18:12–18, 25–27</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="54" style="v" sid="LUK 22:54"/>
    They arrested Jesus and led Him away to the residence of the high priest. Peter followed at a distance.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:54"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="55" style="v" sid="LUK 22:55"/>
    When a fire had been lit in the center of the courtyard and people sat down together, Peter sat among them.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:55"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="56" style="v" sid="LUK 22:56"/>
    A servant girl saw him seated by the fire, looked closely at him, and said, “This man was also with Him.”
    <verse eid="LUK 22:56"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="57" style="v" sid="LUK 22:57"/>
    But Peter denied it, saying, “Woman, I do not know Him.”
    <verse eid="LUK 22:57"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="58" style="v" sid="LUK 22:58"/>
    Shortly afterward, another said, “You also are one of them.” But Peter replied, “Man, I am not.”
    <verse eid="LUK 22:58"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="59" style="v" sid="LUK 22:59"/>
    About an hour later another insisted strongly, “Certainly this man was also with Him, for he is a Galilean.”
    <verse eid="LUK 22:59"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="60" style="v" sid="LUK 22:60"/>
    Peter answered, “Man, I do not know what you are saying.” Immediately, while he was still speaking, a rooster crowed.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:60"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="61" style="v" sid="LUK 22:61"/>
    The Lord turned and looked directly at Peter. Then Peter remembered the word the Lord had spoken to him, that before the rooster crowed he would deny Him three times.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:61"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="62" style="v" sid="LUK 22:62"/>
    And Peter went outside and wept bitterly.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:62"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Jesus Mocked and Beaten</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 26:67–68; Mk. 14:65</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="63" style="v" sid="LUK 22:63"/>
    The men guarding Jesus mocked Him and struck Him.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:63"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="64" style="v" sid="LUK 22:64"/>
    They blindfolded Him and demanded, “Prophesy. Who struck You?”
    <verse eid="LUK 22:64"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="65" style="v" sid="LUK 22:65"/>
    And they hurled many other blasphemous insults against Him.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:65"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Jesus Before the Council</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 26:59–66; Mk. 14:55–64; Jn. 18:19–24</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="66" style="v" sid="LUK 22:66"/>
    When daylight came, the elders of the people assembled, including the chief priests and scribes, and they brought Him before their council.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:66"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="67" style="v" sid="LUK 22:67"/>
    They said, “If You are the Christ, tell us.” He replied, “If I tell you, you will not believe,
    <verse eid="LUK 22:67"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="68" style="v" sid="LUK 22:68"/>
    and if I question you, you will not answer.
    <verse eid="LUK 22:68"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="69" style="v" sid="LUK 22:69"/>
    But from now on the Son of Man will be seated at the right hand of the power of God.”
    <verse eid="LUK 22:69"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="70" style="v" sid="LUK 22:70"/>
    They all said, “Are You then the Son of God?” He answered, “You yourselves declare that I am.”
    <verse eid="LUK 22:70"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="71" style="v" sid="LUK 22:71"/>
    Then they said, “What further testimony do we need? We have heard it ourselves from His own mouth.”
    <verse eid="LUK 22:71"/>
  </para>
  <chapter eid="LUK 22"/>
  <chapter number="23" style="c" sid="LUK 23"/>
  <para style="s1">Jesus Before Pilate</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 27:1-2, 11-14; Mk. 15:1-5; Jn. 18:28-38</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 23:1"/>
    The whole assembly of the Sanhedrin rose up and brought Jesus before Pilate, the Roman governor.
    <verse eid="LUK 23:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 23:2"/>
    They began to accuse Him, saying, “We have found this man subverting and perverting our nation. He forbids paying taxes to Caesar, and He claims to be Christ—a King.”
    <verse eid="LUK 23:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 23:3"/>
    Pilate questioned Jesus directly: “Are You the King of the Jews?’’ Jesus answered him, “You have said so. The words are yours.”
    <verse eid="LUK 23:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 23:4"/>
    Pilate said to the chief priests and the crowds, “I find no basis for guilt in this man. He has committed no crime deserving punishment.”
    <verse eid="LUK 23:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 23:5"/>
    But they kept insisting forcefully, “He stirs up the people with His teaching throughout all Judea, starting from Galilee and coming all the way here to Jerusalem.”
    <verse eid="LUK 23:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Jesus Before Herod</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 23:6"/>
    When Pilate heard this mention of Galilee, he inquired whether the man was a Galilean.
    <verse eid="LUK 23:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 23:7"/>
    When he learned that Jesus fell under Herod’s jurisdiction, he sent Him to Herod, who happened to be in Jerusalem during those days.
    <verse eid="LUK 23:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 23:8"/>
    When Herod saw Jesus, he was extremely glad. He had been wanting to see Him for a long time because he had heard reports about Him. Herod was hoping to see Jesus perform some miraculous sign.
    <verse eid="LUK 23:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 23:9"/>
    Herod questioned Jesus at length with many words, but Jesus gave him no answer whatsoever.
    <verse eid="LUK 23:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 23:10"/>
    The chief priests and scribes stood by, fiercely accusing Jesus.
    <verse eid="LUK 23:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 23:11"/>
    Herod, along with his soldiers, treated Jesus with contempt and mocked Him. They dressed Him in an elegant, shining robe to ridicule His claim to kingship, then sent Him back to Pilate.
    <verse eid="LUK 23:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 23:12"/>
    Herod and Pilate, who had previously been enemies, became friends with each other on that very day.
    <verse eid="LUK 23:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Jesus Receives a Verdict of Condemnation</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 27:15-26; Mk. 15:6-15; Jn. 18:39–19:16</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 23:13"/>
    Pilate assembled the chief priests, the ruling authorities, and the people together.
    <verse eid="LUK 23:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 23:14"/>
    He declared to them, “You delivered this man to me with accusations that He was stirring up rebellion among the people. I have conducted a thorough examination in your presence and have discovered no basis for the charges you are leveling against Him.
    <verse eid="LUK 23:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 23:15"/>
    Herod also found nothing worthy of condemnation, which is why he returned Him to us. It is evident that He has committed no offense deserving execution.
    <verse eid="LUK 23:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 23:16"/>
    I will therefore have Him scourged and then set Him free.”*
    <verse eid="LUK 23:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 23:18"/>
    But the entire crowd erupted, shouting in one voice, “Remove this man from our sight! Give us Barabbas instead!”
    <verse eid="LUK 23:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 23:19"/>
    Now Barabbas was someone who had been incarcerated for leading an uprising within the city and for committing murder.
    <verse eid="LUK 23:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 23:20"/>
    Pilate spoke to them once more, desiring to set Jesus free.
    <verse eid="LUK 23:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 23:21"/>
    But they continued shouting back, “Crucify! Crucify Him!”
    <verse eid="LUK 23:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 23:22"/>
    A third time Pilate questioned them, “What crime has this man actually committed? I have discovered nothing in Him that warrants the death penalty. I will therefore discipline Him with a beating and let Him go.’’
    <verse eid="LUK 23:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 23:23"/>
    But they persisted with loud, insistent voices, demanding that He be crucified. Their shouting gained momentum and overpowered all opposition.
    <verse eid="LUK 23:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 23:24"/>
    Then Pilate rendered his verdict, deciding to grant what they were demanding.
    <verse eid="LUK 23:24"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 23:25"/>
    He freed the man who had been jailed for insurrection and murder—the very one they had requested. But Jesus, he handed over to their will to be crucified.
    <verse eid="LUK 23:25"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Crucifixion of Jesus</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 27:32-44; Mk. 15:21-32; Jn. 19:17-27</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 23:26"/>
    As they led Jesus away for execution, they grabbed hold of a man named Simon, a native of Cyrene, who was entering the city from the rural areas. They forced him to carry the cross, making him walk behind Jesus.
    <verse eid="LUK 23:26"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 23:27"/>
    A substantial multitude of people followed Him, including women who were beating their breasts and wailing in grief over Him.
    <verse eid="LUK 23:27"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 23:28"/>
    But Jesus turned toward them and said, “Daughters of Jerusalem, stop weeping over Me. Weep instead for yourselves and for your children.
    <verse eid="LUK 23:28"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 23:29"/>
    For the time is certainly approaching when people will say, ‘How blessed are the women who never had children, the wombs that never conceived, and the breasts that never nursed an infant!’
    <verse eid="LUK 23:29"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 23:30"/>
    At that time people will cry out to the mountains, ‘Collapse on top of us!’ and to the hills, ‘Bury us and conceal us!’
    <verse eid="LUK 23:30"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 23:31"/>
    For if such things happen to the green tree that is full of vitality, what will become of the dry tree?”
    <verse eid="LUK 23:31"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 23:32"/>
    Two others, both criminals, were also being taken away to be put to death alongside Jesus.
    <verse eid="LUK 23:32"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 23:33"/>
    Upon arriving at the location known as The Skull, they crucified Him there along with the criminals—positioning one to His right and one to His left.
    <verse eid="LUK 23:33"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 23:34"/>
    Jesus spoke these words: “Father, pardon them, for they have no comprehension of what they are doing.” The soldiers then proceeded to distribute His garments among themselves by throwing dice.
    <verse eid="LUK 23:34"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 23:35"/>
    The crowd stood observing the scene. Meanwhile, the religious leaders ridiculed Him with scorn, declaring, “He rescued others from their afflictions. If He truly is God’s Messiah, the Chosen One, let Him rescue Himself!”
    <verse eid="LUK 23:35"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 23:36"/>
    The soldiers likewise mocked Him. They approached and extended sour wine toward Him.
    <verse eid="LUK 23:36"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 23:37"/>
    They taunted Him, saying, “If You are truly the King of the Jews, deliver Yourself from this cross!”
    <verse eid="LUK 23:37"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 23:38"/>
    Above Him was posted a placard with this inscription: “This is the King of the Jews.”
    <verse eid="LUK 23:38"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="39" style="v" sid="LUK 23:39"/>
    One of the criminals who was hanging there began hurling abuse at Jesus: “Are You not the Christ? Prove it by saving Yourself and us!”
    <verse eid="LUK 23:39"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="40" style="v" sid="LUK 23:40"/>
    But the other criminal responded with a rebuke: “Have you no fear of God whatsoever, considering you are experiencing the identical punishment?
    <verse eid="LUK 23:40"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="41" style="v" sid="LUK 23:41"/>
    Our punishment is entirely just, for we are receiving exactly what our actions have earned. But this man has committed absolutely nothing improper.”
    <verse eid="LUK 23:41"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="42" style="v" sid="LUK 23:42"/>
    Then he said, “Jesus, keep me in Your memory when You enter Your royal domain.”
    <verse eid="LUK 23:42"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="43" style="v" sid="LUK 23:43"/>
    Jesus replied to him, “I assure you with absolute certainty: this very day you will be with Me in Paradise.”
    <verse eid="LUK 23:43"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Death of Jesus</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 27:45-56; Mk. 15:33-41; Jn. 19:28-30</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="44" style="v" sid="LUK 23:44"/>
    By this time it was approximately the sixth hour, and darkness enveloped the entire region until the ninth hour.
    <verse eid="LUK 23:44"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="45" style="v" sid="LUK 23:45"/>
    The sun ceased giving its light. The massive veil of the temple sanctuary split down the center into two pieces.
    <verse eid="LUK 23:45"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="46" style="v" sid="LUK 23:46"/>
    Jesus cried out in a powerful voice, “Father, into Your care I entrust My spirit!” Having spoken these words, He breathed his last.
    <verse eid="LUK 23:46"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="47" style="v" sid="LUK 23:47"/>
    When the centurion witnessed what had transpired, he began praising God, proclaiming, “This man was certainly righteous and without guilt!”
    <verse eid="LUK 23:47"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="48" style="v" sid="LUK 23:48"/>
    All the crowds that had assembled to observe this spectacle, upon seeing what had occurred, returned to their homes striking their chests in sorrow and regret.
    <verse eid="LUK 23:48"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="49" style="v" sid="LUK 23:49"/>
    All who were acquainted with Jesus, including the women who had accompanied Him from Galilee, stood far off observing these events.
    <verse eid="LUK 23:49"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Burial of Jesus</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 27:57-61; Mk. 15:42-47; Jn. 19:38-42</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="50" style="v" sid="LUK 23:50"/>
    Now there was a man named Joseph, a member of the Sanhedrin Council—a man of good character and righteousness.
    <verse eid="LUK 23:50"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="51" style="v" sid="LUK 23:51"/>
    He had not given his approval to the Council’s plan and their actions against Jesus. He came from the Judean town of Arimathea and was eagerly anticipating the arrival of God’s kingdom.
    <verse eid="LUK 23:51"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="52" style="v" sid="LUK 23:52"/>
    This man approached Pilate courageously and requested permission to take possession of Jesus’ body.
    <verse eid="LUK 23:52"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="53" style="v" sid="LUK 23:53"/>
    After taking the body down, he wrapped it in fine linen fabric and laid it in a rock-hewn tomb where no one had previously been buried.
    <verse eid="LUK 23:53"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="54" style="v" sid="LUK 23:54"/>
    This took place on the Preparation Day, with the Sabbath about to commence at sunset.
    <verse eid="LUK 23:54"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="55" style="v" sid="LUK 23:55"/>
    The women who had journeyed with Jesus from Galilee followed closely behind Joseph. They carefully noted the tomb’s location and observed exactly how His body was positioned.
    <verse eid="LUK 23:55"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="56" style="v" sid="LUK 23:56"/>
    Then they went back and made preparations of aromatic spices and fragrant ointments. However, they observed the Sabbath rest in accordance with the commandment.
    <verse eid="LUK 23:56"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Notes</para>
  <para style="p">
    16 A textual variant present in part of the manuscript tradition incorporates verse 17. Its phrasing, “
    <char style="it">Now he was duty-bound to release to them one person at the Feast</char>
    ,” serves to bring the narrative into conformity with the Synoptic and Johannine parallels (Matt. 27:15; Mk. 15:6; Jn. 18:39)
  </para>
  <chapter eid="LUK 23"/>
  <chapter number="24" style="c" sid="LUK 24"/>
  <para style="s1">The Resurrection</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 28:1-10; Mk. 16:1-8; Jn. 20:1-10</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 24:1"/>
    At daybreak on the first day of the week, the women arrived at the tomb carrying the aromatic spices they had made ready.
    <verse eid="LUK 24:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 24:2"/>
    They found that the stone blocking the tomb’s entrance had been rolled aside.
    <verse eid="LUK 24:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 24:3"/>
    Upon entering, they were unable to locate the body of the Lord Jesus.
    <verse eid="LUK 24:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 24:4"/>
    As they stood there completely bewildered by this situation, two men dressed in garments that gleamed brilliantly suddenly appeared standing next to them.
    <verse eid="LUK 24:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 24:5"/>
    Filled with terror, the women dropped to the ground with their faces downward. The men asked them, “Why are you searching for the Living One in a place of death?
    <verse eid="LUK 24:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 24:6"/>
    He is not in this place—He has risen! Recall what He told you when He was still with you in Galilee.
    <verse eid="LUK 24:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 24:7"/>
    He declared that the Son of Man had to be handed over to sinful people, undergo crucifixion, and be raised to life on the third day.”
    <verse eid="LUK 24:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 24:8"/>
    At that moment the women recalled His words.
    <verse eid="LUK 24:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 24:9"/>
    They hurried back from the tomb and recounted everything to the eleven and to everyone else who was with them.
    <verse eid="LUK 24:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 24:10"/>
    The ones reporting these events to the apostles were Mary Magdalene, Joanna, Mary the mother of James, and the other women in their company.
    <verse eid="LUK 24:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 24:11"/>
    But the apostles regarded their account as utter foolishness and refused to believe them.
    <verse eid="LUK 24:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 24:12"/>
    Peter, however, jumped up and raced to the tomb. When he bent down to peer inside, he observed nothing except the linen burial cloths lying by themselves. He departed, deeply puzzled about what had taken place.
    <verse eid="LUK 24:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">On the Road to Emmaus</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Mk. 16:12-13</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 24:13"/>
    That same day, two of His followers were making their way to a village named Emmaus, located approximately seven miles distant from Jerusalem.
    <verse eid="LUK 24:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 24:14"/>
    They were engaged in conversation about everything that had occurred.
    <verse eid="LUK 24:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 24:15"/>
    As they talked and reasoned together, Jesus Himself drew near and joined them on their journey.
    <verse eid="LUK 24:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 24:16"/>
    Yet their eyes were restrained so they could not recognize His identity.
    <verse eid="LUK 24:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 24:17"/>
    He asked them, “What matters are you debating with each other as you travel? They came to a halt, their expressions filled with sadness.
    <verse eid="LUK 24:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 24:18"/>
    The one called Cleopas responded, “Are You the only person visiting Jerusalem who remains unaware of the events that have unfolded there during these past few days?”
    <verse eid="LUK 24:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 24:19"/>
    He asked them, “What events?” They replied, &quot;The events involving Jesus of Nazareth, who demonstrated Himself to be a prophet powerful in both action and teaching before God and all the people.
    <verse eid="LUK 24:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 24:20"/>
    The chief priests and our leaders handed Him over to receive a death sentence, and they had Him crucified.
    <verse eid="LUK 24:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 24:21"/>
    Yet we were holding onto the hope that He was the one expected to liberate Israel. What is more, this is now the third day since all this took place.
    <verse eid="LUK 24:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 24:22"/>
    Furthermore, certain women from our group have left us astounded. They visited the tomb at first light this morning
    <verse eid="LUK 24:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 24:23"/>
    but failed to find His body there. They returned claiming they had witnessed a vision of angels who announced that He is alive!
    <verse eid="LUK 24:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 24:24"/>
    Some of our companions then went to the tomb and discovered conditions exactly as the women had described, but Him they did not see.”
    <verse eid="LUK 24:24"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 24:25"/>
    He said to them, “How foolish you are, and how sluggish your hearts are to trust everything the prophets declared!
    <verse eid="LUK 24:25"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 24:26"/>
    Was it not ordained that the Messiah should endure these sufferings and then enter into His glory?”
    <verse eid="LUK 24:26"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 24:27"/>
    Then, starting with Moses and continuing through all the Prophets, He interpreted for them what was written about Himself throughout all the Scriptures.
    <verse eid="LUK 24:27"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 24:28"/>
    As they drew near to the village that was their destination, He gave the impression He intended to continue farther.
    <verse eid="LUK 24:28"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 24:29"/>
    But they pressed Him earnestly, saying, “Remain here with us, because evening is approaching and the day has nearly ended.” So He entered to lodge with them.
    <verse eid="LUK 24:29"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 24:30"/>
    As He reclined at the table with them, He took bread, offered a blessing, broke it into pieces, and distributed it to them.
    <verse eid="LUK 24:30"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 24:31"/>
    At that instant their eyes were fully opened, and they recognised Him. But immediately He disappeared from their view.
    <verse eid="LUK 24:31"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 24:32"/>
    They said to each other, “Did not our hearts feel as though they were aflame within us as He conversed with us along the road and unfolded the meaning of the Scriptures for us?”
    <verse eid="LUK 24:32"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 24:33"/>
    They rose up that very hour and made their way back to Jerusalem, where they located the eleven and their companions assembled together.
    <verse eid="LUK 24:33"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 24:34"/>
    These were declaring, “The Lord has genuinely risen! He has made an appearance to Simon!”
    <verse eid="LUK 24:34"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 24:35"/>
    Then the two recounted what had transpired on the road and how they had come to recognize Jesus when He broke the bread.
    <verse eid="LUK 24:35"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Jesus’ Appearance to the Disciples and the Great Commission</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Matt. 28:16-20; Mk. 16:14-18; Jn. 20:19-23; Acts 1:6-8</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 24:36"/>
    As they were discussing these matters, Jesus Himself took His place among them and said, “Peace to you.”
    <verse eid="LUK 24:36"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 24:37"/>
    They were shocked and overcome with fear, convinced they were witnessing a ghostly apparition.
    <verse eid="LUK 24:37"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 24:38"/>
    He asked them, “Why are you so disturbed? Why do questions of doubt surface in your minds?
    <verse eid="LUK 24:38"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="39" style="v" sid="LUK 24:39"/>
    Examine My hands and My feet—confirm that it is truly I! Put your hands on Me and verify this for yourselves. A spirit does not possess flesh and bones, which you can plainly see I have.”
    <verse eid="LUK 24:39"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="40" style="v" sid="LUK 24:40"/>
    Having said this, He displayed His hands and feet to them.
    <verse eid="LUK 24:40"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="41" style="v" sid="LUK 24:41"/>
    While they remained unable to believe because of their overwhelming joy and continued to marvel, He asked, “Have you any food available here?”
    <verse eid="LUK 24:41"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="42" style="v" sid="LUK 24:42"/>
    They handed Him a portion of cooked fish.
    <verse eid="LUK 24:42"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="43" style="v" sid="LUK 24:43"/>
    He accepted it and consumed it before their eyes.
    <verse eid="LUK 24:43"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="44" style="v" sid="LUK 24:44"/>
    Then He told them, “This is what I explained to you while I was still among you: everything recorded about Me in the Law of Moses, the Prophets, and the Psalms had to reach fulfillment.”
    <verse eid="LUK 24:44"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="45" style="v" sid="LUK 24:45"/>
    Then He opened their understanding so they could comprehend the Scriptures.
    <verse eid="LUK 24:45"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="46" style="v" sid="LUK 24:46"/>
    He explained to them, “It stands written that the Messiah would suffer and rise from death on the third day,
    <verse eid="LUK 24:46"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="47" style="v" sid="LUK 24:47"/>
    and that repentance leading to forgiveness of sins would be proclaimed in His name to all the nations, commencing in Jerusalem.
    <verse eid="LUK 24:47"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="48" style="v" sid="LUK 24:48"/>
    You are witnesses to all these events.
    <verse eid="LUK 24:48"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="49" style="v" sid="LUK 24:49"/>
    Take notice: I am sending upon you what My Father has promised. Remain in the city until you have been clothed with power from heaven.”
    <verse eid="LUK 24:49"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Ascension</para>
  <para style="r">
    <char style="it">Mk. 16:19-20; Acts 1:9-11</char>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="50" style="v" sid="LUK 24:50"/>
    Then He led them out to the vicinity of Bethany, and lifting His hands, He pronounced a blessing upon them.
    <verse eid="LUK 24:50"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="51" style="v" sid="LUK 24:51"/>
    While He was in the act of blessing them, He parted from them and was taken up into heaven.
    <verse eid="LUK 24:51"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="52" style="v" sid="LUK 24:52"/>
    After worshipping Him, they returned to Jerusalem filled with immense joy.
    <verse eid="LUK 24:52"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="53" style="v" sid="LUK 24:53"/>
    They spent their time constantly in the temple, offering praise and glory to God.
    <verse eid="LUK 24:53"/>
  </para>
  <chapter eid="LUK 24"/>
</usx>
